(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1d.f917dee2df.js"]={"18":[{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q01_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with severe COPD presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset right-sided chest pain and dyspnea. Chest X-ray reveals a large right-sided pneumothorax. CT imaging after chest tube placement shows a large subpleural air-filled space (>1 cm diameter) near the right lung apex. Which form of emphysema most likely caused this complication?","choices":{"A":"Bullous emphysema with rupture of large subpleural bleb causing pneumothorax","B":"Centriacinar emphysema with selective respiratory bronchiole involvement","C":"Panacinar emphysema with uniform acinar enlargement throughout lung","D":"Interstitial emphysema with air dissecting through connective tissue planes","E":"Compensatory emphysema with overdistention of remaining functional lung tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bullous emphysema describes the presence of large subpleural blebs or bullae (airspaces >1 cm in diameter) that can occur in any form of emphysema, often located near the lung apex. These thin-walled structures are prone to rupture, particularly during activities that increase intrathoracic pressure or with minimal trauma, leading to pneumothorax. The CT finding of a large subpleural air-filled space near the apex is characteristic of a bulla. Rupture of bullae is a common cause of spontaneous pneumothorax in patients with underlying emphysema. Centriacinar emphysema describes central acinar involvement; the pneumothorax here comes from a ruptured subpleural bulla, which Robbins terms bullous emphysema. Panacinar emphysema describes uniform acinar enlargement; a subpleural space over 1 cm is a bulla, and bulla rupture causes the pneumothorax. Interstitial emphysema is air in connective tissue planes, not a subpleural bulla. Compensatory hyperinflation is alveolar dilation after loss of lung substance elsewhere, not a subpleural bulla.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bullous emphysema is a descriptive term for large subpleural blebs or bullae (> 1 cm when distended) that can occur in any form of emphysema, often near the apex; rupture may cause pneumothorax. FA: secondary spontaneous pneumothorax comes from diseased lung such as emphysematous bullae. COMLEX: FA — pneumothorax gives ↓ fremitus, hyperresonance and diminished breath sounds on the affected side.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 2 — spontaneous, secondary and tension pneumothorax","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Centriacinar emphysema describes central acinar involvement;","C":"Panacinar emphysema describes uniform acinar enlargement; a ","D":"Interstitial emphysema is air in connective tissue planes, n","E":"Compensatory hyperinflation is alveolar dilation after loss "}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q02_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with severe emphysema develops sudden neck swelling and crackling sensation in the chest wall after a prolonged coughing episode. Physical examination reveals subcutaneous crepitus extending from the neck to the upper chest. Chest X-ray shows air outlining the mediastinal structures and dissecting into subcutaneous tissues. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of subpleural bulla causing direct communication between alveoli and pleural space","B":"Alveolar tear lets air into the connective tissue stroma during a pressure surge","C":"Infection with gas-forming organisms producing subcutaneous emphysema and mediastinal air","D":"Bronchial perforation from foreign body aspiration allowing air escape into surrounding tissues","E":"Pulmonary vascular rupture with air embolism dissecting through tissue planes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has interstitial emphysema (pneumomediastinum with subcutaneous emphysema). Interstitial emphysema occurs when air enters the connective tissue stroma of the lung, mediastinum, or subcutaneous tissue. In patients with pulmonary emphysema, alveolar tears can occur during transient increases in intraalveolar pressure, such as during vigorous coughing, straining, or positive pressure ventilation. Air escapes from ruptured alveoli into the interstitium and tracks along perivascular and peribronchial connective tissue planes to the mediastinum, then dissects into subcutaneous tissues of the neck and chest wall, producing characteristic crepitus. A ruptured bulla gives a pneumothorax; air in the mediastinum and subcutaneous tissue is interstitial emphysema from alveolar tears. Robbins attributes air in the mediastinum and subcutaneous tissue in emphysema to alveolar tears during coughing, not to gas-forming infection. No aspiration is described; Robbins attributes such air in emphysema to alveolar tears from pressure surges such as coughing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial emphysema — air enters the connective tissue stroma of the lung, mediastinum or subcutaneous tissue, usually through alveolar tears in emphysematous lungs during transient rises in intraalveolar pressure such as coughing; less often chest wounds or rib fractures provide the entry. COMLEX: Subcutaneous crepitus over the neck and chest after coughing or trauma is the bedside sign.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — the mechanics of expiration, including the alveolar pressure rises of coughing and straining","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A ruptured bulla gives a pneumothorax; air in the mediastinu","C":"Robbins attributes air in the mediastinum and subcutaneous t","D":"No aspiration is described; Robbins attributes such air in e"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q03_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man undergoes left lower lobectomy for lung cancer. Six months postoperatively, chest X-ray shows the remaining left upper lobe hyperlucent and expanded to fill the left hemithorax. Which mechanism best explains these radiographic findings?","choices":{"A":"Progressive development of centriacinar emphysema from undiagnosed smoking history","B":"Panacinar emphysema from occult α₁-antitrypsin deficiency accelerated by surgery","C":"Bullous emphysema with formation of multiple large subpleural blebs postoperatively","D":"Interstitial emphysema from barotrauma during mechanical ventilation in perioperative period","E":"Compensatory emphysema with overdistention of the remaining lobe to fill the hemithorax"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Compensatory hyperinflation (compensatory emphysema) is dilation of alveoli in response to loss of lung substance elsewhere, for example after surgical removal of a lung or lobe (Robbins Ch 15). After lobectomy the remaining lobe overexpands to fill the space; this is overdistention of structurally normal alveoli, not true emphysema with wall destruction. Centriacinar, panacinar and bullous emphysema are destructive processes that develop over years and are not caused by surgery, and interstitial emphysema from barotrauma puts air in the interstitium rather than producing a hyperexpanded lobe months later.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Compensatory hyperinflation is dilation of alveoli in response to loss of lung substance elsewhere, as after removal of a lung or lobe for cancer; Robbins lists it among conditions with lung overinflation that are not true destructive emphysema. COMLEX: Guyton — removing an entire lung halves membrane surface area, yet resting gas exchange is preserved until area falls to about one-third to one-fourth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity and its dependence on membrane surface area","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Centriacinar, panacinar and bu"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q04_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy presents with episodic wheezing and dyspnea, worse at night and triggered by exposure to cats. He has a history of eczema, and his father has allergic rhinitis. Skin prick testing shows positive wheal-and-flare reactions to cat dander, dust mites, and tree pollens. Serum testing reveals elevated total IgE and positive radioallergosorbent tests (RAST) for specific allergens. Which asthma phenotype best characterizes this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"Nonatopic asthma triggered by respiratory viral infections without allergen sensitization","B":"Drug-induced asthma related to aspirin sensitivity with associated nasal polyps","C":"Atopic asthma representing IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity to environmental allergens","D":"Occupational asthma caused by workplace exposure to organic dusts or chemical fumes","E":"Exercise-induced asthma without evidence of allergic sensitization or family history"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has classic atopic asthma, an IgE-mediated (type I) hypersensitivity reaction. Key features include: (1) childhood onset, (2) environmental allergen triggers (cat dander, dust, pollen), (3) positive family history (father with allergic rhinitis), (4) personal history of other atopic conditions (eczema), (5) positive skin prick tests showing immediate wheal-and-flare reactions, and (6) laboratory evidence of allergen sensitization (elevated total IgE and positive RAST). Atopic asthma typically begins in childhood and represents the most common asthma phenotype. Nonatopic asthma follows infection without allergen sensitization; this boy has cat sensitization and eczema. Drug-induced asthma follows aspirin or other NSAIDs, with rhinitis and nasal polyps, not cat exposure. Occupational asthma follows repeated workplace exposure to fumes, dusts or chemicals, not cat dander.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Atopic asthma, the most common form, is a classic IgE-mediated type I reaction — childhood onset, environmental allergens (dusts, pollens, dander, foods), often with viral cofactors, a positive family history, and an immediate wheal-and-flare skin test; high total IgE or RAST also support it. COMLEX: FA — asthma is associated with atopy (eczema, allergic rhinitis) and triggered by allergens, viral URIs and stress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast-cell immunologic process of an asthma attack","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Nonatopic asthma follows infection without allergen sensitiz","B":"Drug-induced asthma follows aspirin or other NSAIDs, with rh","D":"Occupational asthma follows repeated workplace exposure to f"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q05_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with a history of asthma and chronic rhinitis presents to the emergency department with acute bronchospasm and urticaria 30 minutes after taking ibuprofen for a headache. She reports similar reactions in the past after taking aspirin. Physical examination reveals nasal polyps on anterior rhinoscopy. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains this patient's asthma exacerbation?","choices":{"A":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation triggered by cross-reactive drug allergen epitopes","B":"Direct histamine release from basophils causing immediate bronchoconstriction and urticaria","C":"Viral respiratory infection exacerbating underlying chronic airway inflammation and hyperreactivity","D":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition decreasing prostaglandin E₂ and increasing leukotriene production","E":"Occupational allergen exposure to pharmaceutical compounds causing type I hypersensitivity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has aspirin-sensitive (aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease) asthma, an uncommon phenotype associated with the classic triad: asthma, chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal polyps, and sensitivity to aspirin/NSAIDs. The mechanism involves cyclooxygenase (COX) pathway inhibition by aspirin and other NSAIDs. COX inhibition causes rapid decrease in prostaglandin E₂ (PGE₂), which normally inhibits 5-lipoxygenase. Loss of PGE₂ inhibition leads to shunting of arachidonic acid metabolism toward the lipoxygenase pathway, causing overproduction of proinflammatory leukotrienes (B₄, C₄, D₄, E₄). These leukotrienes cause prolonged bronchoconstriction, increased vascular permeability, mucus secretion, and urticaria. Robbins classes this as drug-induced asthma caused by cyclooxygenase inhibition, not as an IgE-mediated allergy to the drug. Robbins attributes aspirin-sensitive asthma to cyclooxygenase inhibition lowering PGE2, which normally restrains leukotriene production, not to direct histamine release. The reaction follows ibuprofen, not a viral infection. No occupational exposure is described.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin-sensitive asthma (uncommon) occurs with recurrent rhinitis and nasal polyps; aspirin and NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase, rapidly lowering prostaglandin E2, which normally restrains leukotriene-generating enzymes — leukotrienes then drive the attack and urticaria. FA: Samter's triad — asthma, nasal polyps, COX-inhibitor sensitivity. COMLEX: FA — leukotriene receptor antagonists are especially good for aspirin-induced asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes, including leukotriene modifiers, for asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins attributes aspirin-sensitive asthma to cyclooxygenas","C":"The reaction follows ibuprofen, not a viral infection.","E":"No occupational exposure is described."}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q06_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with asthma experiences frequent exacerbations during winter months, often following upper respiratory infections. He denies environmental allergies, has negative skin prick tests to common allergens, and has normal total serum IgE levels. His family history is negative for asthma or atopy. Which asthma phenotype best describes this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"Atopic asthma with IgE-mediated hypersensitivity to seasonal allergens","B":"Nonatopic asthma with viral respiratory infections as predominant triggers","C":"Drug-induced asthma from aspirin or NSAID sensitivity","D":"Occupational asthma triggered by workplace chemical exposures","E":"Exercise-induced asthma exacerbated by cold air during winter activities"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has nonatopic asthma, characterized by: (1) absence of allergen sensitization (negative skin tests, normal IgE), (2) negative family history of asthma/atopy, and (3) viral respiratory infections as primary triggers. Common viral triggers include rhinovirus, parainfluenza virus, and respiratory syncytial virus (RSV). Nonatopic asthma patients experience bronchospasm triggered by respiratory infections, air pollutants (tobacco smoke, sulfur dioxide, ozone, nitrogen dioxide), cold air, and exercise, without evidence of IgE-mediated allergic mechanisms. The winter clustering reflects increased viral respiratory infections during cold months. Atopic asthma shows allergen sensitization, with positive skin tests or high IgE, both absent here. No aspirin or NSAID trigger is described. No workplace exposure is described. Winter exacerbations follow upper respiratory infections, not exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nonatopic asthma — no allergen sensitization, usually negative skin tests, less often a positive family history; respiratory viruses (rhinovirus, parainfluenza, RSV) are common triggers, and pollutants, cold or exercise can also provoke attacks. COMLEX: Upper respiratory infections are a leading trigger for exacerbations; the pulmonary OMM lab's URI approach targets the upper thoracic region, ribs and thoracic inlet.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 9 — viral bronchiolitis and its sequelae","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Atopic asthma shows allergen sensitization, with positive sk","C":"No aspirin or NSAID trigger is described.","D":"No workplace exposure is described.","E":"Winter exacerbations follow upper respiratory infections, no"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q07_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying atopic asthma pathogenesis investigates the role of T helper cells in the disease. Which cytokine profile secreted by Th2 cells is most responsible for the characteristic features of atopic asthma, including IgE production, eosinophil recruitment, and mucus hypersecretion?","choices":{"A":"IL-12 promoting Th1 differentiation and interferon-gamma production for cellular immunity","B":"IL-4 drives IgE, IL-5 activates eosinophils, and IL-13 promotes mucus secretion","C":"IL-17 recruiting neutrophils and IL-22 enhancing epithelial barrier function","D":"TNF-α inducing systemic inflammation and IL-1β activating endothelial adhesion molecules","E":"IL-10 suppressing inflammatory responses and TGF-β promoting regulatory T cell function"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Th2 cells are central to atopic asthma pathogenesis and secrete a characteristic cytokine profile: (1) IL-4 stimulates B cells to undergo class switching to produce IgE antibodies, which bind to mast cell Fc receptors; (2) IL-5 activates and recruits eosinophils, key effector cells in allergic inflammation; and (3) IL-13 stimulates mucus secretion from bronchial submucosal glands and also promotes IgE production by B cells. This cytokine profile creates the type 2 immunity characteristic of allergic diseases. Group 2 innate lymphoid cells (ILC2s) produce similar cytokines and contribute to asthma pathogenesis. IL-12 drives T cells toward Th1 (First Aid), not the Th2 cytokines IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13 that drive atopic asthma. IL-17 and IL-22 are Th17 cytokines that induce neutrophilic infiltration (First Aid), not the Th2 profile of atopic asthma. TNF-alpha and IL-1 are macrophage cytokines of acute inflammation (First Aid), not Th2 cytokines. TGF-beta with IL-10 is the regulatory T cell profile that maintains tolerance (First Aid); Robbins's asthma Th2 cytokines are IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — Th2 cells secrete IL-4 (stimulates IgE), IL-5 (activates recruited eosinophils) and IL-13 (stimulates submucosal mucus secretion and promotes IgE); group 2 innate lymphoid cells make the same cytokines (type 2 immunity). COMLEX: FA's biologics map onto this list — omalizumab (anti-IgE), mepolizumab/reslizumab/benralizumab (IL-5 axis), dupilumab (IL-4Rα).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"IL-12 drives T cells toward Th1 (First Aid), not the Th2 cyt","C":"IL-17 and IL-22 are Th17 cytokines that induce neutrophilic ","D":"TNF-alpha and IL-1 are macrophage cytokines of acute inflamm","E":"TGF-beta with IL-10 is the regulatory T cell profile that ma"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q08_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with atopic asthma undergoes allergen inhalation challenge in a research study. Within 15 minutes, she develops acute bronchoconstriction, increased mucus production, and wheezing (early phase reaction). Four hours later, she experiences recurrent bronchospasm with eosinophil and T cell infiltration (late phase reaction). Which mechanism initiates the early phase reaction?","choices":{"A":"Th2 cell activation and cytokine secretion causing delayed eosinophil recruitment","B":"Neutrophil degranulation releasing myeloperoxidase and neutrophil elastase","C":"Mast cell degranulation triggered by IgE cross-linking releasing preformed mediators","D":"Macrophage activation producing tumor necrosis factor and interleukin-1","E":"B cell antibody production causing immune complex deposition in airways"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The early phase reaction (immediate hypersensitivity) in atopic asthma is initiated when inhaled allergen cross-links IgE antibodies bound to Fc receptors on mast cells, triggering mast cell degranulation. Mast cells release preformed mediators (histamine, tryptase, heparin) and rapidly synthesize new mediators (leukotrienes C₄, D₄, E₄; prostaglandin D₂; platelet-activating factor). These mediators cause immediate bronchoconstriction (within minutes), increased mucus production, vasodilation, and increased vascular permeability. The late phase reaction (setting in 2 to 24 hours later, Robbins Ch 6) involves recruitment of eosinophils, neutrophils, and additional T cells through chemokines and cytokines. Robbins says the early phase is triggered by allergen cross-linking IgE on mast cells; neutrophils are recruited later, in the late phase. Robbins says the early phase is triggered by IgE cross-linking on mast cells, not by macrophage activation. Atopic asthma is a type I, IgE-mediated reaction, not immune complex deposition.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Repeat allergen exposure crosslinks IgE on submucosal mast cells, which release granule contents — the early phase of bronchoconstriction, mucus, vasodilation and permeability, partly via vagal reflexes; the late phase is leukocyte recruitment, notably eosinophils. FA: immediate phase in minutes (histamine, tryptase, leukotrienes), late phase in hours. COMLEX: Bronchoconstriction has an inflammatory and a parasympathetic arm — FA notes therapy targets both.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast-cell process that mediates an asthma attack","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins says the early phase is triggered by allergen cross-","D":"Robbins says the early phase is triggered by IgE cross-linki","E":"Atopic asthma is a type I, IgE-mediated reaction, not immune"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q09_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student reviewing asthma pathophysiology learns about various inflammatory mediators involved in bronchospasm. Which mediator is most responsible for the prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting several hours after allergen exposure, and whose role is supported by the clinical efficacy of specific antagonist drugs?","choices":{"A":"Histamine causing immediate but transient bronchoconstriction through H1 receptor activation","B":"Acetylcholine released from parasympathetic nerves stimulating muscarinic receptors","C":"Prostaglandin D₂ producing vasodilation and bronchial smooth muscle contraction","D":"Leukotrienes C₄, D₄, and E₄ causing bronchoconstriction that lasts hours","E":"Platelet-activating factor triggering platelet aggregation and serotonin release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Leukotrienes C₄, D₄, and E₄ (collectively called slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis, SRS-A) are the most important mediators of prolonged bronchoconstriction in asthma. They are synthesized by mast cells, eosinophils, and other inflammatory cells through the 5-lipoxygenase pathway. Leukotrienes cause: (1) prolonged, potent bronchoconstriction lasting several hours, (2) increased vascular permeability and edema, (3) mucus hypersecretion, and (4) inflammatory cell recruitment. The clinical efficacy of leukotriene receptor antagonists (montelukast) and 5-lipoxygenase inhibitors (zileuton) in treating asthma supports their central role in disease pathogenesis. Platelet-activating factor, which aggregates platelets and releases serotonin, is in the group Robbins calls minor contributors because potent antagonists have not proved effective. Robbins ranks histamine among mediators with minor roles because potent antagonists lack efficacy, unlike leukotrienes, which cause prolonged bronchoconstriction. Robbins does support a role for acetylcholine acting on muscarinic receptors, but it is the leukotrienes he describes as causing prolonged bronchoconstriction. Robbins ranks prostaglandin D2 among mediators with minor roles because antagonists and synthesis inhibitors lack efficacy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leukotrienes C4, D4 and E4 (Guyton's slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) sustain bronchoconstriction; Robbins notes their central role in aspirin-induced asthma. COMLEX: FA — montelukast and zafirlukast block CysLT1 receptors and zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase, the clinical proof of their role.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — pharmacologic classes, including antileukotrienes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins ranks histamine among mediators with minor roles bec","B":"Robbins does support a role for acetylcholine acting on musc","C":"Robbins ranks prostaglandin D2 among mediators with minor ro"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 71-year-old man with advanced COPD undergoes chest CT for evaluation of worsening dyspnea. The scan reveals multiple large air-filled spaces (2-4 cm diameter) in the upper lobes bilaterally, predominantly in subpleural regions. His pulmonologist discusses surgical options for these lesions. Which complication is this patient at highest risk for developing?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary embolism from hypercoagulability associated with chronic inflammation","B":"Aspergilloma formation within pre-existing cavitary spaces and fungal colonization","C":"Spontaneous pneumothorax from rupture of thin-walled subpleural air spaces","D":"Pulmonary hypertension from compression of pulmonary vasculature by expanding lesions","E":"Bronchopulmonary fistula causing persistent air leak requiring surgical intervention"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The patient has bullous emphysema with large bullae (>1 cm airspaces) in subpleural locations. The primary complication is spontaneous pneumothorax from bulla rupture, which can occur with minimal trauma, coughing, or changes in intrathoracic pressure. Bullae have thin walls and are prone to rupture, particularly those located in subpleural regions near the apex. This is the most common acute complication requiring urgent intervention. Surgical options include bullectomy (removing large bullae that compress adjacent lung) or pleurodesis to prevent recurrent pneumothorax. Pulmonary emboli almost always come from deep leg vein thrombi, not from bullae, and aspergillomas grow in pre-existing cavities, classically old tuberculous ones; Robbins names rupture with pneumothorax as the complication of bullae. Robbins notes bullae can compress lung vessels, but the complication he names for subpleural bullae is rupture with pneumothorax, which can be fatal. Robbins names pneumothorax, not a bronchopleural fistula, as the result of subpleural bulla rupture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bullae (> 1 cm subpleural spaces) can occur in any form of emphysema, often near the apex, and their rupture may give rise to pneumothorax; Robbins lists bullectomy among treatment options for COPD. COMLEX: FA — tension pneumothorax (air in, not out) deviates the trachea away, kinks venous return and causes obstructive shock with JVD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — alveolar changes in emphysema versus pneumonia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Robbins notes bullae can compress lung vessels, but the comp","E":"Robbins names pneumothorax, not a bronchopleural fistula, as"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with severe emphysema is intubated for respiratory failure. Shortly after initiating positive pressure ventilation at high tidal volumes, she develops sudden hypotension, jugular venous distention, and subcutaneous crepitus palpable in the suprasternal notch and anterior chest wall. Chest X-ray shows air outlining the heart border and dissecting into soft tissues. Which mechanism caused this complication?","choices":{"A":"Tension pneumothorax from bulla rupture with one-way valve effect trapping air","B":"Pulmonary embolism with acute right heart strain and cardiovascular collapse","C":"Cardiac tamponade from pericardial effusion compressing cardiac chambers","D":"Alveolar overdistention and rupture with air tracking into the mediastinum","E":"Esophageal perforation during intubation allowing gastric air to enter mediastinum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has developed interstitial emphysema (pneumomediastinum with subcutaneous emphysema) from barotrauma. Positive pressure ventilation with high tidal volumes in emphysematous lungs can cause alveolar overdistention and rupture. Air escapes from ruptured alveoli into the interstitium and tracks along perivascular and peribronchial connective tissue planes to the mediastinum (seen as air outlining the heart on X-ray). Air then dissects into neck and chest wall soft tissues, causing subcutaneous crepitus. The hypotension results from decreased venous return due to increased intrathoracic pressure. A tension pneumothorax could also cause hypotension and jugular distention, but its film would show a collapsed lung with mediastinal shift, not air outlining the heart and soft tissues; pulmonary embolism and tamponade do not put air in the mediastinum, and nothing here suggests esophageal injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Interstitial emphysema arises from alveolar tears when intraalveolar pressure surges, letting air dissect along the stroma into the mediastinum and subcutaneous tissue. FA: mechanical ventilation at high pressures causes barotrauma and can produce pneumothorax. COMLEX: FA — sudden hypotension, tachycardia and JVD in a ventilated patient suggest tension pneumothorax with obstructive shock.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 1 — pulmonary barotrauma and its pathophysiology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"A tension pneumothorax could a","E":"A tension pneumothorax could a"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Other Forms of Emphysema Associated with Lung Overinflation or Focal Emphysematous Changes","batch":1,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a slow-growing carcinoid tumor that partly obstructs his left lower lobe bronchus. Expiratory chest radiographs show the left lower lobe hyperlucent and enlarged rather than collapsed, and it is beginning to compress the adjacent left upper lobe. Which mechanism best explains the lobe's overexpansion?","choices":{"A":"Air trapped behind a ball-valve partial obstruction","B":"Loss of lung tissue elsewhere filled by expansion","C":"Air entering the interstitium through alveolar tears","D":"Complete obstruction with resorption of distal air","E":"Elastase destruction of alveolar walls around the tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins describes obstructive overinflation, in which the lung expands because air is trapped within it, most commonly from subtotal obstruction of an airway by a tumor or foreign object. It occurs either because the obstruction acts as a ball valve, letting air in on inspiration but not out on expiration, or because collateral channels (pores of Kohn and canals of Lambert) bring air in behind the obstruction; it can be life-threatening if the affected portion distends enough to compress adjacent uninvolved lung. Expansion to fill space left by lost lung describes compensatory hyperinflation after lobectomy. Air in the connective tissue stroma through alveolar tears is interstitial emphysema. Complete obstruction with absorption of distal air causes resorption atelectasis, a collapsed lobe. Elastase destruction of alveolar walls is the mechanism of COPD-associated emphysema, not of a lobe distended behind a partial obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obstructive overinflation — the lung expands because air is trapped within it, commonly from subtotal obstruction by a tumor or foreign body acting as a ball valve (air in on inspiration, not out on expiration) or from collateral filling; it can be life-threatening if it compresses adjacent lung. COMLEX: Contrast with complete obstruction, where trapped air is absorbed and the lobe collapses (obstructive atelectasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 8 — carcinoid tumors, classic endobronchial obstructing masses","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old factory worker presents with episodic wheezing and dyspnea that began 6 months after starting a new job in furniture manufacturing. Symptoms improve significantly on weekends and during vacations but recur within hours of returning to work. Peak flow monitoring shows marked variability correlating with work exposure. Which asthma phenotype best characterizes this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Atopic asthma with IgE-mediated sensitization to wood dust allergens","B":"Nonatopic asthma triggered by viral respiratory infections","C":"Aspirin-sensitive asthma with associated chronic rhinosinusitis","D":"Exercise-induced bronchospasm exacerbated by physical labor","E":"Occupational asthma triggered by wood dust and formaldehyde at work"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has occupational asthma, defined by the characteristic pattern of symptoms correlating with workplace exposure and improving away from work. Furniture manufacturing involves exposure to multiple respiratory sensitizers including wood dusts (red cedar, oak), formaldehyde (used in adhesives and finishes), and organic chemicals. The 6-month latency period suggests sensitization development. Mechanisms vary: some agents cause type I IgE reactions, others cause direct bronchospasm, and some trigger hypersensitivity of unknown mechanism. The temporal relationship with work exposure is diagnostic. Robbins classes asthma triggered by workplace dusts and chemicals such as wood and formaldehyde as occupational asthma, whose mechanisms include but are not limited to type I reactions. Viral infections are the trigger of nonatopic asthma, not workplace exposure. No aspirin sensitivity or nasal polyps are described; Robbins links aspirin-sensitive asthma to rhinitis and nasal polyps. Symptoms that track workplace exposure and ease on weekends fit occupational asthma, not exercise as the trigger.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Occupational asthma can be triggered by fumes (epoxy resins, plastics), organic and chemical dusts (wood, cotton, platinum), gases (toluene) or chemicals (formaldehyde, penicillin products); minute quantities trigger attacks after repeated exposure, by type I reactions, direct bronchoconstrictor release or unknown hypersensitivity. COMLEX: Symptoms that improve on weekends and recur at work point to an occupational cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — using exposure history to differentiate the pneumoconioses","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins classes asthma triggered by workplace dusts and chem","B":"Viral infections are the trigger of nonatopic asthma, not wo","C":"No aspirin sensitivity or nasal polyps are described; Robbin","D":"Symptoms that track workplace exposure and ease on weekends "}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has episodic wheezing triggered by dust and cat dander, and her mother has asthma. She cannot stop her daily antihistamine, so skin-prick testing is not possible. Which laboratory test does Robbins describe for demonstrating allergen sensitization in suspected atopic asthma?","choices":{"A":"Sweat chloride concentration test","B":"Serum α₁-antitrypsin concentration","C":"Anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies","D":"Serum RAST for allergen-specific IgE","E":"Sputum culture for Aspergillus species"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins notes that atopic asthma, a type I IgE-mediated hypersensitivity that usually begins in childhood with a positive family history, can be diagnosed by an immediate wheal-and-flare skin test reaction or, alternatively, from high total serum IgE or evidence of allergen sensitization on serum radioallergosorbent tests (RASTs), which detect IgE antibodies specific for individual allergens. Sweat chloride testing diagnoses cystic fibrosis. Serum α₁-antitrypsin is measured for suspected early panacinar emphysema. ANCA testing evaluates vasculitides such as granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Aspergillus in sputum is pursued in allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis, a complication of asthma or cystic fibrosis, not to show sensitization to dust and dander.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — atopic asthma is diagnosed by an immediate wheal-and-flare skin test, or by high total serum IgE or allergen-specific IgE on radioallergosorbent tests (RAST). FA: type I testing — skin test or blood test (ELISA) for allergen-specific IgE. COMLEX: A serum test for allergen-specific IgE is the alternative whenever skin testing cannot be done.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the IgE–mast cell process in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"ANCA testing evaluates vasculi"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Etiology and Pathogenesis of Asthma, Differentiating Among Various Phenotypes","batch":1,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl with atopic asthma, eczema, and seasonal allergic rhinitis undergoes immunologic evaluation. Laboratory testing reveals markedly elevated serum IgE levels and strongly positive radioallergosorbent tests (RAST) for multiple environmental allergens. Her mother and maternal grandmother both have asthma. Which genetic susceptibility factor most strongly associated with her phenotype?","choices":{"A":"HLA-DR4 alleles linked to autoimmune-mediated bronchial inflammation","B":"Chromosome 7 mutations causing cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator dysfunction","C":"Chromosome 5q polymorphisms near IL-4, IL-5, IL-13 gene cluster and IL-4 receptor","D":"α₁-antitrypsin gene variants on chromosome 14 causing protease-antiprotease imbalance","E":"Chromosome 11 mutations in surfactant protein genes causing alveolar instability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A susceptibility locus for atopic asthma is located on chromosome 5q near genes encoding IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13, and IL-4 receptor. Among these, IL-13 gene polymorphisms have the strongest and most consistent associations with asthma and allergic disease, while IL-4 receptor variants associate with atopy, elevated IgE, and asthma. These cytokines are central to Th2-mediated allergic inflammation: IL-4 drives IgE production, IL-5 activates eosinophils, and IL-13 promotes mucus secretion and IgE. Other genetic associations include IL-33/ST2 genes and thymic stromal lymphopoietin (TSLP) gene variants. First Aid links HLA-DR4 to rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diabetes and Addison disease; Robbins's strongest atopic asthma locus is on chromosome 5q. CFTR gene defects on chromosome 7 cause cystic fibrosis, not atopic asthma. Alpha-1 antitrypsin variants cause emphysema, not atopic asthma. Robbins's asthma susceptibility loci are the chromosome 5q cytokine cluster, HLA class II alleles, IL-33 and TSLP, not surfactant protein genes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — a susceptibility locus for asthma lies on chromosome 5q near the cluster encoding IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9 and IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor. COMLEX: Robbins — atopic asthma typically begins in childhood with a positive family history, consistent with this inherited susceptibility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"First Aid links HLA-DR4 to rheumatoid arthritis, type 1 diab","B":"CFTR gene defects on chromosome 7 cause cystic fibrosis, not","D":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin variants cause emphysema, not atopic ast","E":"Robbins's asthma susceptibility loci are the chromosome 5q c"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying asthma pathophysiology examines bronchial biopsies from patients with chronic atopic asthma. Structural changes observed include smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia, subepithelial fibrosis, increased airway vascularity, and mucous gland enlargement. Which term best describes these collective changes resulting from repeated allergen exposure?","choices":{"A":"Acute bronchospasm reflecting immediate hypersensitivity response to allergen","B":"Bronchiectasis representing permanent airway dilation from chronic infection","C":"Type I hypersensitivity demonstrating IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation","D":"Airway remodeling caused by chronic inflammation and growth factor secretion","E":"Emphysematous destruction showing alveolar wall breakdown and airspace enlargement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Airway remodeling describes structural changes in bronchial walls from repeated allergen exposure and chronic inflammation in asthma. These changes include: (1) smooth muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia (increased muscle mass), (2) subepithelial fibrosis (sub-basement membrane collagen deposition), (3) increased airway vascularity (angiogenesis), (4) mucous gland enlargement (subepithelial gland hyperplasia), (5) epithelial injury and thickening, and (6) thickened basement membrane. Remodeling results from growth factors (TGF-β, VEGF, PDGF) secreted by inflammatory cells and epithelium. It contributes to progressive, partially irreversible airflow obstruction. Type I hypersensitivity is the immediate IgE and mast cell reaction that triggers each attack; the lasting structural changes it leads to over time are what Robbins calls remodeling. Bronchiectasis is permanent dilation from infection destroying smooth muscle and elastic tissue, the opposite of the muscle hypertrophy and fibrosis of remodeling. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, whereas these changes are in the bronchial wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Repeated allergen exposure and immune reactions produce airway remodeling — bronchial smooth-muscle hypertrophy and hyperplasia, epithelial injury, increased airway vascularity, subepithelial mucous gland enlargement and subepithelial fibrosis. COMLEX: FA — inhaled glucocorticoids, which inhibit synthesis of virtually all cytokines, are first-line for chronic asthma control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — integrating pathophysiology to choose asthma and COPD regimens","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Bronchiectasis is permanent dilation from infection destroyi","E":"Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, whereas these changes are"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with atopic asthma undergoes allergen challenge testing. Investigators measure inflammatory cells in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid 8 hours after allergen exposure. Which cell type would be most predominant in the late-phase reaction?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophils recruited by IL-5 and chemokines released during Th2 response","B":"Mast cells undergoing degranulation and releasing preformed histamine granules","C":"Plasma cells producing IgE antibodies specific for environmental allergens","D":"CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes causing direct epithelial cell destruction","E":"Alveolar macrophages phagocytosing inhaled particulate matter and pathogens"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The late-phase reaction (setting in 2 to 24 hours after allergen exposure, Robbins Ch 6) is characterized by recruitment of inflammatory cells, predominantly eosinophils, along with neutrophils and additional T cells. Eosinophils are recruited by: (1) IL-5 secreted by Th2 cells and ILC2s, which activates and attracts eosinophils; (2) eotaxin and other chemokines produced by epithelium and other cells; and (3) leukotriene B₄. Eosinophils release toxic granule proteins (major basic protein, eosinophil cationic protein, eosinophil peroxidase) and galectin-10 (forms Charcot-Leyden crystals), amplifying inflammation and airway damage. Eosinophil-predominant infiltration is a hallmark of atopic asthma. Alveolar macrophages are resident cells that are always present; they are not the leukocytes recruited in the late phase. Mast cell degranulation triggers the early phase; the late phase is dominated by recruited eosinophils, neutrophils and T cells. Robbins says the late phase is dominated by eosinophils, neutrophils and T cells, not plasma cells. Robbins's late phase is dominated by eosinophils, neutrophils and T cells, with Th2 cells the dominant lymphoid type, not CD8 cytotoxic cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — the late-phase reaction is dominated by leukocyte recruitment, notably eosinophils (activated by IL-5), with neutrophils and more T cells; eosinophil products such as major basic protein and eosinophil cationic protein damage the epithelium. COMLEX: FA — anti-IL-5 biologics (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab) target this eosinophilic arm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for key asthma drug classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Mast cell degranulation triggers the early phase; the late p","C":"Robbins says the late phase is dominated by eosinophils, neu","D":"Robbins's late phase is dominated by eosinophils, neutrophil","E":"Alveolar macrophages are resid"}},{"id":"sdl18_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Fundamental Abnormality of Asthma - Exaggerated Th2 Response to Environmental Antigens","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmaceutical company develops a new asthma medication that blocks the synthesis of leukotrienes C₄, D₄, and E₄ by inhibiting 5-lipoxygenase. Clinical trials demonstrate efficacy in reducing bronchoconstriction. Which characteristic of these leukotrienes makes them critical therapeutic targets?","choices":{"A":"Rapid metabolism within seconds producing only transient bronchospasm effects","B":"Prolonged bronchoconstriction lasting hours, unlike histamine's brief effect","C":"Selective action on large central airways without affecting small peripheral bronchioles","D":"Production exclusively by mast cells during immediate early-phase reaction only","E":"Primary role in mucus hypersecretion without significant bronchoconstriction effects"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Leukotrienes C₄, D₄ and E₄ (slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis) cause prolonged, potent bronchoconstriction lasting hours, unlike the rapid but transient effect of histamine, and they also increase vascular permeability and mucus secretion. The clinical benefit of leukotriene receptor antagonists (montelukast) and 5-lipoxygenase inhibitors (zileuton) supports their central role. They are made by mast cells, eosinophils and macrophages and act on the late phase as well, they act throughout the airways rather than only on central bronchi, and they are not cleared within seconds. Robbins says leukotrienes C4, D4 and E4 cause prolonged bronchoconstriction as well as increased mucus secretion and vascular permeability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leukotrienes C4, D4 and E4 cause prolonged bronchoconstriction (Guyton's slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis), and prostaglandin E2 normally restrains their synthesis (Robbins). FA: zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase, lowering conversion of arachidonic acid to leukotrienes. COMLEX: FA flags zileuton as hepatotoxic, unlike the CysLT1 blockers montelukast and zafirlukast.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — adverse effects and safety of asthma and COPD drug classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"They are made by mast cells, e","C":"They are made by mast cells, e","E":"Robbins says leukotrienes C4, D4 and E4 cause prolonged bron"}}],"19":[{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q01_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with severe asthma dies in the emergency department despite aggressive treatment. Autopsy reveals overinflated lungs with complete occlusion of bronchi and bronchioles. Microscopic examination of the airway contents shows shed epithelial cells, numerous eosinophils, and hexagonal bipyramidal crystals. Which protein comprises these characteristic crystals?","choices":{"A":"Major basic protein released from eosinophil primary granules causing epithelial damage","B":"Surfactant protein D forming aggregates in response to inflammatory airway injury","C":"Crystallized mucin glycoproteins from hyperplastic goblet cells and submucosal glands","D":"Galectin-10 (eosinophil-derived protein) forming Charcot-Leyden crystals","E":"Immunoglobulin E antibodies precipitated by high local concentrations in airways"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Charcot-Leyden crystals are a characteristic finding in the sputum and airway contents of patients with atopic asthma, particularly in fatal status asthmaticus. These hexagonal bipyramidal crystals are composed of galectin-10 (also called Charcot-Leyden crystal protein), a protein released from eosinophil granules. Recent research shows these crystals are not merely byproducts but are strong inducers of inflammation and mucus production, contributing to disease pathogenesis. They are commonly seen alongside numerous eosinophils in bronchoalveolar lavage fluid and autopsy specimens from patients dying of acute severe asthma. Robbins says major basic protein damages the epithelium, while the crystals are Charcot-Leyden crystals made of galectin-10. Robbins identifies these as Charcot-Leyden crystals of the eosinophil protein galectin-10, not surfactant protein D. Extruded mucus forms Curschmann spirals; the crystals are Charcot-Leyden crystals of eosinophil-derived galectin-10. Robbins identifies these as Charcot-Leyden crystals of eosinophil-derived galectin-10, not precipitated IgE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Status asthmaticus at autopsy — overinflated lungs with small atelectatic areas and bronchi occluded by thick, tenacious mucus plugs containing shed epithelium; sputum or lavage shows Curschmann spirals, many eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals made of the eosinophil protein galectin-10. FA: Charcot-Leyden crystals are eosinophilic, hexagonal, double-pointed crystals from eosinophil breakdown. COMLEX: Eosinophil-rich sputum marks type 2 (Th2) inflammation, the target of anti-IL-5 biologics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2 response at the core of asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins says major basic protein damages the epithelium, whi","B":"Robbins identifies these as Charcot-Leyden crystals of the e","C":"Extruded mucus forms Curschmann spirals; the crystals are Ch","E":"Robbins identifies these as Charcot-Leyden crystals of eosin"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q02_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"An epidemiologist studying rising asthma prevalence in industrialized nations investigates the \"hygiene hypothesis.\" Which mechanism best explains how reduced early childhood microbial exposure may increase later asthma risk?","choices":{"A":"Nutritional deficiencies from processed foods impairing normal immune system development","B":"Urban pollution exposure overwhelms respiratory defenses causing chronic airway inflammation","C":"Increased allergen production by dust mites thriving in modern climate-controlled buildings","D":"Genetic mutations accumulating in populations with reduced selective pressure from infections","E":"Limited early microbial exposure impairs immune maturation and protective responses"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The hygiene hypothesis proposes that reduced microbial exposure during early childhood prevents proper immune system maturation and shifts immune responses toward allergic (Th2) rather than protective (Th1) patterns. Microbial antigens help \"train\" the developing immune system; without adequate exposure, children may be more susceptible to developing allergic diseases including asthma and atopy. While the exact mechanisms remain unclear, this hypothesis explains why asthma prevalence is higher in industrialized societies and why early-life microbial exposure (farm environments, daycare, older siblings) appears protective. This has prompted trials of probiotics and intentional early allergen exposure. Robbins's hygiene hypothesis concerns limited early-life microbial exposure, not nutritional deficiency. Robbins presents pollutants acting as allergens as a separate idea from the hygiene hypothesis. The hygiene hypothesis concerns limited early-life microbial exposure, not allergen production by dust mites. The hygiene hypothesis concerns limited early-life microbial exposure, not accumulated genetic mutations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hygiene hypothesis (Robbins) — city life limits early childhood exposure to microbial antigens, which may protect against asthma and atopy; mechanisms are unclear, but it has prompted trials of probiotics and early allergen exposure. Infections are cofactors: sensitized young children with rhinovirus C or RSV lower respiratory infection have a 10- to 30-fold higher risk of persistent or severe asthma. COMLEX: Ask about early viral wheezing illnesses when taking a childhood asthma history.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the etiology and phenotypes of asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins's hygiene hypothesis concerns limited early-life mic","B":"Robbins presents pollutants acting as allergens as a separat","C":"The hygiene hypothesis concerns limited early-life microbial exposure, not aller","D":"The hygiene hypothesis concerns limited early-life microbial exposure, not accum"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q03_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from a patient who died from status asthmaticus. In addition to mucus plugging and eosinophilic inflammation, characteristic spiral-shaped structures are observed in the airway lumens. Which process most likely produces these Curschmann spirals?","choices":{"A":"Helical arrangement of inflammatory cells adhering to damaged bronchial epithelium","B":"Twisted arrangement of shed epithelial cells sloughed during acute airway inflammation","C":"Spiral contraction patterns of hypertrophied bronchial smooth muscle during bronchospasm","D":"Mucus plugs extruded from submucosal gland ducts or bronchioles form spiral casts","E":"Coiled collagen fibers deposited during sub-basement membrane fibrosis and remodeling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Curschmann spirals are characteristic spiral-shaped mucus casts found in sputum and bronchoalveolar lavage specimens from patients with atopic asthma, particularly during acute exacerbations and status asthmaticus. They are thought to result from extrusion of mucus plugs from subepithelial mucous gland ducts or bronchioles, forming twisted cylindrical structures with a central core. Along with numerous eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals, Curschmann spirals are a diagnostic feature helping identify asthma. Their presence reflects the severe mucus hypersecretion and airway obstruction characteristic of acute severe asthma. Robbins attributes Curschmann spirals to extruded mucus plugs from gland ducts or bronchioles, not to helically arranged inflammatory cells. Robbins attributes the spirals to extruded mucus plugs; shed epithelium is only part of the plug contents. Curschmann spirals are extruded mucus plugs, not patterns of smooth muscle contraction. Curschmann spirals are extruded mucus plugs, not collagen from sub-basement membrane fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Curschmann spirals, seen in sputum or lavage of atopic asthma, may form from extrusion of mucus plugs from subepithelial mucous gland ducts or bronchioles; FA describes them as shed epithelium forming whorled mucus plugs. They accompany eosinophils and Charcot-Leyden crystals. COMLEX: FA — mucus plugging is part of asthma's mechanism alongside hyperresponsive bronchi and reversible bronchoconstriction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast-cell immunologic process of an asthma attack","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins attributes Curschmann spirals to extruded mucus plug","B":"Robbins attributes the spirals to extruded mucus plugs; shed","C":"Curschmann spirals are extruded mucus plugs, not patterns of","E":"Curschmann spirals are extruded mucus plugs, not collagen fr"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q04_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents with chronic productive cough, recurrent sinusitis, and infertility. Chest CT shows bilateral bronchial dilation extending to the pleural surfaces. He reports that his symptoms began in childhood. Genetic testing reveals mutations affecting ciliary motor proteins. Which syndrome best explains this constellation of findings?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis with CFTR gene mutations causing thick viscous secretions","B":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia with possible Kartagener syndrome including situs abnormalities","C":"Immunodeficiency syndrome with recurrent bacterial pneumonias causing airway destruction","D":"Idiopathic bronchiectasis from dysfunctional host immunity to infectious agents","E":"Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis with Th2-mediated hypersensitivity to Aspergillus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has primary ciliary dyskinesia, an autosomal recessive disease (1 in 10,000-20,000 births) caused by mutations affecting ciliary motor proteins (e.g., dynein). Ciliary dysfunction prevents mucociliary clearance, leading to recurrent infections and bronchiectasis. The triad of chronic sinusitis, bronchiectasis, and male infertility (from sperm dysmotility) is characteristic. Approximately 50% of patients have Kartagener syndrome, which includes situs inversus or partial lateralizing abnormalities because ciliary function is required during embryogenesis for proper organ rotation. The childhood onset, chronic productive cough, sinusitis, infertility, and genetic findings confirm primary ciliary dyskinesia. Robbins ties cystic fibrosis to an ion transport defect with thick secretions, whereas ciliary motor protein mutations define primary ciliary dyskinesia. Immunodeficiency can cause bronchiectasis, but ciliary motor protein mutations with male infertility from sperm dysmotility define primary ciliary dyskinesia. ABPA is a Th2 hyperimmune response to Aspergillus in asthma or cystic fibrosis, not a ciliary motor protein defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary ciliary dyskinesia — autosomal recessive, 1 in 10,000–20,000 births, mutations in ciliary motor proteins such as dynein; poor mucociliary clearance leads to bronchiectasis, and about half have Kartagener syndrome (situs inversus with bronchiectasis and sinusitis); males are often infertile from sperm dysmotility. COMLEX: FA — a low nasal nitric oxide is the screening test; women have ↑ ectopic pregnancy risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — tracheal structure and cell types, including ciliated cells","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins ties cystic fibrosis to an ion transport defect with","C":"Immunodeficiency can cause bronchiectasis, but ciliary motor","E":"ABPA is a Th2 hyperimmune response to Aspergillus in asthma "}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q05_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with chronic bronchiectasis undergoes chest CT. The radiologist notes that the bronchi can be clearly visualized extending almost to the pleural surfaces. In a normal lung, bronchioles typically cannot be seen by eye beyond what distance from the pleural surface?","choices":{"A":"2-3 cm from the pleural surface on gross inspection","B":"1 cm from the pleural surface except near hilum","C":"5-7 cm from the pleural surface in all lung zones","D":"8-10 cm from the pleural surface in upper lobes","E":"Bronchioles are normally visible to the pleural surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A characteristic morphologic feature of bronchiectasis is that the dilated bronchi and bronchioles can be followed almost to the pleural surfaces (Robbins describes this on gross examination of the lung). In contrast, in normal lungs the bronchioles cannot be seen by eye beyond a point 2-3 cm from the pleural surface. This finding, along with airways dilated up to four times normal size and cystic appearance filled with mucopurulent secretions on cut surfaces, is diagnostic of bronchiectasis. The ability to trace airways to the periphery reflects the pathologic dilation from chronic infection and inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In bronchiectasis the dilated bronchi and bronchioles can be followed almost to the pleura, whereas normal bronchioles cannot be seen beyond 2–3 cm from the pleural surface; airways may dilate up to four times normal. COMLEX: FA — bronchiectasis is confirmed by imaging showing airway dilation and bronchial thickening, with an obstructive PFT pattern.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — branching morphogenesis and the bronchopulmonary segments","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"In contrast, in normal lungs t"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q06_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with post-infectious bronchiectasis presents with worsening symptoms. Sputum culture grows organisms typical of the bronchiectatic microenvironment. Which two bacteria are most commonly isolated from bronchiectasis patients?","choices":{"A":"Streptococcus pneumoniae (80%) and Staphylococcus aureus (40%)","B":"Mycobacterium tuberculosis (60%) and Klebsiella pneumoniae (30%)","C":"Haemophilus influenzae (50%) and Pseudomonas aeruginosa (12-30%)","D":"Escherichia coli (70%) and Enterococcus species (35%)","E":"Legionella pneumophila (45%) and Moraxella catarrhalis (25%)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Haemophilus influenzae is found in approximately 50% and Pseudomonas aeruginosa in 12-30% of sputum cultures from bronchiectasis patients, with four other bacterial types (including nontuberculous mycobacteria) making up most remaining cases. This narrow microbial spectrum suggests that the inflamed, mucoid, sometimes anaerobic bronchiectatic microenvironment supports colonization by relatively few bacterial species. These organisms are well-adapted to persist in damaged airways with impaired clearance. In cystic fibrosis-associated bronchiectasis, Pseudomonas is particularly common and associated with worse outcomes. The figures given for Streptococcus pneumoniae and Staphylococcus aureus, or for Legionella and Moraxella, have no support in Robbins, which names Haemophilus influenzae and Pseudomonas aeruginosa as the two leading isolates. Robbins gives H influenzae (about 50%) and Pseudomonas (12% to 30%) as the usual isolates, not M tuberculosis and Klebsiella. Robbins gives H influenzae (about 50%) and Pseudomonas (12% to 30%) as the usual isolates, not E coli and Enterococcus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — H. influenzae is found in about 50% and P. aeruginosa in 12–30% of bronchiectasis sputum cultures, with four other bacterial types (including nontuberculous mycobacteria) making up most of the rest; the inflamed, mucoid, sometimes anaerobic environment supports few species. COMLEX: FA lists Mycobacterium avium among the infectious causes of bronchiectasis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 3 — the key community-acquired organisms, including H. influenzae and P. aeruginosa","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins gives H influenzae (about 50%) and Pseudomonas (12% to 30%) as the usual isolates, not M tub","D":"Robbins gives H influenzae (about 50%) and Pseudomonas (12% to 30%) as the usual isolates, not E col","E":"The figures given for Streptoc"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q07_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old man presents with severe persistent cough and foul-smelling sputum production that is particularly worse in the mornings when he first gets out of bed. He reports occasional hemoptysis. Chest CT shows dilated airways extending to the lung periphery. Which pathophysiologic mechanism best explains why symptoms worsen upon rising in the morning?","choices":{"A":"Circadian variation in bronchial smooth muscle tone causing increased airway resistance after sleep","B":"Change of position drains pus and secretions pooled in dilated airways into the bronchi","C":"Nocturnal accumulation of histamine and leukotrienes triggering early morning bronchoconstriction","D":"Morning cortisol surge paradoxically increasing airway inflammation before medications take effect","E":"Overnight mucus plug formation from goblet cell hypersecretion during supine positioning"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has bronchiectasis, characterized by permanently dilated airways that accumulate purulent secretions and pus. Paroxysms of cough are particularly frequent when the patient rises in the morning because the change from supine to upright position causes collections of pus and secretions to drain from the dilated peripheral airways into the central bronchi, triggering intense coughing. This positional effect on symptoms is characteristic of bronchiectasis and distinguishes it from other causes of chronic productive cough. The foul-smelling sputum reflects chronic bacterial colonization (H. influenzae, P. aeruginosa) in the dilated, poorly-cleared airways. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to the change in position draining pooled pus and secretions into the bronchi, not to circadian bronchial tone. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to a nightly build-up of histamine and leukotrienes. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to a cortisol surge. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to pus and secretions already collected in dilated airways draining into the bronchi when position changes, not new mucus plugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchiectasis causes severe persistent cough with foul-smelling, sometimes bloody sputum, and occasionally massive hemoptysis; coughing paroxysms are worst on rising in the morning, when the change in position drains pooled pus and secretions into the bronchi. COMLEX: Robbins credits antibiotics combined with physical therapy for nearly doubling life expectancy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — complications of bacterial pneumonia such as abscess and empyema","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to the change in po","C":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to a nightly build-up ","D":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to a cortisol surge.","E":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to pus and secretio"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q08_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A medical student is learning to differentiate obstructive lung diseases. Which clinical finding most reliably distinguishes bronchiectasis from COPD and asthma?","choices":{"A":"Chronic cough with foul-smelling purulent sputum and hemoptysis","B":"Presence of wheezing and dyspnea indicating airflow obstruction","C":"FEV₁/FVC ratio less than 0.7 on spirometry testing","D":"Evidence of chronic inflammation and airway remodeling on biopsy","E":"Progressive worsening of symptoms over months to years"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bronchiectasis is distinguished from other obstructive diseases by severe persistent cough with foul-smelling, purulent sputum (often bloody) reflecting chronic bacterial colonization in dilated airways. The foul odor results from anaerobic bacterial metabolism in poorly-cleared secretions. Hemoptysis (sometimes massive) occurs from inflammation and erosion of bronchial vessels in damaged airway walls. COPD typically produces clear or white sputum unless infected; asthma produces minimal sputum or clear/white mucoid secretions. The combination of chronic purulent, foul-smelling sputum with hemoptysis is highly characteristic of bronchiectasis. An FEV1/FVC below 0.7 marks airflow obstruction shared by COPD and asthma, and slow worsening over months to years is also typical of COPD, so neither separates bronchiectasis. Robbins describes chronic inflammation and remodeling in asthma and chronic bronchitis too, so it does not single out bronchiectasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchiectasis stands apart from COPD and asthma by daily purulent, foul-smelling sputum, recurrent infections and hemoptysis (Robbins; FA: chronic cough with daily purulent sputum). COMLEX: FA — confirm with imaging showing airway dilation and bronchial thickening, and look for causes such as cystic fibrosis, Kartagener syndrome or ABPA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of the major obstructive lung diseases","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Robbins describes chronic inflammation and remodeling in ast"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q09_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man undergoes pulmonary function testing to evaluate chronic dyspnea. Results show FEV₁ 1.9 L (55% predicted), FVC 2.8 L (68% predicted), FEV₁/FVC 0.68, and TLC 7.2 L (125% predicted). Which obstructive disease pattern do these findings most strongly suggest?","choices":{"A":"Asthma with reversible bronchospasm and normal lung volumes between episodes","B":"Restrictive lung disease with reduced volumes and preserved FEV₁/FVC ratio","C":"Bronchiectasis with mixed obstruction and localized restrictive components","D":"Emphysema with air trapping, hyperinflation, and increased total lung capacity","E":"Chronic bronchitis with increased airway resistance but normal elastic recoil"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These findings show obstruction (FEV₁/FVC 0.68, which is <0.7) with marked hyperinflation (TLC 125% predicted). The increased TLC results from air trapping due to loss of elastic recoil and dynamic airway collapse during expiration in emphysema. Despite reduced absolute lung volumes (low FEV₁ and FVC from obstruction), the TLC is increased because residual volume and functional residual capacity are elevated from inability to fully exhale. This pattern is characteristic of emphysema, particularly in \"pink puffers.\" Chronic bronchitis also causes obstruction but typically shows less hyperinflation. A TLC of 125% predicted is not a normal lung volume, so a pattern with normal volumes does not fit. Restriction keeps FEV1/FVC normal with a low TLC, but here the ratio is 0.68 and TLC is raised.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FEV1/FVC < 0.7 with a raised TLC is obstruction with hyperinflation — the emphysema pattern (FA: obstructive disease ↑ RV, FRC and TLC). Robbins: emphysema-dominant patients have low elastic recoil and severe overdistention. COMLEX: Robbins' 'pink puffer' — barrel chest, prolonged expiration, pursed lips, low diffusion capacity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — obstructive versus restrictive patterns on the flow-volume curve","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A TLC of 125% predicted is not a normal lung volume, so a pa","B":"Restriction keeps FEV1/FVC normal with a low TLC, but here t"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q10_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with a family history of asthma and atopic dermatitis presents with worsening dyspnea and wheezing. Genetic analysis reveals polymorphisms on chromosome 5q affecting cytokine production. Which cytokine gene cluster is most likely affected by these genetic variants?","choices":{"A":"IL-8, IL-10, and IL-12","B":"IL-1, IL-6, and TNF-α","C":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13","D":"IL-17, IL-22, and IL-23","E":"IL-2, IL-7, and IL-15"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chromosome 5q contains a critical gene cluster encoding IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13, which are central to allergic inflammation in asthma. Polymorphisms in this region increase susceptibility to asthma by promoting Th2 immune responses, IgE production, and eosinophilic inflammation. Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-8, IL-10 and IL-12. Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-1, IL-6 and TNF. Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-17, IL-22 and IL-23. Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-2, IL-7 and IL-15.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — an asthma susceptibility locus on chromosome 5q lies near the genes for IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9 and IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor; IL-4 drives IgE, IL-5 activates eosinophils, IL-13 drives mucus secretion and IgE. COMLEX: FA's IL-4Rα blocker dupilumab and anti-IL-5 agents act on products of this same cluster.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2 response in asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-8, IL-10 and IL-1","B":"Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-1, IL-6 and TNF.","D":"Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-17, IL-22 and IL-","E":"Robbins gives the chromosome 5q cluster as IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-9, IL-13 and the IL-4 receptor, not IL-2, IL-7 and IL-15"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q11_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old construction worker develops progressive dyspnea and wheezing after 5 years of exposure to isocyanates in spray foam insulation. Pulmonary function testing shows reversible airflow obstruction. What mechanism best explains the development of occupational asthma in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Isocyanates act as haptens binding to airway proteins","B":"Particulate matter directly destroys bronchial epithelium","C":"Chronic hypoxemia triggers smooth muscle hypertrophy","D":"Silica deposits activate alveolar macrophage responses","E":"Nitrogen dioxide causes irreversible fibrotic changes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Occupational asthma from isocyanates occurs through haptenization, where the chemical acts as a hapten by binding to airway proteins, creating neoantigens that trigger IgE-mediated allergic responses and airway inflammation. Robbins attributes occupational asthma to type I reactions, direct release of bronchoconstrictors, or other hypersensitivity responses, not to particles destroying the epithelium, and nitrogen dioxide-driven fibrosis would not give the reversible obstruction seen here. Robbins lists occupational asthma mechanisms as type I reactions, direct release of bronchoconstrictors and hypersensitivity of unknown origin, not hypoxemia. Silica causes silicosis, a pneumoconiosis, not isocyanate occupational asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — occupational asthma follows repeated exposure to fumes, dusts, gases or chemicals, and minute quantities then trigger attacks; mechanisms include type I reactions, direct release of bronchoconstrictors and hypersensitivity of unknown origin. COMLEX: An exposure history that links symptoms to the workplace is the key to diagnosis and removal from exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — asthma phenotypes, including occupational asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Robbins lists occupational asthma mechanisms as type I react","D":"Silica causes silicosis, a pneumoconiosis, not isocyanate oc"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q12_b1","objective":7,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 7 — Genetic susceptibility and environmental factors in asthma; morphology in status asthmaticus","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman dies from status asthmaticus despite maximal therapy. Autopsy examination of the lungs reveals severely overinflated parenchyma with airways completely occluded by thick mucus plugs. What underlying mechanism caused the hyperinflation observed at autopsy?","choices":{"A":"Destruction of alveolar walls from chronic inflammation","B":"Air trapping behind mucus plugs and bronchospasm","C":"Pulmonary vascular congestion with edema formation","D":"Fibrotic changes reducing lung tissue compliance","E":"Loss of elastic recoil from proteolytic enzyme activity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The hyperinflation in status asthmaticus results from air trapping due to complete airway obstruction by mucus plugs combined with severe bronchospasm. This creates a ball-valve effect where air enters but cannot exit, progressively overinflating the lungs. Alveolar wall destruction and protease-driven loss of elastic recoil describe emphysema, fibrosis stiffens the lung and shrinks it rather than overinflating it, and vascular congestion with edema is not what Robbins describes at autopsy in acute severe asthma (overinflated lungs with mucus plugs and small areas of atelectasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In status asthmaticus the lungs are overinflated, with small areas of atelectasis, because thick mucus plugs and bronchospasm occlude bronchi and bronchioles and trap air. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab lists severe asthma among causes of respiratory failure — persistent accessory muscle use signals muscle fatigue and impending failure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 4 — overinflation from airway obstruction, including the ball-valve mechanism","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Alveolar wall destruction and "}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q13_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with recurrent pneumonias develops chronic productive cough with copious purulent sputum. CT chest shows permanently dilated bronchi with destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue in airway walls. What pathogenic mechanism primarily causes this structural damage?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction by antineutrophil antibodies","B":"Genetic defects causing defective collagen synthesis","C":"Necrotizing inflammation from persistent infection","D":"Chronic hypoxemia leading to tissue ischemia","E":"Accumulated cigarette smoke toxins damaging fibers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The permanent dilation in bronchiectasis results from necrotizing inflammation caused by persistent or severe infections that destroy the smooth muscle and elastic tissue framework of bronchial walls. This structural damage prevents the airways from maintaining normal caliber. Robbins names obstruction and infection as the major conditions behind bronchiectasis; antineutrophil autoimmunity and defective collagen synthesis are not among its listed causes, and cigarette smoke is the cause of chronic bronchitis and emphysema. Robbins attributes bronchiectasis to persistent or severe infection whose inflammation destroys smooth muscle and elastic tissue, not to hypoxemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bronchiectasis is destruction of smooth muscle and elastic tissue by inflammation from persistent or severe infection, giving permanent dilation of bronchi and bronchioles. FA: an initial infection plus obstruction or impaired clearance → dysregulated host response → permanently dilated airways. COMLEX: FA — most commonly associated with cystic fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — the pathology and pathogenesis of cystic fibrosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Robbins attributes bronchiectasis to persistent or severe in"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q14_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with bronchiectasis undergoes surgical resection of the affected lung segment. Gross pathological examination reveals airways that are dilated and easily visible extending to the pleural surface. What is the normal maximum distance from the pleura at which bronchioles should remain visible?","choices":{"A":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 2-3 cm from pleura","B":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 7-8 cm from pleura","C":"Bronchioles normally visible at all depths in tissue","D":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 1-2 cm from pleura","E":"Bronchioles normally invisible beyond 5-6 cm from pleura"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In bronchiectasis, dilated airways become visible all the way to the pleural surface, which is pathologic. Normally, bronchioles should not be visible beyond 2-3 cm from the pleural surface because they taper to microscopic size in the peripheral lung. 7 to 8 cm is far beyond the normal 2 to 3 cm. Bronchioles are not normally visible at all depths. 1 to 2 cm is closer than the normal 2 to 3 cm. 5 to 6 cm is farther than the normal 2 to 3 cm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — in bronchiectasis the airways can be followed to the pleura, but normal bronchioles are not visible beyond 2–3 cm from the pleural surface; on cut section the dilated bronchi look cystic and are filled with mucopurulent secretions. COMLEX: Distribution matters — bilateral lower-lobe disease suggests a diffuse cause; localized disease suggests obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — bronchioles and their normal size and structure","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"7 to 8 cm is far beyond the normal 2 to 3 cm.","C":"Bronchioles are not normally visible at all depths.","D":"1 to 2 cm is closer than the normal 2 to 3 cm.","E":"5 to 6 cm is farther than the normal 2 to 3 cm."}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q15_b1","objective":8,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 8 — Bronchiectasis — pathogenesis, morphology, clinical features","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with cystic fibrosis develops worsening bronchiectasis with frequent exacerbations. Sputum cultures repeatedly grow the same organism known to form biofilms and cause progressive lung damage. Which organism is most likely responsible for this clinical pattern?","choices":{"A":"Klebsiella pneumoniae","B":"Streptococcus pneumoniae","C":"Staphylococcus epidermidis","D":"Moraxella catarrhalis","E":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pseudomonas aeruginosa accounts for 12-30% of bronchiectasis infections and is particularly common in cystic fibrosis patients. It forms biofilms, causes progressive lung damage, and leads to frequent exacerbations due to its virulence and antimicrobial resistance. Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus epidermidis, and Moraxella are not among the organisms Robbins ties to cystic fibrosis (Staphylococcus aureus, Haemophilus influenzae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa), and about 80% of patients are colonized by Pseudomonas by age 20.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — the inflamed, mucoid, sometimes anaerobic bronchiectatic airway supports colonization by relatively few microbial species, and P. aeruginosa is one of the main ones. FA: cystic fibrosis is the most common association of bronchiectasis. COMLEX: Recurrent Pseudomonas in CF airways drives repeated exacerbations and progressive lung damage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu","B":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu","C":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu","D":"Klebsiella, Streptococcus pneu"}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q16_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman presents with chronic productive cough and reports that her symptoms are significantly worse in the morning, requiring her to hang her head over the bed edge to clear secretions. She produces copious amounts of foul-smelling sputum daily. What finding best explains her postural symptom pattern?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bronchospasm from circadian rhythm changes","B":"Gravity-dependent drainage of pooled airway secretions","C":"Supine position worsening ventilation-perfusion mismatch","D":"Morning cortisol surge triggering inflammatory mediators","E":"Overnight accumulation of inspired environmental irritants"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The morning postural drainage symptom is classic for bronchiectasis. Overnight, secretions pool in the dilated, damaged airways due to impaired mucociliary clearance. In the morning, changing position allows gravity to drain these pooled secretions, causing productive cough. Robbins attributes the morning cough of bronchiectasis to positional drainage of pooled pus, whereas nighttime bronchoconstriction is typical of asthma. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to the change in position draining pooled pus and secretions, not to V/Q mismatch. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to a cortisol surge. Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to overnight irritant build-up.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — coughing paroxysms are particularly frequent on rising in the morning, because the change in position drains collections of pus and secretions into the bronchi. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab notes rib raising helps loosen tenacious mucus and improve rib motion in pulmonary disease, alongside physical therapy and antibiotics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — host defenses such as mucociliary clearance that bronchiectasis loses","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins attributes the morning cough of bronchiectasis to po","C":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to the change in po","D":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to a cortisol surge.","E":"Robbins attributes the morning paroxysms to positional drainage of pooled pus and secretions, not to overnight irritant "}},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q17_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients have bronchiectasis on CT. In the first, dilated bronchi involve the lower lobes of both lungs, mainly the vertical air passages. In the second, the dilation is confined to a single segment of the right middle lobe and the rest of both lungs is normal. Which cause is most likely in the second patient?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis with thick, viscous secretions","B":"Primary ciliary dyskinesia from dynein defects","C":"Severe necrotizing pneumonia of both lungs","D":"An idiopathic defect of host immunity to microbes","E":"Bronchial obstruction by tumor or foreign body"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins explains that bronchiectasis usually affects the lower lobes bilaterally, particularly vertical air passages, but when a tumor, aspiration of a foreign body, or mucus impaction obstructs a bronchus, the bronchiectasis is localized to the obstructed lung segment, because secretions pool and become infected distal to the obstruction. Cystic fibrosis and primary ciliary dyskinesia impair mucociliary clearance throughout the airways and cause diffuse disease. Severe necrotizing pneumonia of both lungs would not confine the damage to one segment. Idiopathic cases, up to 50% of the total, reflect dysfunctional host immunity and are not characteristically limited to one obstructed segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins — bronchiectasis usually affects the lower lobes bilaterally, particularly vertical air passages, and is most severe distally; when a tumor or aspirated foreign body causes it, involvement is localized. COMLEX: Robbins notes aspirated material enters the more vertical right mainstem bronchus more often, so localized right-sided disease raises suspicion of a foreign body.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 3 — atelectasis types, including obstructive atelectasis from a blocked bronchus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl19_ex1_q18_b1","objective":9,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 9 — Clinical differentiation of obstructive lung diseases (presentation, diagnostics, pathology)","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with dyspnea undergoes pulmonary function testing showing total lung capacity of 8.2 L (125% predicted) with FEV₁/FVC ratio of 0.68. High-resolution CT reveals widespread destruction of alveolar walls with enlarged airspaces. What mechanism explains the increased lung capacity?","choices":{"A":"Loss of elastic recoil from alveolar wall destruction","B":"Mucus hypersecretion causing airway distension","C":"Vascular remodeling increasing blood volume","D":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy increasing airway rigidity","E":"Pulmonary fibrosis reducing tissue compliance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The increased TLC (125% predicted) with obstructive pattern (FEV₁/FVC <0.70) and alveolar wall destruction indicates emphysema. Loss of elastic recoil from destroyed alveolar walls allows the lungs to hyperinflate, increasing total lung capacity and causing air trapping. Pulmonary fibrosis is restrictive: it reduces lung volume and compliance, the opposite of the raised TLC here. Mucus hypersecretion is the feature of chronic bronchitis; raised TLC with alveolar wall destruction reflects emphysema's low elastic recoil. Smooth muscle hypertrophy is a bronchial wall change of asthma and chronic bronchitis, not the alveolar wall destruction shown here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and elastic tissue, so elastic recoil falls (Robbins Table 15.4), compliance rises (FA), and the lungs hyperinflate — TLC and RV rise with FEV1/FVC < 0.7. COMLEX: The pulmonary OMM lab ties COPD hyperinflation to a barrel chest with shortened, high-tone respiratory muscles.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — the four types of emphysema and their morphology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucus hypersecretion is the feature of chronic bronchitis; r","D":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy is a bronchial wall change of asth"}}],"22":[{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with newly diagnosed COPD is started on maintenance therapy with two inhaled medications: one is a long-acting β2-agonist taken twice daily, and the other is a long-acting muscarinic antagonist with once-daily dosing. Which combination is most likely prescribed for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Terbutaline and aclidinium","B":"Albuterol and ipratropium","C":"Salmeterol and montelukast","D":"Theophylline and budesonide","E":"Formoterol and tiotropium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of formoterol (a long-acting β2-agonist with 12-hour duration requiring twice-daily dosing) and tiotropium (a long-acting antimuscarinic agent with 24-hour duration allowing once-daily dosing) is a standard maintenance regimen for COPD. This combination provides sustained bronchodilation through complementary mechanisms. Terbutaline is a short-acting agonist, and aclidinium, though a long-acting antimuscarinic, lasts about 12 hours and is taken twice daily; albuterol and ipratropium are both short-acting; montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist, not an antimuscarinic; theophylline is a methylxanthine and budesonide an inhaled corticosteroid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LABAs salmeterol and formoterol last about 12 hours (twice daily); tiotropium and umeclidinium last 24 hours (once daily), while aclidinium lasts 12 hours. Katzung: daily tiotropium improves functional capacity and reduces COPD exacerbations. COMLEX: FA — muscarinic antagonists block bronchoconstriction and are used in COPD; tiotropium is the long-acting agent.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — current asthma and COPD treatment guidelines","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with persistent asthma takes an oral medication daily that blocks leukotriene receptors. She reports this medication also helps control her allergic rhinitis symptoms. Which drug class does this medication belong to?","choices":{"A":"Leukotriene pathway modifiers","B":"Methylxanthine phosphodiesterase inhibitors","C":"Mast cell stabilizers","D":"Long-acting beta-2 adrenergic agonists","E":"Inhaled corticosteroids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Montelukast is a leukotriene receptor antagonist (LTD4-receptor antagonist) taken orally once daily. It belongs to the leukotriene pathway modifiers class and is particularly useful for patients with concurrent asthma and allergic rhinitis, as it effectively treats both conditions. Methylxanthines such as theophylline inhibit phosphodiesterase, mast cell stabilizers (cromolyn, nedocromil) prevent degranulation, long-acting β2 agonists are inhaled bronchodilators, and inhaled corticosteroids suppress inflammation; none of them blocks the leukotriene receptor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leukotriene pathway modifiers — zafirlukast and montelukast block the LTD4 (CysLT1) receptor, and zileuton inhibits 5-lipoxygenase; all three improve asthma control and reduce exacerbations, though less effectively than even low-dose ICS (Katzung). FA: antileukotrienes are especially good for aspirin-induced and exercise-induced asthma. COMLEX: Katzung — many patients prefer them because they work when taken orally.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — asthma phenotypes, including aspirin-sensitive asthma","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with severe asthma uncontrolled by high-dose inhaled corticosteroids and long-acting β2-agonists has elevated peripheral eosinophil counts. His physician prescribes a subcutaneous injection targeting interleukin-5. Which pharmacological class does this medication represent?","choices":{"A":"Short-acting muscarinic antagonists","B":"Phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitors","C":"Leukotriene synthesis inhibitors","D":"Targeted monoclonal antibody therapy","E":"Methylxanthine bronchodilators"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibodies (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab) represent targeted biologic therapy for severe asthma with eosinophilia that remains uncontrolled despite ICS/LABA treatment. These biologics reduce eosinophil numbers and activity in the airway mucosa. Short-acting muscarinic antagonists such as ipratropium are inhaled bronchodilators, roflumilast is an oral PDE4 inhibitor, leukotriene synthesis inhibitors such as zileuton block 5-lipoxygenase, and theophylline is an oral methylxanthine; none is an injected antibody against IL-5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monoclonal antibodies for asthma (Katzung Table 20–1) — omalizumab (IgE), mepolizumab and reslizumab (IL-5), benralizumab (IL-5 receptor), dupilumab (IL-4 receptor), tezepelumab (TSLP); anti-IL-5 agents treat severe eosinophilic asthma. FA: IL-5 blockade prevents eosinophil differentiation, maturation, activation and survival. COMLEX: Peripheral eosinophilia in uncontrolled asthma on ICS/LABA identifies the patient likely to respond.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — environmental factors and the eosinophil-rich morphology of status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Short-acting muscarinic antago"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma experiences daily symptoms and nocturnal awakenings more than once a week. Her physician recommends daily controller therapy to prevent bronchial inflammation and hyperreactivity. What is the primary therapeutic goal of initiating inhaled corticosteroid therapy in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Provide immediate bronchodilation during acute symptoms","B":"Block acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors","C":"Antagonize leukotriene receptors in smooth muscle","D":"Inhibit phosphodiesterase to increase cyclic AMP","E":"Reduce airway inflammation and prevent exacerbations"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The primary therapeutic goal of inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) therapy is to reduce chronic airway inflammation by inhibiting inflammatory cytokine production. This anti-inflammatory action prevents exacerbations, reduces bronchial hyperreactivity, and maintains long-term asthma control. ICS therapy is the most effective controller medication for asthma. Blocking acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors is the action of antimuscarinics such as ipratropium and tiotropium, and leukotriene receptor antagonism is the action of montelukast and zafirlukast, not of corticosteroids. Immediate bronchodilation is the job of relievers such as albuterol; inhaled corticosteroids do not relax airway smooth muscle directly but act against inflammation. Phosphodiesterase inhibition is a proposed action of theophylline and roflumilast, not of inhaled corticosteroids, which act by broad anti-inflammatory effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corticosteroids do not relax airway smooth muscle directly; their most important action is inhibiting infiltration of the airways by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells, and regular use reduces bronchial reactivity and exacerbations (Katzung). FA: ICS inhibit synthesis of virtually all cytokines by inactivating NF-κB and are first-line for chronic asthma. COMLEX: Katzung calls ICS the core 'long-term controllers' of asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the Th2 inflammation that controller therapy suppresses","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Immediate bronchodilation is the job of relievers such as al","D":"Phosphodiesterase inhibition is a proposed action of theophy"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with COPD experiences progressive dyspnea and demonstrates irreversible airflow obstruction on spirometry. His physician prescribes tiotropium to improve his functional capacity. What is the primary mechanism by which long-acting antimuscarinic agents achieve therapeutic benefit in COPD?","choices":{"A":"Stimulating adenylyl cyclase to increase cAMP production","B":"Inhibiting inflammatory cytokine gene transcription","C":"Blocking leukotriene-mediated bronchoconstriction","D":"Reducing parasympathetic-mediated airway tone","E":"Preventing mast cell degranulation and mediator release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-acting antimuscarinic agents like tiotropium competitively block acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors, thereby reducing parasympathetic (vagal) tone in the airways. This results in sustained bronchodilation and reduced mucus secretion. In COPD, anticholinergics are first-line maintenance therapy and have been shown to reduce exacerbation frequency. Raising cAMP through adenylyl cyclase is the β-agonist mechanism, blocking cytokine gene transcription is the corticosteroid mechanism, and preventing mast cell degranulation is the action of cromolyn and nedocromil. Blocking leukotriene-mediated bronchoconstriction is the action of leukotriene antagonists, not tiotropium, which is an antimuscarinic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Muscarinic antagonists block acetylcholine from vagal motor nerves, preventing airway smooth-muscle contraction and the mucus secretion vagal activity drives (Katzung). Guyton: vagal fibers cause mild to moderate bronchiolar constriction, worsened when disease has already narrowed the airways. COMLEX: FA — tiotropium (long-acting) and ipratropium are used in COPD; Katzung notes ipratropium is about as effective as albuterol in COPD with partly reversible obstruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — sympathetic versus parasympathetic effects on bronchiole diameter","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Blocking leukotriene-mediated bronchoconstriction is the act"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with asthma uses albuterol as needed for symptom relief but requires it more than twice weekly. Her physician adds a daily inhaled corticosteroid to achieve better disease control. What fundamental distinction exists between the therapeutic strategies of short-term relievers and long-term controllers in asthma management?","choices":{"A":"Relievers inhibit phosphodiesterase; controllers stimulate adenylyl cyclase","B":"Relievers block leukotriene receptors; controllers inhibit mast cells","C":"Relievers prevent exacerbations; controllers treat acute symptoms","D":"Relievers provide bronchodilation; controllers reduce inflammation","E":"Relievers reduce eosinophils; controllers antagonize muscarinic receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Short-term relievers (SABAs like albuterol) provide rapid bronchodilation for acute symptom relief by stimulating β2-adrenergic receptors. Long-term controllers (ICS) reduce chronic airway inflammation, prevent exacerbations, and decrease bronchial hyperreactivity. This fundamental distinction guides the stepwise approach to asthma management. This misassigns the mechanisms: beta agonist relievers stimulate adenylyl cyclase, while the main controllers are inhaled corticosteroids acting against inflammation. Relievers are bronchodilators, not leukotriene receptor blockers, and the main controllers are anti-inflammatory inhaled corticosteroids. Relievers are bronchodilators, and the main controllers are inhaled corticosteroids, which curb eosinophil infiltration, rather than muscarinic antagonists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung divides asthma therapy into 'short-term relievers' for acute bronchoconstriction (inhaled β2 agonists such as albuterol) and 'long-term controllers' that reduce symptoms and prevent attacks (especially inhaled corticosteroids). FA: albuterol is for acute symptoms; ICS are first-line for chronic asthma. COMLEX: Needing a reliever more often is the cue to add or step up a controller.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — current pharmacologic treatment guidelines for asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"This misassigns the mechanisms: beta agonist relievers stimu","B":"Relievers are bronchodilators, not leukotriene receptor bloc","E":"Relievers are bronchodilators, and the main controllers are "}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman using high-dose inhaled corticosteroids for asthma control reports persistent hoarseness and white patches in her mouth. Physical examination reveals oropharyngeal candidiasis. What adverse effect profile is most characteristic of inhaled corticosteroid therapy?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and tachycardia","B":"Hepatotoxicity and elevated liver enzymes","C":"Nausea, vomiting, and cardiac arrhythmias","D":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","E":"Urinary retention and cognitive impairment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The most common local adverse effects of inhaled corticosteroids are oropharyngeal candidiasis (thrush) and dysphonia (hoarseness). These effects result from local drug deposition in the oropharynx. Risk can be reduced by using a spacer device and gargling/expectorating after each use. The candidiasis is easily treated with topical clotrimazole. Tremor and tachycardia belong to β2 agonists, liver enzyme elevation to zileuton, nausea and arrhythmias to theophylline, and urinary retention with cognitive concerns to antimuscarinics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A special problem of inhaled corticosteroids is oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia; gargling water after use and using a spacer reduce the risk, and candidiasis responds to topical clotrimazole (Katzung). FA: use a spacer or rinse the mouth to prevent thrush. COMLEX: Katzung — chronic ICS use may also raise osteoporosis and cataract risk and slows children's growth by about 1 cm in the first year.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale for each pharmacologic class","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with COPD is prescribed theophylline for additional bronchodilation. His physician orders serum drug level monitoring due to the medication's narrow therapeutic index. Which adverse effect profile is most concerning with theophylline therapy?","choices":{"A":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and adrenal suppression","B":"Dry mouth and urinary retention","C":"Neuropsychiatric effects and hepatotoxicity","D":"Skeletal muscle tremor and hypokalemia","E":"Nausea, tremor, arrhythmias, and seizures"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index and requires serum level monitoring. Its dose-dependent adverse effects include nausea, vomiting, tremor, cardiac arrhythmias, and at toxic levels, seizures. These central nervous system and cardiac toxicities, combined with numerous drug-drug interactions affecting metabolism, have made theophylline largely obsolete in asthma treatment. Oropharyngeal candidiasis and adrenal suppression are inhaled corticosteroid effects, and dry mouth with urinary retention are antimuscarinic effects. Neuropsychiatric events and liver monitoring belong to montelukast and zileuton; Katzung lists theophylline toxicity as nausea, vomiting, tremulousness and arrhythmias. Tremor with low potassium is Katzung's beta-agonist profile; theophylline's narrow-index toxicity is nausea, vomiting, tremulousness and arrhythmias, with convulsions in overdose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Theophylline is a phosphodiesterase inhibitor that raises cAMP (and blocks adenosine); its use has declined because of toxicities — nausea, vomiting, tremulousness, arrhythmias — and the need for serum level monitoring given a narrow therapeutic index and variable metabolism (Katzung). FA: narrow therapeutic index with cardiotoxicity and neurotoxicity; metabolized by cytochrome P-450. COMLEX: Watch drug interactions — Katzung notes frequent drug–drug interactions make monitoring more necessary.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the therapeutic rationale and place of each drug class","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Neuropsychiatric events and liver monitoring belong to monte","D":"Tremor with low potassium is Katzung's beta-agonist profile;"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with asthma is prescribed a long-acting β2-agonist in combination with an inhaled corticosteroid. Her physician emphasizes that the LABA should never be used as monotherapy for asthma. What is the primary safety concern that necessitates concurrent inhaled corticosteroid therapy with LABA use?","choices":{"A":"LABAs cause adrenal suppression requiring steroid coverage","B":"LABAs provide bronchodilation but lack anti-inflammatory action","C":"LABAs reduce bronchial hyperreactivity only with steroids","D":"LABAs increase risk of cardiac arrhythmias without protection","E":"LABAs cause oropharyngeal candidiasis prevented by steroids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long-acting β2-agonists (LABAs) provide sustained bronchodilation but have no anti-inflammatory action. When used as monotherapy, they can mask ongoing airway inflammation while patients experience prolonged symptom relief, potentially leading to severe exacerbations. LABAs should always be combined with inhaled corticosteroids for asthma treatment to address both bronchoconstriction and inflammation. Adrenal suppression is a corticosteroid effect, not a LABA effect; LABAs need an inhaled steroid because they have no anti-inflammatory action. LABAs do not reduce hyperreactivity; the reason for combining them is that they lack anti-inflammatory action. Katzung calls the arrhythmia concern with inhaled beta agonists unsubstantiated; LABA monotherapy is avoided because LABAs lack anti-inflammatory action. Candidiasis is an inhaled corticosteroid effect, not a LABA effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LABAs have no anti-inflammatory action, so they should not be used as monotherapy for asthma; their prolonged bronchodilation masks symptoms of bronchial inflammation, so they are given only with an ICS in asthma — though they may be used alone in COPD (Katzung). COMLEX: Katzung notes LABAs also appear to interact with inhaled corticosteroids to improve asthma control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — guideline differences between asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Adrenal suppression is a corticosteroid effect, not a LABA e","C":"LABAs do not reduce hyperreactivity; the reason for combinin","D":"Katzung calls the arrhythmia concern with inhaled beta agoni","E":"Candidiasis is an inhaled corticosteroid effect, not a LABA "}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man presents to the emergency department with an acute COPD exacerbation. He receives nebulized treatments combining a short-acting β2-agonist with a short-acting muscarinic antagonist for rapid bronchodilation. Which medication combination is most likely administered?","choices":{"A":"Formoterol and aclidinium","B":"Salmeterol and tiotropium","C":"Albuterol and ipratropium","D":"Terbutaline and montelukast","E":"Theophylline and budesonide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The combination of albuterol (a short-acting β2-agonist) and ipratropium (a short-acting antimuscarinic agent) is the standard nebulized treatment for acute COPD exacerbations. This combination provides rapid bronchodilation through complementary mechanisms: β2-receptor stimulation and muscarinic receptor blockade. Formoterol, salmeterol, aclidinium, and tiotropium are long-acting maintenance drugs, montelukast is a leukotriene antagonist used for prophylaxis, and theophylline and budesonide are a methylxanthine and an inhaled corticosteroid, so none of those pairs is a short-acting β2 agonist plus a short-acting antimuscarinic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Albuterol is a short-acting β2 agonist (relaxes airway smooth muscle via cAMP); ipratropium is an inhaled quaternary atropine analog, poorly absorbed and so relatively free of systemic atropine-like effects (Katzung). FA: bronchoconstriction has an inflammatory and a parasympathetic arm, so the two bronchodilators act on separate pathways. COMLEX: Guyton — airway irritation from dust, smoke or infection triggers vagal constrictor reflexes, which antimuscarinics block.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — sympathetic versus parasympathetic control of bronchioles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with persistent asthma is prescribed a daily inhaled medication containing both fluticasone and salmeterol in a single device. Which two pharmacological classes are combined in this formulation?","choices":{"A":"Short-acting β2-agonist and leukotriene modifier","B":"Antimuscarinic agent and methylxanthine","C":"Inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting β2-agonist","D":"Mast cell stabilizer and phosphodiesterase inhibitor","E":"Leukotriene modifier and monoclonal antibody"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fluticasone/salmeterol combines an inhaled corticosteroid (ICS) with a long-acting β2-agonist (LABA) in a single inhaler. These combination products (also include budesonide/formoterol, mometasone/formoterol) are widely used for asthma maintenance therapy, providing both anti-inflammatory control and sustained bronchodilation. Fluticasone is not an antimuscarinic, mast cell stabilizer, or leukotriene modifier, and salmeterol is not a methylxanthine, phosphodiesterase inhibitor, or monoclonal antibody. Fluticasone is a corticosteroid and salmeterol is a long-acting, not short-acting, beta agonist, and neither is a leukotriene modifier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Fluticasone/salmeterol pairs an ICS (anti-inflammatory controller) with a 12-hour LABA (bronchodilator); because LABAs lack anti-inflammatory action, asthma guidance uses them only with an ICS (Katzung). FA: ICS inhibit cytokine synthesis via NF-κB; salmeterol and formoterol relax bronchial smooth muscle. COMLEX: Rinse the mouth or use a spacer after the ICS component to prevent thrush (FA).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 6 — using pathophysiology to choose and justify regimens","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Fluticasone is a corticosteroid and salmeterol is a long-act"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — List and categorize commonly prescribed medications within each pharmacological class used for the treatment of asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with severe COPD inadequately controlled on bronchodilators is prescribed an oral medication that selectively inhibits phosphodiesterase-4 to reduce inflammation and exacerbation frequency. Which drug class does this medication represent?","choices":{"A":"Methylxanthine phosphodiesterase inhibitors","B":"Selective PDE4 inhibitors","C":"Leukotriene synthesis inhibitors","D":"Long-acting muscarinic antagonists","E":"Targeted monoclonal antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Roflumilast is a selective phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor approved for severe COPD with chronic bronchitis and history of exacerbations. Unlike theophylline (a non-selective PDE inhibitor), selective PDE4 inhibition reduces inflammatory cell activity and exacerbation frequency with fewer cardiac and CNS effects. Long-acting muscarinic antagonists are inhaled bronchodilators that block acetylcholine, and monoclonal antibodies are injected agents aimed at IgE, IL-5, or related targets; neither inhibits PDE4. Leukotriene synthesis inhibitors act on 5-lipoxygenase, not on phosphodiesterase-4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Roflumilast, a selective PDE4 inhibitor, improves pulmonary function and reduces exacerbation frequency and is FDA-approved for COPD; other PDE4 inhibitors were abandoned for nausea, headache and diarrhea (Katzung). FA: roflumilast raises cAMP for bronchodilation and less airway inflammation — used in COPD only. COMLEX: It suits the bronchitis-dominant, exacerbation-prone patient (Robbins' 'blue bloater' pattern).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the pink puffer and blue bloater phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Unlike theophylline (a non-sel","C":"Leukotriene synthesis inhibitors act on 5-lipoxygenase, not "}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with asthma uses albuterol only before exercise to prevent exercise-induced bronchoconstriction. He experiences asthma symptoms less than twice weekly and has no nocturnal awakenings. What is the primary therapeutic goal of this as-needed β2-agonist strategy?","choices":{"A":"Provide rapid bronchodilation for symptom relief","B":"Inhibit leukotriene-mediated inflammatory responses","C":"Suppress eosinophil recruitment to airway mucosa","D":"Reduce chronic airway inflammation and prevent remodeling","E":"Block vagally-mediated bronchoconstriction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For mild intermittent asthma with infrequent symptoms, as-needed short-acting β2-agonists (SABAs) provide rapid bronchodilation for acute symptom relief or pre-exercise prophylaxis. This patient does not require daily controller therapy. The therapeutic goal is immediate relief of bronchoconstriction, not chronic inflammation control. Suppressing eosinophil recruitment is an anti-inflammatory goal served by corticosteroids or anti-IL-5 antibodies; β2 agonists have no anti-inflammatory action. Blocking vagal bronchoconstriction is an antimuscarinic action; albuterol is a beta-2 agonist producing rapid bronchodilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A short-acting β2 agonist is a 'short-term reliever' — rapid bronchodilation for acute symptoms (FA: albuterol is for acute symptoms); Katzung notes leukotriene modifiers also block airway responses to exercise. COMLEX: Robbins — exercise and cold can trigger attacks, especially in nonatopic asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — asthma phenotypes and triggers such as exercise","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Suppressing eosinophil recruit","D":"The therapeutic goal is immedi","E":"Blocking vagal bronchoconstriction is an antimuscarinic acti"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is started on combination fluticasone/salmeterol therapy. Her physician explains that combining these medications allows better asthma control than using either agent alone at higher doses. What pharmacological interaction explains this synergistic effect?","choices":{"A":"LABAs enhance histone deacetylase activity of corticosteroids","B":"LABAs directly inhibit eosinophil infiltration of the airways","C":"Corticosteroids potentiate β2 agonists and suppress inflammation","D":"Corticosteroids directly relax airway smooth muscle within minutes","E":"LABAs inhibit inflammatory cytokine transcription"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Corticosteroids do not relax airway smooth muscle directly; they reduce airway obstruction mainly by inhibiting the infiltration of the airways by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells, and in part by potentiating the effects of β-receptor agonists (Katzung Ch 20). The LABA supplies sustained bronchodilation, which the corticosteroid lacks, and salmeterol is described as potentiating corticosteroid effects; this complementary action is why adding a LABA is more effective than doubling the ICS dose. LABAs do not block eosinophil infiltration or cytokine transcription — those are corticosteroid actions — and enhancement of histone deacetylase is not the explanation offered for the combination.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — corticosteroids reduce airway obstruction mainly by inhibiting infiltration by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells, and in part by contracting engorged mucosal vessels and potentiating β-agonist effects; salmeterol in turn potentiates corticosteroid effects. COMLEX: The combination lets a lower ICS dose achieve control, limiting thrush, dysphonia and the bone and eye risks of high doses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — the rationale behind each pharmacologic class","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"LABAs do not block eosinophil "}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacological strategies for asthma and COPD management, focusing on the key therapeutic goals","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with asthma demonstrates poor adherence to inhaled corticosteroid therapy, resulting in frequent exacerbations requiring emergency department visits and oral prednisone courses. What fundamental principle of asthma management is violated by discontinuing controller therapy during symptom-free periods?","choices":{"A":"Oral steroids are more effective than inhaled steroids","B":"Relievers provide superior long-term disease control","C":"Controller therapy must be taken continuously to work","D":"Bronchodilators alone prevent airway remodeling","E":"Exacerbations only occur during viral infections"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A fundamental principle of asthma management is that controller medications (especially ICS) must be used continuously, even during symptom-free periods, to maintain anti-inflammatory effects and prevent exacerbations. Discontinuing controller therapy allows airway inflammation to worsen, increasing exacerbation risk. This is distinct from as-needed reliever use. Because of severe toxicity with chronic use, oral corticosteroids are reserved for urgent treatment, while inhaled steroids are the controllers. Relievers give symptom relief, not long-term control. Bronchodilators relieve bronchospasm but have no anti-inflammatory action, which is why LABAs are not used alone in asthma. Robbins lists many triggers besides viral infections, including irritants, cold air, stress and exercise.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhaled corticosteroids reduce bronchial reactivity and the frequency of exacerbations only if taken regularly (Katzung); they are FA's first-line therapy for chronic asthma. COMLEX: Katzung notes many of the roughly 3500 annual US asthma deaths are considered preventable — adherence to controller therapy is central.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — environmental factors and the morphology of fatal status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Because of severe toxicity with chronic use, oral corticoste","B":"Relievers give symptom relief, not long-term control.","D":"Bronchodilators relieve bronchospasm but have no anti-inflam","E":"Robbins lists many triggers besides viral infections, includ"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with acute asthma exacerbation receives multiple nebulized albuterol treatments in the emergency department. She develops fine tremor in her hands and complains of palpitations. Her ECG shows sinus tachycardia at 110 bpm. Which adverse effect profile is characteristic of high-dose β2-agonist therapy?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and tachycardia","B":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","C":"Nausea, vomiting, and cardiac arrhythmias","D":"Dry mouth and urinary retention","E":"Neuropsychiatric effects and hepatotoxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"High-dose or frequent use of β2-agonists causes dose-dependent adverse effects including skeletal muscle tremor (most common), tachycardia, palpitations, and hypokalemia. These effects result from β2-receptor stimulation in non-airway tissues. While β2-selective agents minimize cardiac effects compared to non-selective agents, they still occur at higher doses. Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia come from inhaled corticosteroids, and dry mouth with urinary retention comes from antimuscarinics. Nausea, vomiting and arrhythmias is Katzung's theophylline profile, not albuterol's tremor and tachycardia. Neuropsychiatric events and liver monitoring are linked to montelukast and zileuton, not to beta agonists, whose effects are tremor, tachycardia and low potassium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: β-agonist adverse effects — tachycardia, skeletal muscle tremor and decreased serum potassium (β2 stimulation of Na+/K+-ATPase drives K+ into cells); overdose can cause arrhythmias (Katzung). FA: inhaled β2 agonists can cause tremor and arrhythmia. COMLEX: Check potassium when a patient receives repeated nebulized albuterol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — sympathetic β-adrenergic dilation of the bronchioles","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Nausea, vomiting and arrhythmias is Katzung's theophylline p","E":"Neuropsychiatric events and liver monitoring are linked to m"}},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with severe asthma requires chronic oral prednisone therapy to maintain disease control despite maximal inhaled therapy. After 6 months, he develops hyperglycemia, weight gain, and decreased bone mineral density. Which adverse effect profile is associated with chronic systemic corticosteroid therapy?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and hypokalemia","B":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","C":"Dry mouth, urinary retention, and dementia risk","D":"Nausea, tremor, and cardiac arrhythmias","E":"Hyperglycemia, osteoporosis, and immunosuppression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic systemic corticosteroid therapy causes multiple serious adverse effects including hyperglycemia/diabetes, osteoporosis, weight gain, immunosuppression, adrenal suppression, hypertension, cataracts, and cushingoid features. These toxicities underscore the importance of minimizing systemic steroid use and maximizing inhaled therapy to achieve adequate asthma control. Tremor and hypokalemia are β2-agonist effects, candidiasis and dysphonia are local effects of inhaled corticosteroids, dry mouth, urinary retention, and dementia risk are antimuscarinic effects, and nausea, tremor, and arrhythmias are theophylline toxicities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Systemic glucocorticoids (FA) cause Cushing syndrome, osteoporosis, hyperglycemia and immunosuppression; Katzung uses prednisone 30–60 mg/day for severe exacerbations and tapers it, and when switching chronic oral therapy to an ICS, tapers slowly to avoid adrenal insufficiency. COMLEX: Higher-dose ICS often allows oral prednisone to be tapered and stopped, because ICS systemic risks are negligible by comparison (Katzung).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — guideline steps, including when oral corticosteroids are used","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the key adverse effects and safety considerations associated with the major pharmacological classes used to treat asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with COPD is prescribed ipratropium and tiotropium for maintenance therapy. His physician advises against using both short-acting and long-acting antimuscarinic agents concurrently due to dose-dependent adverse effects. Which adverse effect profile necessitates this precaution?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle tremor and tachycardia","B":"Oropharyngeal candidiasis and dysphonia","C":"Hyperglycemia and adrenal suppression","D":"Dry mouth, urinary retention, and confusion","E":"Nausea, vomiting, and ventricular arrhythmias"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antimuscarinic agents cause dose-dependent anticholinergic adverse effects including dry mouth (most common), urinary retention in susceptible individuals, and potential cognitive impairment/dementia risk with advancing age. Concurrent use of short-acting and long-acting antimuscarinics increases these risks and is not recommended. Spacer use and dose reduction can minimize symptoms. Tremor and tachycardia are β2-agonist effects, candidiasis and dysphonia are inhaled corticosteroid effects, hyperglycemia and adrenal suppression are corticosteroid effects, and nausea, vomiting, and arrhythmias are theophylline toxicities.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Inhaled antimuscarinics can cause dry mouth (managed with a spacer or lower dose), rarely urinary retention in susceptible people, and raise concern for dementia with advancing age; because these effects are dose dependent, combining short- and long-acting inhaled antimuscarinics is not recommended (Katzung). COMLEX: Ask older men with COPD about urinary hesitancy before adding antimuscarinic therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — therapeutic rationale and safety of each class","isHighYield":false}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q01_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with persistent asthma inadequately controlled on low-dose inhaled corticosteroids is started on combination fluticasone/salmeterol therapy instead of doubling her ICS dose. What therapeutic rationale supports adding a LABA rather than increasing the corticosteroid dose?","choices":{"A":"Combination therapy reduces medication administration frequency","B":"LABAs prevent corticosteroid-induced adrenal suppression","C":"Higher ICS doses cause unacceptable systemic toxicity","D":"LABAs provide anti-inflammatory effects equivalent to ICS","E":"ICS plus LABA works better than doubling the ICS dose"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Multiple studies demonstrate that adding a long-acting β2-agonist (LABA) to a standard ICS dose provides better asthma control than doubling the ICS dose. The combination achieves synergistic effects: ICS increases β2-receptor expression and prevents receptor downregulation, while LABAs may enhance corticosteroid anti-inflammatory effects. Systemic toxicity is not the reason: Katzung notes that even high inhaled doses cause at most mild adrenal suppression, with risks negligible compared with oral corticosteroids. Katzung's rationale is that ICS plus LABA is more effective than doubling the ICS dose, not fewer doses to take.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When a standard ICS dose fails, Katzung's options are to double the ICS or add a drug; theophylline or a leukotriene antagonist adds modest benefit, but adding a LABA (salmeterol, formoterol) gives the most impressive gains and has repeatedly beaten doubling the ICS dose. COMLEX: Fixed-dose ICS–LABA inhalers (fluticasone/salmeterol, budesonide/formoterol) keep the LABA always paired with its ICS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — the pharmacologic strategies and goals in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Katzung's rationale is that ICS plus LABA is more effective "}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q02_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old man with severe COPD and frequent exacerbations despite LABA/LAMA therapy undergoes blood testing showing peripheral eosinophilia. His physician considers adding inhaled corticosteroids based on this biomarker. What therapeutic rationale supports using eosinophil counts to guide ICS therapy in COPD?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophils indicate bacterial infection requiring antibiotics","B":"Low eosinophil counts contraindicate bronchodilator use","C":"Elevated eosinophils predict ICS treatment responsiveness","D":"Eosinophilia indicates need for systemic corticosteroids","E":"Eosinophil levels correlate with theophylline efficacy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recent evidence-based guidelines recommend using blood eosinophil levels to predict ICS benefit in COPD. Elevated blood eosinophils identify patients with T2-high inflammation more likely to respond to ICS therapy, while low eosinophils suggest lower likelihood of benefit and potentially increased pneumonia risk from ICS use. Katzung uses blood eosinophil counts to judge the likely benefit of ICS in COPD, not to detect bacterial infection. Katzung uses blood eosinophils to judge the likely benefit of inhaled corticosteroids, not a need for systemic steroids. Eosinophils guide ICS use, not theophylline; a large trial of low-dose theophylline showed no benefit on COPD exacerbations.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — ICS is less central in COPD (lower efficacy; associated bacterial pneumonia risk) and is recommended for severe obstruction, prior exacerbations, or a clear asthma history; guidelines now use low versus high blood eosinophils to predict low versus reasonable ICS benefit, mirroring T2-low and T2-high asthma. COMLEX: Katzung — COPD inflammation is commonly neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic, which is why most COPD responds poorly to ICS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 3 — the clinical phenotypes of COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Katzung uses blood eosinophil counts to judge the likely ben","D":"Katzung uses blood eosinophils to judge the likely benefit o","E":"Eosinophils guide ICS use, not theophylline; a large trial o"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q03_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with severe eosinophilic asthma uncontrolled on high-dose ICS/LABA therapy is started on mepolizumab, an anti-IL-5 monoclonal antibody. What therapeutic rationale explains targeting IL-5 in this patient's treatment regimen?","choices":{"A":"IL-5 drives eosinophil maturation and survival","B":"IL-5 activates mast cell degranulation","C":"IL-5 stimulates mucus hypersecretion in airways","D":"IL-5 enhances β2-receptor downregulation","E":"IL-5 increases IgE production by B lymphocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Interleukin-5 (IL-5) is the primary cytokine responsible for eosinophil recruitment, activation, and survival in the airways. Anti-IL-5 biologics (mepolizumab, reslizumab, benralizumab) reduce eosinophil numbers and activity, providing benefit in severe eosinophilic asthma phenotypes that remain uncontrolled despite maximal inhaled therapy. β2-receptor downregulation (tachyphylaxis) follows repeated β-agonist use, not IL-5, and B-cell IgE production is driven mainly by IL-4 (with IL-13), not IL-5. Katzung describes IL-5 as a pro-eosinophilic cytokine; mast cell degranulation is triggered by allergen cross-linking of mast cell-bound IgE. Robbins assigns mucus secretion to IL-13 and eosinophil activation to IL-5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Some severe asthma has airway and peripheral eosinophilia driven by IL-5-secreting T2 lymphocytes; mepolizumab and reslizumab (anti-IL-5) and benralizumab (anti-IL-5 receptor) treat severe eosinophilic asthma (Katzung). FA: IL-5 blockade prevents eosinophil differentiation, maturation, activation and survival. COMLEX: Robbins — IL-5 activates recruited eosinophils, whose major basic protein and cationic protein damage the epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the Th2 response at the heart of asthma","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Katzung describes IL-5 as a pro-eosinophilic cytokine; mast ","C":"Robbins assigns mucus secretion to IL-13 and eosinophil acti"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q04_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with asthma experiences symptoms four times weekly requiring albuterol use. He wakes at night with symptoms twice a month, and his FEV₁ is 85% of predicted. According to current asthma management guidelines, what is the appropriate initial pharmacological approach for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Add low-dose ICS to as-needed SABA","B":"Add leukotriene modifier to as-needed SABA","C":"Start combination ICS/LABA therapy","D":"Continue as-needed SABA monotherapy only","E":"Initiate daily LABA with as-needed SABA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has mild persistent asthma: symptoms on more than 2 days a week but not daily, infrequent nocturnal symptoms and an FEV₁ above 80% of predicted. Guidelines recommend starting controller therapy with low-dose inhaled corticosteroid, the most effective controller medication, while keeping a SABA for relief; a leukotriene modifier is an alternative if ICS is not tolerated. SABA alone is no longer adequate once symptoms are persistent, ICS/LABA combination belongs to the next step up, and a LABA must never be used without an ICS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — when rescue therapy is needed more than twice a week, nocturnal symptoms occur more than twice a month, or FEV1 is below 80% of predicted, add treatment; the first recommendation is low-dose ICS, with a leukotriene receptor antagonist as an alternative. COMLEX: Katzung warns that even mild asthma carries a low but nonzero risk of a severe, life-threatening attack.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 2 — therapeutic goals and strategies in asthma management","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"SABA alone is no longer adequa","E":"SABA alone is no longer adequa"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q05_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with COPD experiences persistent dyspnea limiting his daily activities despite using as-needed albuterol. Spirometry shows FEV₁ 55% predicted with minimal reversibility. According to GOLD guidelines, what is the most appropriate initial maintenance therapy?","choices":{"A":"High-dose inhaled corticosteroid monotherapy","B":"Theophylline with as-needed bronchodilators","C":"Long-acting bronchodilator (LABA or LAMA)","D":"Combination of short-acting bronchodilators only","E":"Oral prednisone for continuous inflammation control"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For COPD patients with persistent symptoms and activity limitation, GOLD guidelines recommend initiating long-acting bronchodilator therapy (LABA or LAMA, or both). LAMAs are particularly effective first-line agents for COPD. Unlike asthma, ICS is not first-line therapy for COPD and is reserved for patients with severe obstruction, frequent exacerbations, or elevated eosinophils. Inhaled corticosteroid monotherapy is not first-line in COPD, which responds poorly even to high-dose ICS, and oral prednisone is a short course for exacerbations, not continuous maintenance, because of its systemic toxicity. Katzung advises a long-acting bronchodilator for persistent COPD symptoms, and a large trial of low-dose theophylline showed no exacerbation benefit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung (following GOLD) — for COPD with persistent exertional dyspnea and activity limitation, use a long-acting bronchodilator regularly: a LABA, a long-acting anticholinergic, or both; short-acting β agonist and/or ipratropium relieve acute symptoms. LABAs may be used alone in COPD, unlike asthma. COMLEX: Katzung — daily tiotropium improves functional capacity and reduces exacerbations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the drug classes used in asthma and COPD","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Katzung advises a long-acting bronchodilator for persistent "}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q06_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with acute respiratory distress. Her history reveals she has both asthma and COPD. She is treated with oxygen, nebulized bronchodilators, and systemic corticosteroids. What represents a key difference in the management of acute exacerbations between asthma and COPD?","choices":{"A":"Asthma exacerbations require oxygen; COPD exacerbations do not","B":"Asthma exacerbations use bronchodilators; COPD uses only steroids","C":"COPD exacerbations require hospitalization; asthma can be outpatient","D":"Asthma exacerbations use systemic steroids; COPD uses only inhaled","E":"COPD flares routinely get antibiotics; asthma flares usually do not"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A key difference in managing acute exacerbations is that COPD exacerbations routinely receive antibiotic therapy because they frequently involve bacterial infection of lower airways (commonly H. influenzae), while asthma exacerbations are typically triggered by viral infections and do not routinely require antibiotics. Both conditions use oxygen, nebulized bronchodilators, and systemic corticosteroids for acute management. Katzung says management of the two kinds of exacerbation differs little except for routine antibiotics in COPD, not by need for hospitalization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — management of COPD and asthma exacerbations is similar except for routine antibiotics in COPD, because COPD exacerbations frequently involve bacterial infection of the lower airways; β-lactams, doxycycline or azithromycin cover the prevalent pathogen Haemophilus influenzae. COMLEX: Katzung — COPD exacerbations carry higher mortality than asthma exacerbations, partly from cardiovascular comorbidity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 3 — the key community-acquired organisms, including H. influenzae","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Katzung says management of the two kinds of exacerbation dif","D":"Both conditions use oxygen, ne"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q07_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with asthma has positive skin tests to perennial aeroallergens and elevated serum IgE levels (450 IU/mL). Despite high-dose ICS/LABA therapy, she continues having frequent exacerbations. Based on her pathophysiology, which targeted therapy is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mepolizumab targeting IL-5","B":"Roflumilast inhibiting PDE4","C":"Omalizumab targeting IgE","D":"Tiotropium blocking muscarinic receptors","E":"Zileuton inhibiting 5-lipoxygenase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient demonstrates classic IgE-mediated allergic asthma pathophysiology with documented allergen sensitivity and elevated IgE. Omalizumab (anti-IgE monoclonal antibody) is specifically indicated for patients with moderate-to-severe allergic asthma, positive allergen testing, and an IgE level within the range that injections can reduce sufficiently (Katzung Ch 20), who remain uncontrolled on ICS/LABA therapy. Mepolizumab targets IL-5 for the eosinophilic phenotype, roflumilast is approved for COPD but not asthma, tiotropium is a bronchodilator, and zileuton blocks leukotriene synthesis; none lowers IgE, the driver in this allergic, high-IgE patient.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Omalizumab binds the portion of IgE that attaches to its receptors, so it blocks IgE binding without activating IgE already bound (no mast-cell degranulation); it is restricted to moderate-to-severe asthma with perennial allergic sensitization, dosed by total IgE and body weight (Katzung). FA: used in allergic asthma with ↑ IgE resistant to ICS and LABA. COMLEX: Katzung — its main clinical effect is fewer and less severe exacerbations with lower corticosteroid requirement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the IgE–mast cell process behind an asthma attack","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q08_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with COPD continues to have exacerbations despite optimal bronchodilator therapy. Pulmonary function tests show FEV₁ 45% predicted, and complete blood count reveals low blood eosinophil levels. Based on his pathophysiology, what treatment consideration is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Add high-dose inhaled corticosteroids","B":"Initiate anti-IL-5 biologic therapy","C":"Start omalizumab for allergic component","D":"Optimize bronchodilators without adding ICS","E":"Add leukotriene receptor antagonist therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has severe COPD with low eosinophil counts, indicating neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic inflammation (T2-low phenotype). Low blood eosinophil levels predict poor ICS response and potentially increased pneumonia risk. Current guidelines recommend against routine ICS use in COPD patients with low eosinophils, favoring optimized bronchodilator therapy. Omalizumab is restricted to moderate to severe asthma with perennial allergic sensitization, which this patient does not have. Anti-IL-5 antibodies are approved as add-on therapy for severe eosinophilic asthma, and his eosinophils are low. Katzung's COPD options are long-acting bronchodilators, roflumilast, ICS for selected patients and azithromycin, with no role given to leukotriene antagonists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — COPD is commonly neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic and responds poorly even to high-dose ICS; ICS is linked to bacterial pneumonia, and low blood eosinophils predict a low likelihood of ICS benefit, so bronchodilators are optimized instead. COMLEX: Katzung — roflumilast reduces exacerbation frequency and is an option when exacerbations continue on bronchodilators.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — adverse effects and safety of the major drug classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Current guidelines recommend a","B":"Anti-IL-5 antibodies are approved as add-on therapy for seve","E":"Katzung's COPD options are long-acting bronchodilators, rofl"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q09_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with moderate persistent asthma demonstrates eosinophilic airway inflammation on induced sputum analysis. His symptoms occur daily with nocturnal awakenings three times weekly despite regular SABA use. What pharmacological approach best addresses the underlying inflammatory pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Increase SABA frequency to prevent symptoms","B":"Add LABA for sustained bronchodilation","C":"Add oral theophylline for bronchodilator effect","D":"Start inhaled corticosteroid controller therapy","E":"Start antimuscarinic agent to reduce mucus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has moderate persistent asthma with documented eosinophilic inflammation. The pathophysiology of eosinophilic asthma involves T2 lymphocyte-mediated inflammation with IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 production. Inhaled corticosteroids directly address this pathophysiology by inhibiting inflammatory cytokine production and are the most effective controller therapy for eosinophilic asthma. SABAs alone do not address underlying inflammation. A LABA has no anti-inflammatory action and should not be used without an ICS in asthma, and antimuscarinics have only a limited, mainly bronchodilator role in asthma. Katzung recommends an inhaled corticosteroid first at this symptom frequency; adding theophylline gives only a modest gain in control.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung — 'T2-high' asthma (more sputum and blood eosinophilia, positive skin tests, higher IgE) improved FEV1 with 8 weeks of ICS, whereas 'T2-low' asthma did not; ICS act mainly by inhibiting airway infiltration by lymphocytes, eosinophils and mast cells. COMLEX: FA — ICS inactivate NF-κB and are first-line controller therapy for chronic asthma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — environmental factors and eosinophil-rich asthma morphology","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"SABAs alone do not address und","C":"Katzung recommends an inhaled corticosteroid first at this s","E":"A LABA has no anti-inflammator"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is prescribed budesonide/formoterol combination therapy to be used both twice daily for maintenance and as needed for symptom relief (SMART therapy). What therapeutic rationale supports this flexible dosing strategy compared to traditional fixed-dose controller plus separate reliever approach?","choices":{"A":"Formoterol has a slower onset than albuterol for symptom relief","B":"One inhaler gives anti-inflammatory and bronchodilator action","C":"Budesonide prevents formoterol-induced tachyphylaxis","D":"Combination therapy eliminates need for rescue medication","E":"SMART therapy reduces total medication costs significantly"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SMART (single maintenance and reliever therapy) uses budesonide/formoterol for both scheduled maintenance and as-needed relief. The rationale is that each symptom-requiring dose provides both rapid bronchodilation (formoterol has rapid onset like albuterol) and additional anti-inflammatory effect (budesonide), potentially providing better inflammation control than fixed-dose ICS with separate SABA reliever. Katzung bases SMART on formoterol's rapid onset, letting one budesonide-formoterol inhaler act as controller and reliever, not on preventing tachyphylaxis. SMART does not eliminate the reliever, since formoterol serves as the reliever. Katzung's rationale for SMART is controller and rapid-onset reliever in one inhaler, not cost.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SMART (single maintenance and reliever therapy) — formoterol's rapid onset lets low-dose budesonide/formoterol serve as both controller and reliever; it is part of US guidelines since 2020 for scheduled and as-needed use (Katzung). COMLEX: Each rescue puff also delivers corticosteroid, so the anti-inflammatory dose rises when symptoms do.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — the pharmacologic classes, including ICS–LABA combinations","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Katzung bases SMART on formoterol's rapid onset, letting one","D":"SMART does not eliminate the reliever, since formoterol serv","E":"Katzung's rationale for SMART is controller and rapid-onset "}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with severe COPD continues having frequent exacerbations despite triple therapy with LABA/LAMA/ICS. His physician considers adding roflumilast. What therapeutic rationale supports adding a selective PDE4 inhibitor in this clinical context?","choices":{"A":"PDE4 inhibitors provide additional bronchodilation beyond triple therapy","B":"Roflumilast replaces the need for inhaled corticosteroids","C":"PDE4 inhibition reduces exacerbations through anti-inflammatory effects","D":"PDE4 inhibitors enhance mucociliary clearance of bacteria","E":"Selective PDE4 inhibition prevents LABA-induced tachycardia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Roflumilast is a selective phosphodiesterase-4 (PDE4) inhibitor that reduces inflammatory cell activity, particularly neutrophils, and decreases exacerbation frequency in severe COPD with chronic bronchitis. Unlike non-selective theophylline, selective PDE4 inhibition provides anti-inflammatory benefits with fewer cardiac and CNS effects. It's added when exacerbations persist despite optimal bronchodilator and ICS therapy. Katzung ties PDE4 inhibition to reduced cytokine release and PDE3 inhibition to airway relaxation, so roflumilast's role in COPD is fewer exacerbations rather than additional bronchodilation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Roflumilast is a selective PDE4 inhibitor (raises cAMP, lowers airway inflammation) that reduces the frequency of COPD exacerbations; it is approved for COPD but not for asthma, and Katzung credits it with a better therapeutic ratio than theophylline. COMLEX: Its place is the patient who keeps exacerbating on maximal inhaled therapy, and Katzung notes that PDE4 inhibitors were limited by nausea, headache, and diarrhea.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 3 — the narrow therapeutic index of theophylline, the nonselective PDE inhibitor that roflumilast improves on","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Katzung ties PDE4 inhibition to reduced cytokine release and"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the therapeutic rationale for key pharmacologic classes used in asthma and COPD management","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with asthma and aspirin sensitivity experiences severe bronchospasm after taking ibuprofen for headache. Her physician explains that her leukotriene levels are elevated. What therapeutic rationale supports preferential use of leukotriene modifiers in aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease?","choices":{"A":"Leukotriene modifiers prevent NSAID absorption","B":"Aspirin sensitivity indicates IgE-mediated allergy","C":"NSAID hypersensitivity contraindicates corticosteroids","D":"Leukotriene modifiers enhance aspirin metabolism","E":"Aspirin triggers leukotriene overproduction pathway"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (AERD) involves cyclooxygenase-1 inhibition by NSAIDs, shunting arachidonic acid metabolism toward the leukotriene pathway, causing cysteinyl leukotriene overproduction. This leads to severe bronchospasm and rhinosinusitis. Leukotriene modifiers (montelukast, zafirlukast, zileuton) are particularly effective in AERD by blocking this pathophysiologic mechanism. ICS remains important, but leukotriene modifiers provide additional targeted benefit. Katzung says AERD shows no evidence of allergic sensitization to aspirin; COX inhibition shifts arachidonic acid toward the leukotriene pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease combines asthma, nasal polyps, and reactions to COX-1–inhibiting NSAIDs, with leukotriene overproduction driving the bronchoconstriction; montelukast and zafirlukast block the CysLT1 receptor and zileuton blocks 5-lipoxygenase. COMLEX: FA singles out antileukotrienes as especially good for aspirin-induced asthma, so ask any asthmatic with nasal polyps about NSAID reactions before suggesting an NSAID.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — the asthma phenotypes, including the aspirin-sensitive form","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Katzung says AERD shows no evidence of allergic sensitizatio"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with asthma experiences daily symptoms requiring albuterol use and has nocturnal awakenings four times weekly. Her FEV₁ is 65% predicted. According to stepwise asthma management guidelines, what is the most appropriate treatment approach?","choices":{"A":"Low-dose ICS daily with as-needed SABA only","B":"Daily leukotriene modifier as monotherapy","C":"High-dose ICS monotherapy with as-needed albuterol","D":"Biologic therapy with omalizumab injections","E":"Low-dose ICS–formoterol maintenance and reliever"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has moderate persistent asthma (daily symptoms, frequent nocturnal awakenings and reduced FEV₁). At this step, guidelines prefer low-dose ICS–formoterol taken both as maintenance and as reliever (the SMART approach, now part of US guidelines); Katzung notes that low-dose budesonide–formoterol used this way is as effective as a four-times-higher dose of budesonide, and that adding a LABA is more effective than doubling the ICS dose (Ch 20). Low-dose ICS with SABA alone is the step for mild persistent asthma, a leukotriene modifier alone is a weaker alternative, high-dose ICS monotherapy is inferior to ICS plus LABA, and omalizumab is reserved for severe allergic asthma uncontrolled on high-dose therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rescue SABA use more than twice a week, night symptoms more than twice a month, or FEV1 below 80% predicted means as-needed albuterol alone is no longer enough; Katzung notes low-dose budesonide–formoterol taken as both controller and reliever (SMART) works as well as a four-times-higher budesonide dose. COMLEX: Formoterol's rapid onset is what lets one combination inhaler serve as the reliever as well as the controller.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC, and the FEV1/FVC ratio used to grade airway obstruction","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old former smoker with COPD reports dyspnea with minimal exertion and has had three exacerbations requiring antibiotics and prednisone in the past year. His FEV₁ is 42% predicted with blood eosinophils of 350 cells/μL. According to GOLD guidelines, which treatment regimen is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"LABA/LAMA/ICS triple therapy","B":"LAMA monotherapy","C":"LABA plus LAMA combination","D":"SABA as needed only","E":"LABA monotherapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has severe airflow limitation (FEV₁ 42% predicted), frequent exacerbations (three in the past year), and raised blood eosinophils, which suggest ICS responsiveness (Katzung Ch 20). GOLD guidelines recommend LABA/LAMA/ICS triple therapy for patients with severe airflow limitation and frequent exacerbations, especially with elevated eosinophils predicting ICS benefit. As-needed SABA alone is for acute relief and is far too little for persistent symptoms and repeated exacerbations, which call for regular long-acting bronchodilators. With repeated exacerbations Katzung says daily ICS is appropriate, which LAMA monotherapy lacks. LABA plus LAMA lacks the inhaled steroid Katzung advises for two or more exacerbations a year, with high eosinophils predicting likely benefit. LABA monotherapy lacks the inhaled steroid Katzung advises for patients with two or more exacerbations a year.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In COPD, blood eosinophil counts predict which patients will benefit from an inhaled corticosteroid; severe airflow obstruction plus frequent exacerbations plus high eosinophils is the combination that justifies LABA/LAMA/ICS. COMLEX: Weigh that benefit against the higher risk of bacterial pneumonia that Katzung links to ICS use in COPD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — the etiology, pathology, and clinical course of COPD, the exacerbation-prone disease treated here","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"With repeated exacerbations Katzung says daily ICS is approp","C":"LABA plus LAMA lacks the inhaled steroid Katzung advises for","E":"LABA monotherapy lacks the inhaled steroid Katzung advises f"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply the current pharmacological treatment guidelines for the management of asthma and COPD, highlighting both commonalities and differences","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with COPD asks why his physician prescribes him both a long-acting bronchodilator and encourages smoking cessation but doesn't emphasize inhaled steroids as strongly as his friend with asthma. What principle distinguishes COPD management priorities from asthma management?","choices":{"A":"ICS is contraindicated in all COPD patients due to infection risk","B":"COPD requires antibiotics as primary therapy; asthma requires steroids","C":"Bronchodilators work in COPD; only biologics work in asthma","D":"COPD centers on smoking cessation and bronchodilators, not ICS","E":"COPD management focuses on systemic steroids; asthma uses inhaled"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A key difference in management priorities is that COPD treatment emphasizes smoking cessation (slows disease progression) and bronchodilators (LABA/LAMA) as first-line therapy. Unlike asthma where ICS is the cornerstone controller therapy, ICS plays a less central role in COPD, reserved for patients with frequent exacerbations, severe obstruction, or elevated eosinophils, partially due to lower efficacy and pneumonia risk. Antibiotics are given routinely for COPD exacerbations, not as primary maintenance therapy, which rests on long-acting bronchodilators. Bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids are asthma mainstays; Katzung reserves biologics for moderate to severe asthma uncontrolled by ICS/LABA. COPD maintenance rests on long-acting bronchodilators, while Katzung reserves systemic steroids for urgent treatment because of their chronic toxicity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Katzung contrasts the two diseases — COPD inflammation is usually neutrophilic rather than eosinophilic, responds poorly even to high-dose ICS, and its obstruction is not fully reversible, whereas ICS is the cornerstone controller in asthma. COMLEX: Long-acting antimuscarinics (tiotropium, umeclidinium, aclidinium) are approved for COPD maintenance and reduce exacerbations, which is why bronchodilators anchor COPD care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinically differentiating the obstructive lung diseases, the distinction that drives the two treatment plans","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Antibiotics are given routinely for COPD exacerbations, not ","C":"Bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids are asthma mains","E":"COPD maintenance rests on long-acting bronchodilators, while"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with asthma reports using his albuterol inhaler 4-5 times daily for the past month. Despite this frequent SABA use, he continues having symptoms and his peak flow measurements show progressive decline. What pathophysiologic principle explains why increasing SABA frequency without adding controller therapy is inadequate?","choices":{"A":"β2-receptors undergo downregulation with frequent agonist exposure","B":"SABAs provide bronchodilation but do not address airway inflammation","C":"Albuterol loses efficacy after one month of regular use","D":"Frequent SABA use causes paradoxical bronchoconstriction","E":"SABAs induce corticosteroid resistance in airway cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pathophysiologic principle is that SABAs provide symptomatic bronchodilation but have no anti-inflammatory effect on the underlying eosinophilic inflammation driving asthma. Frequent SABA use (>2 times/week) indicates inadequate disease control requiring controller therapy (ICS) to address inflammation. Relying on bronchodilators alone allows inflammation to progress, increasing exacerbation risk despite temporary symptom relief. Albuterol does not simply stop working after a month; regular use causes some tachyphylaxis at low doses, but maximal bronchodilation is still reached within usual doses. Katzung notes some tachyphylaxis with regular beta-agonist use, yet maximal bronchodilation is preserved; the gap is that relievers leave untreated the inflammation that ICS controllers address.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rescue SABA use more than twice a week signals the need for a controller — a low-dose ICS first, with a leukotriene antagonist as the alternative — because bronchodilators relax airway smooth muscle but leave the airway inflammation untreated. COMLEX: FA lists inhaled glucocorticoids as first-line therapy for chronic asthma; rising rescue use with falling peak flows calls for stepping up, not more albuterol.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 9 — the mast cell–driven immunologic process of an asthma attack","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Katzung notes some tachyphylaxis with regular beta-agonist u"}},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with 40 pack-year smoking history and COPD continues smoking despite counseling. His physician explains that smoking cessation is the only intervention proven to slow FEV₁ decline in COPD. What pathophysiologic mechanism explains why pharmacologic therapy cannot substitute for smoking cessation in preventing disease progression?","choices":{"A":"Bronchodilators enhance cigarette smoke absorption","B":"ICS therapy accelerates emphysema progression in smokers","C":"Nicotine directly antagonizes LABA and LAMA mechanisms","D":"Smoke keeps driving inflammation and alveolar destruction","E":"Inhaled medications are completely inactivated by smoke"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pathophysiologic principle is that ongoing cigarette smoke exposure causes continuous oxidative stress, neutrophilic inflammation, protease activation, and progressive alveolar destruction (emphysema). While medications improve symptoms and reduce exacerbations, they cannot prevent the structural lung damage from ongoing smoke exposure. Smoking cessation is the only intervention that slows the accelerated FEV₁ decline characteristic of COPD, addressing the root cause rather than just managing symptoms. Katzung describes no antagonism of LABA or LAMA action by nicotine; the limit is that bronchodilators relieve airflow without stopping the smoke-driven inflammation, which keeps progressing while exposure continues.","boardPrep":"Step 1: COPD airflow limitation reflects an abnormal inflammatory response to cigarette smoke that progresses as long as exposure continues; FA ties the alveolar destruction to oxidative stress, CD8+ T cells, neutrophils, macrophages, and a protease–antiprotease imbalance that destroys elastic fibers. COMLEX: Drugs relieve symptoms and cut exacerbations, but Katzung reports mixed data on whether even LAMAs slow lung-function decline — removing the smoke is the disease-modifying step.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — centriacinar emphysema, the smoking-driven alveolar destruction that continues with ongoing exposure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Integrate knowledge of the pathophysiology (pathology) to select and justify appropriate pharmacological treatment regimens for patients with asthma and COPD","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with asthma presents with an acute exacerbation triggered by a viral upper respiratory infection. Her symptoms are not improving with frequent albuterol use. Based on understanding viral-triggered asthma pathophysiology, what treatment addition is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Systemic corticosteroids to reduce inflammation","B":"LABA to provide longer bronchodilation","C":"Antihistamines to reduce viral inflammation","D":"Antibiotics to treat the viral infection","E":"Leukotriene modifiers to prevent viral replication"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Viral respiratory infections trigger asthma exacerbations by inducing airway inflammation, increasing bronchial hyperreactivity, and causing epithelial damage that enhances allergen exposure. The pathophysiology involves amplified inflammatory response with increased eosinophils, cytokines, and mucus production. Systemic corticosteroids (prednisone or methylprednisolone) directly address this inflammatory cascade, reduce airway obstruction, and prevent hospitalization. Antibiotics are ineffective for viral infections, and bronchodilators alone cannot resolve the inflammatory component. Robbins notes potent histamine antagonists lack efficacy in asthma, whereas Katzung treats severe attacks with systemic prednisone or methylprednisolone. Leukotriene modifiers block leukotriene synthesis or the LTD4 receptor; they do not act on viral replication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asthma exacerbations are most often associated with viral respiratory infections and combine intense smooth-muscle contraction with mucus plugging and mucosal edema; an exacerbation not responding to bronchodilators needs systemic corticosteroids (Katzung: oral prednisone 30–60 mg/day or IV methylprednisolone 0.5–1 mg/kg every 6–12 hours). COMLEX: Katzung notes systemic steroids can usually be stopped in 5–10 days once the obstruction improves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 7 — the environmental triggers of asthma and the morphology of status asthmaticus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Antibiotics are ineffective fo","C":"Robbins notes potent histamine antagonists lack efficacy in ","E":"Leukotriene modifiers block leukotriene synthesis or the LTD"}}],"25":[{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old male former smoker presents with progressive dyspnea and nonproductive cough over 18 months. High-resolution CT shows reticular opacities with honeycombing predominantly in the lower lobes and subpleural regions. Lung biopsy reveals patchy interstitial fibrosis with areas of dense fibrosis adjacent to relatively normal lung. What pathologic feature is the hallmark of this patient's disease?","choices":{"A":"Temporal homogeneity of fibrotic lesions","B":"Noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics","C":"Temporal heterogeneity with fibroblastic foci","D":"Masson bodies in alveolar spaces","E":"Accumulation of pigmented macrophages in airspaces"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) with the usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern. The hallmark features are spatial heterogeneity (normal lung adjacent to fibrotic areas) and temporal heterogeneity (coexistence of early fibroblastic foci and late dense fibrosis). This contrasts with NSIP, which shows temporal homogeneity. Noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics point to sarcoidosis, Masson bodies in airspaces to organizing pneumonia, and airspaces packed with pigmented macrophages to desquamative interstitial pneumonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UIP, the histologic pattern of IPF, is patchy fibrosis with spatial heterogeneity (normal lung beside scar) and temporal heterogeneity (early fibroblastic foci beside dense old collagen), worst in the lower lobes, subpleural regions, and interlobular septa, ending in honeycomb lung. COMLEX: An older patient with insidious exertional dyspnea, dry cough, and velcro-like crackles fits IPF; Robbins gives a median survival of about 3.8 years.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — the restrictive pattern on the maximum expiratory flow–volume curve","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with IPF undergoes genetic testing revealing mutations in TERT and TERC genes. What pathogenic mechanism do these mutations affect in the development of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Telomere maintenance, causing cell senescence","B":"Surfactant protein folding in type II pneumocytes","C":"Mucin secretion altering mucociliary clearance","D":"Collagen synthesis by activated fibroblasts","E":"TGF-β production by injured epithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"TERT and TERC genes encode components essential for telomere maintenance. Mutations in these genes (along with PARN and RTEL1) are associated with increased risk of IPF (Robbins Ch 15). Defective telomere maintenance leads to cellular senescence, making alveolar epithelial cells more susceptible to environmental injury. Robbins does not tie TERT and TERC to surfactant folding, mucin secretion, or collagen synthesis; mucin secretion is the effect of the separate MUC5B promoter variant, and collagen deposition is a downstream step of fibrosis driven by cytokines such as TGF-β. TGF-β is a profibrotic factor released by injured epithelium after the injury, whereas TERT and TERC are telomere-maintenance genes whose defects cause senescence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TERT, TERC, PARN, and RTEL1 mutations impair telomere maintenance and push alveolar epithelium into senescence; up to 15% of familial IPF carries inherited telomere-gene defects and up to 25% of sporadic IPF shows abnormal telomere shortening. COMLEX: IPF rarely appears before age 50, and hypoxemia, cyanosis, and clubbing arrive late in its insidious course.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — the alveolar epithelial cells whose recurrent injury and senescence start IPF","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"TGF-β is a profibrotic factor released by injured epithelium"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old African American woman presents with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest X-ray during routine evaluation. Mediastinal lymph node biopsy reveals well-formed noncaseating granulomas composed of epithelioid macrophages. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid shows a CD4:CD8 ratio of 10:1. What cytokine is released in particularly large amounts and serves as a marker of disease activity?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin-5","B":"Interleukin-13","C":"Interferon-gamma","D":"Tumor necrosis factor","E":"Interleukin-4"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has sarcoidosis. Activated alveolar macrophages release tumor necrosis factor (TNF) in particularly large amounts, and TNF concentration in bronchoalveolar fluid serves as a marker of sarcoidosis disease activity. The elevated CD4:CD8 ratio (5:1 to 15:1) reflects oligoclonal T-cell expansion in response to an unidentified antigen. IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 cytokines of allergic asthma, and IFN-γ, though raised in sarcoidosis as a Th1 cytokine that activates macrophages, is not the macrophage product Robbins names as the activity marker.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sarcoidosis is a CD4+ Th1 disease — BAL CD4:CD8 of 5:1 to 15:1 with IL-2 and IFN-γ — and activated alveolar macrophages release TNF in particularly large amounts, so BAL TNF tracks disease activity. COMLEX: Systemic clues include anergy to PPD and Candida skin tests and polyclonal hypergammaglobulinemia (Robbins); FA adds raised serum ACE and hypercalcemia from macrophage 1α-hydroxylase.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — TB granuloma biology and the TST, the infectious mimic that must be excluded before diagnosing sarcoidosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 ","C":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 ","E":"IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13 are Th2 "}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q04_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old dairy farmer presents with episodic dyspnea, fever, and cough occurring 4-6 hours after working in the barn with moldy hay. Lung biopsy shows interstitial pneumonitis with lymphocytes and plasma cells, along with poorly formed nonnecrotizing granulomas. What is the primary immunologic mechanism underlying this patient's condition?","choices":{"A":"IgE-mediated type I hypersensitivity","B":"Type III and IV hypersensitivity to inhaled organic antigens","C":"Autoantibody formation against alveolar basement membrane","D":"Deficiency of alpha-1 antitrypsin","E":"Interleukin-5 mediated eosinophilic inflammation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has hypersensitivity pneumonitis (farmer's lung) caused by thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay. The pathogenesis involves both type III hypersensitivity (immune complex deposition, evidenced by serum antibodies and complement in vessel walls) and type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity (granuloma formation, T-cell infiltration). The episodic timing (4-6 hours post-exposure) is characteristic. α1-Antitrypsin deficiency causes panacinar emphysema, not a granulomatous pneumonitis after organic dust exposure. Unlike asthma, hypersensitivity pneumonitis injures the alveolar walls, and its evidence points to immune complexes and T cell–mediated (type IV) granulomas, not an IgE-mediated reaction. Antibodies against alveolar and glomerular basement membranes define Goodpasture syndrome (hemoptysis with hematuria), not a pneumonitis after organic-dust exposure. IL-5–recruited eosinophils define the pulmonary eosinophilias, whereas the infiltrate of hypersensitivity pneumonitis is lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages with rare eosinophils.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis is a mixed type III (serum antibodies; complement and immunoglobulin in vessel walls) and type IV (nonnecrotizing granulomas in about two-thirds) reaction to inhaled organic antigens such as thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay; unlike asthma, it targets the alveolar walls. COMLEX: Attacks of fever, dyspnea, and cough begin 4–6 hours after exposure and recur with re-exposure, so the occupational and hobby history is the key diagnostic step.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 6 — the exaggerated Th2, type I reaction of asthma, the contrasting hypersensitivity pattern","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Unlike asthma, hypersensitivity pneumonitis injures the alve","C":"Antibodies against alveolar and glomerular basement membrane","E":"IL-5–recruited eosinophils define the pulmonary eosinophilia"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q05_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old pigeon breeder develops progressive dyspnea over several years despite reducing bird contact. Lung biopsy shows interstitial fibrosis with fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis. What phase of hypersensitivity pneumonitis does this morphology represent?","choices":{"A":"Acute phase with alveolar damage","B":"Subacute phase with granulomas","C":"Resolution phase with normal architecture","D":"Organizing phase with Masson bodies","E":"Chronic phase with irreversible fibrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This represents chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis with progression to irreversible interstitial fibrosis. The chronic phase shows fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis. Some chronic cases progress to a UIP-like pattern that lacks characteristic features and may be irreversible despite antigen removal. This contrasts with acute/subacute phases, which may be reversible with antigen avoidance. Fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis are Robbins' chronic-phase changes, not the picture of a lung with normal architecture. Masson bodies are polypoid airspace plugs of organizing pneumonia over normal lung architecture, not the fibroblastic foci and honeycombing seen here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Continuous, protracted antigen exposure turns episodic hypersensitivity pneumonitis into a chronic form with interstitial fibrosis, fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchiolitis alongside granulomas. COMLEX: FA notes acute HP is often self-limiting once the stimulus is removed but chronic HP leaves irreversible fibrosis, so early antigen removal is the intervention that matters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — how fibrosis and other disease states impair gas diffusion across the respiratory membrane","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Fibroblastic foci, honeycombing, and obliterative bronchioli","D":"Masson bodies are polypoid airspace plugs of organizing pneu"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q06_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with progressive dyspnea undergoes lung biopsy showing diffuse interstitial fibrosis with lesions of roughly the same age and appearance. No fibroblastic foci, honeycomb changes, or granulomas are identified. What key morphologic feature distinguishes this pattern from usual interstitial pneumonia?","choices":{"A":"Presence of Masson bodies in alveolar ducts","B":"Accumulation of smokers' macrophages","C":"Noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics","D":"Temporal homogeneity of fibrotic lesions","E":"Eosinophilic infiltration of alveolar walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) with the fibrosing pattern. The key distinguishing feature from UIP is temporal homogeneity—all fibrotic lesions appear roughly the same age. NSIP lacks the fibroblastic foci and honeycomb changes characteristic of UIP. NSIP has a better prognosis than UIP and may respond to immunosuppressive therapy. Masson bodies define organizing pneumonia, smokers' macrophages define desquamative interstitial pneumonia, granulomas along lymphatics define sarcoidosis, and eosinophil-rich infiltrates define the pulmonary eosinophilias; none of these is what separates NSIP from UIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The fibrosing pattern of NSIP shows lesions of roughly the same age (temporal homogeneity) with no honeycombing, hyaline membranes, or granulomas; NSIP carries a much better prognosis than UIP. COMLEX: Typical patients are female nonsmokers in their sixth decade with bilateral, symmetric, lower-lobe reticular opacities, and unlike UIP the disease may respond to immunosuppressive drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 4 — FEV1, FVC, and the preserved FEV1/FVC ratio of restrictive disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q07_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man develops cough and dyspnea following viral pneumonia. Chest CT shows patchy areas of airspace consolidation. Lung biopsy reveals polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue within alveolar ducts and alveoli, all of the same age. The underlying lung architecture is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","B":"Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","C":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","D":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","E":"Sarcoidosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient has cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (COP), characterized by Masson bodies (polypoid plugs of organizing connective tissue) of uniform age in alveolar ducts, alveoli, and bronchioles. The underlying architecture is normal with no interstitial fibrosis or honeycomb change. COP can follow infections or be idiopathic. Most patients require oral steroids for 6+ months but have good long-term prognosis. Sarcoidosis forms noncaseating granulomas along lymphatics, not airspace plugs of organizing connective tissue. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis shows bronchiolocentric interstitial pneumonitis of lymphocytes and plasma cells with nonnecrotizing granulomas, not Masson-body plugs over normal lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Masson bodies — polypoid plugs of loose organizing connective tissue in alveolar ducts, alveoli, and often bronchioles — define organizing pneumonia, which is called cryptogenic only when no cause (infection, inhaled toxins, drugs, connective tissue disease, GVHD) is found. COMLEX: Patients have cough, dyspnea, and patchy subpleural or peribronchial consolidation; some recover spontaneously, but most need oral steroids for 6 months or longer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — the injury pattern of viral pneumonia, one of the infections that organizing pneumonia can follow","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The underlying architecture is","D":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis shows bronchiolocentric interst"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q08_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old male current smoker presents with insidious dyspnea and dry cough with clubbing. Lung biopsy shows abundant macrophages with dusty brown pigment filling airspaces, with only mild thickening of alveolar septa and minimal interstitial fibrosis. What distinguishes this entity from other interstitial lung diseases?","choices":{"A":"Presence of noncaseating granulomas","B":"Macrophages filling airspaces, with little fibrosis","C":"Fibroblastic foci with temporal heterogeneity","D":"Organizing connective tissue plugs in alveoli","E":"Eosinophilic infiltration with Charcot-Leyden crystals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP), characterized by large collections of pigmented macrophages (smokers' macrophages) filling the airspaces with minimal interstitial fibrosis. Despite its name suggesting \"desquamated pneumocytes,\" the cells are actually macrophages. DIP occurs in smokers, has excellent response to steroids and smoking cessation, and has much better prognosis than UIP. Noncaseating granulomas point to sarcoidosis or hypersensitivity pneumonitis, fibroblastic foci with temporal heterogeneity to UIP, organizing plugs to organizing pneumonia, and eosinophils with Charcot-Leyden crystals to asthma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DIP fills the airspaces with macrophages holding dusty brown pigment (smokers' macrophages), once mistaken for desquamated pneumocytes — hence the misnomer — with only mild septal thickening and fibrosis; virtually all patients smoke. COMLEX: It presents in the fourth or fifth decade with insidious dyspnea, dry cough, and clubbing, and typically responds well to steroids and smoking cessation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, another smoking-related interstitial disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q09_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"Two women with connective tissue disease have biopsy-proven nonspecific interstitial pneumonia. The first biopsy shows mild to moderate interstitial inflammation of lymphocytes and a few plasma cells; the second shows diffuse interstitial fibrosis of uniform age. According to Robbins, how does the first patient's pattern compare with the second's?","choices":{"A":"It carries the worse prognosis of the two patterns","B":"It shows fibroblastic foci and honeycombing","C":"It is the pattern of older patients with UIP","D":"It does not respond to immunosuppressive drugs","E":"Its patients are younger, with a better prognosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins divides nonspecific interstitial pneumonia into a cellular pattern, mild to moderate chronic interstitial inflammation of lymphocytes and a few plasma cells, and a fibrosing pattern of diffuse or patchy fibrosis of roughly the same age (temporal homogeneity). Patients with the cellular pattern are somewhat younger than those with the fibrosing pattern and have a better prognosis, and NSIP as a whole has a much better prognosis than UIP. A worse prognosis belongs to the fibrosing pattern, and worse still to UIP. Fibroblastic foci and honeycombing are absent in both NSIP patterns and mark UIP. Unlike UIP, NSIP may benefit from immunosuppressive drugs, suggesting an immune basis. UIP is patchy, temporally heterogeneous fibrosis with fibroblastic foci and honeycombing in older patients, unlike the cellular NSIP pattern of interstitial inflammation in younger patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins splits NSIP into a cellular pattern (lymphocytes and a few plasma cells) and a fibrosing pattern (uniform-age fibrosis); cellular-pattern patients are somewhat younger and have the better prognosis, and NSIP as a whole does far better than UIP. COMLEX: Immunosuppressive drugs may help NSIP, which suggests an immune basis, so the biopsy pattern shapes both prognosis and treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 9 — diffusing capacity, the PFT measure that falls as interstitial disease thickens the alveolar walls","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"UIP is patchy, temporally heterogeneous fibrosis with fibrob"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 34-year-old African American woman presents with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on routine chest X-ray. Lymph node biopsy reveals noncaseating granulomas containing laminated calcium concretions and star-shaped inclusions within multinucleated giant cells. What are these pathologic inclusions called?","choices":{"A":"Charcot-Leyden crystals and Curschmann spirals","B":"Schaumann bodies and asteroid bodies","C":"Ferruginous bodies and asbestos fibers","D":"Mallory bodies and Councilman bodies","E":"Russell bodies and Dutcher bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Schaumann bodies (laminated calcifications) and asteroid bodies (star-shaped inclusions within giant cells) are characteristic but not diagnostic findings in sarcoidosis granulomas. These inclusions are found within multinucleated giant cells in noncaseating granulomas. While not pathognomonic, they support the diagnosis of sarcoidosis in the appropriate clinical context. Charcot-Leyden crystals and Curschmann spirals are found in the sputum of patients with asthma, not in sarcoid granulomas. Ferruginous (asbestos) bodies are golden-brown dumbbell-shaped rods of asbestosis, not inclusions within sarcoid giant cells. Mallory bodies (damaged keratin filaments in alcoholic hepatitis) and Councilman bodies (apoptotic globules) are liver biopsy findings, not sarcoid granuloma inclusions. Russell (cytoplasmic) and Dutcher (nuclear) bodies are immunoglobulin inclusions in plasma cells, not inclusions within granuloma giant cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sarcoid granulomas are well formed and nonnecrotizing; Schaumann bodies (laminated calcium–protein concretions) and asteroid bodies (stellate inclusions) sit within giant cells in about 60% of granulomas but also occur in TB, so they are characteristic, not pathognomonic. COMLEX: Hilar node or lung involvement occurs in 90% of cases, and granulomas in the bronchial submucosa give bronchoscopic biopsy its high diagnostic yield.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — GPA's necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis, a contrast to sarcoid's nonnecrotizing granulomas","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Ferruginous (asbestos) bodies are golden-brown dumbbell-shap","D":"Mallory bodies (damaged keratin filaments in alcoholic hepat","E":"Russell (cytoplasmic) and Dutcher (nuclear) bodies are immun"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with IPF has a family history of pulmonary fibrosis affecting his father and sister. Genetic testing reveals a MUC5B promoter polymorphism. What role does this genetic variant play in the pathogenesis of idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Defective telomere maintenance leading to cellular senescence","B":"Impaired surfactant protein folding in type II pneumocytes","C":"Enhanced collagen synthesis by activated fibroblasts","D":"Increased TGF-β signaling in epithelial cells","E":"Altered mucin production affecting mucociliary clearance"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The MUC5B promoter polymorphism (rs35705950) is the strongest genetic risk factor for IPF, associated with roughly one-third of IPF cases (Robbins Ch 15). This variant increases MUC5B mucin production, and the pathogenic mechanism appears to involve altered mucociliary clearance and impaired host defense, leading to recurrent epithelial injury. However, the exact mechanism by which increased mucin leads to fibrosis remains under investigation. Collagen production by fibroblasts is the downstream fibrotic step driven by cytokines such as TGF-β, not what the MUC5B variant changes. Defective telomere maintenance comes from TERT, TERC, PARN, and RTEL1 mutations, not from the MUC5B promoter variant. Surfactant protein folding defects come from rare familial mutations in surfactant genes, not from the MUC5B promoter variant, which increases mucin secretion. TGF-β is released by injured epithelium or immune cells after injury, whereas the MUC5B promoter variant increases MUC5B mucin secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A MUC5B promoter polymorphism, present in roughly one-third of IPF cases, greatly increases MUC5B mucin secretion and may alter mucociliary clearance; exactly how this raises IPF risk is still uncertain. COMLEX: Inherited risk still interacts with exposures — cigarette smoking raises IPF risk several-fold, and air pollution, microaspiration, metal fumes, and wood dust are also implicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the ciliated and goblet cells of the mucociliary apparatus that MUC5B mucin feeds","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Defective telomere maintenance comes from TERT, TERC, PARN, ","B":"Surfactant protein folding defects come from rare familial m","D":"TGF-β is released by injured epithelium or immune cells afte"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major restrictive lung diseases","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old man presents with bilateral hilar adenopathy, erythema nodosum, and arthralgias. Chest imaging shows symmetric hilar and right paratracheal lymphadenopathy without parenchymal infiltrates. Which statement best describes the expected course of this patient's disease?","choices":{"A":"Recovery with minimal or no residual disease, as in most patients","B":"Permanent loss of lung function in most patients despite steroids","C":"Inexorable progression to pulmonary fibrosis in most patients","D":"Death from pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale in most patients","E":"Remission occurs only during continuous corticosteroid therapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This patient presents with Löfgren syndrome (bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, arthralgias; Bootcamp), an acute presentation of sarcoidosis. Robbins (Ch 15) describes an unpredictable course, but overall 65–70% of patients recover with minimal or no residual manifestations. Twenty percent have permanent loss of some lung function or some permanent visual impairment, so permanent lung damage is not the usual outcome. Of the remaining 10–15%, some die of cardiac or central nervous system damage, but most succumb to progressive pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale, so progression to fibrosis and death are minority outcomes. Remissions, sometimes permanent, may be spontaneous or induced by steroid therapy, so remission does not depend on continuous corticosteroids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löfgren syndrome is fever with bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, erythema nodosum, and migratory polyarthritis (Bootcamp), an acute presentation of sarcoidosis; erythema nodosum is a septal panniculitis producing painful red nodules on the shins (Robbins). Robbins outcome figures: 65–70% recover with minimal or no residual disease, 20% keep some permanent loss of lung function or vision, and most of the remaining 10–15% who die succumb to pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale. COMLEX: Robbins notes sarcoidosis may remit spontaneously or with steroids, and FA reserves glucocorticoids for symptomatic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 9 — lymphomas among the miscellaneous lung tumors, a differential for bilateral hilar adenopathy","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old male smoker with insidious dyspnea undergoes lung biopsy showing accumulation of pigmented macrophages in airspaces with minimal interstitial fibrosis. High-resolution CT demonstrates ground-glass opacities in lower lobes. What feature best distinguishes this entity from respiratory bronchiolitis-associated interstitial lung disease (RB-ILD)?","choices":{"A":"RB-ILD shows Masson bodies; DIP shows noncaseating granulomas","B":"RB-ILD centers on bronchioles; DIP fills alveoli diffusely","C":"RB-ILD responds to steroids; DIP requires smoking cessation alone","D":"RB-ILD has better prognosis; DIP progresses to honeycomb fibrosis","E":"RB-ILD shows temporal heterogeneity; DIP shows fibroblastic foci"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP): pigmented macrophages filling the airspaces with minimal fibrosis. DIP and RB-ILD are both smoking-related and share pigmented macrophages, but they differ in distribution: RB-ILD is patchy and bronchiolocentric, with macrophages in respiratory bronchioles, while DIP involves the alveolar spaces diffusely (Robbins Ch 15). Prognosis does not separate them — DIP typically has an excellent response to steroid therapy and smoking cessation and only occasionally progresses to fibrosis. Masson bodies belong to organizing pneumonia and granulomas to sarcoidosis or hypersensitivity pneumonitis; temporal heterogeneity and fibroblastic foci define UIP; and both entities improve with smoking cessation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RB-ILD is patchy and bronchiolocentric — pigmented macrophages within first- and second-order respiratory bronchioles, with mild peribronchiolar fibrosis — whereas DIP fills the alveolar spaces diffusely; both occur in smokers and can coexist in different parts of the same lung. COMLEX: RB-ILD usually affects current smokers with more than 30 pack-years in the fourth or fifth decade, and stopping smoking usually brings improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — centriacinar emphysema, the smoking lesion that also centers on the respiratory bronchiole","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with scleroderma develops progressive dyspnea. Lung biopsy shows interstitial fibrosis with temporal uniformity, lacking fibroblastic foci or honeycomb changes. High-resolution CT reveals bilateral ground-glass opacities and reticular changes. What pattern of interstitial pneumonia is most commonly associated with systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)","B":"Organizing pneumonia (OP)","C":"Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP)","D":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia (DIP)","E":"Lymphocytic interstitial pneumonia (LIP)"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSIP is the most common pattern of interstitial lung disease in systemic sclerosis (scleroderma), particularly the fibrosing subtype. NSIP shows temporal homogeneity (uniform age of lesions), lacks the fibroblastic foci characteristic of UIP, and has better prognosis. While UIP can also occur in connective tissue diseases, NSIP is more prevalent in scleroderma and has better response to immunosuppressive therapy. Organizing pneumonia fills airspaces with Masson bodies over normal lung architecture, without the interstitial fibrosis described here. Desquamative interstitial pneumonia fills the airspaces of smokers with pigmented macrophages and has only mild fibrosis, not uniform interstitial fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Systemic sclerosis causes diffuse interstitial fibrosis in which the NSIP pattern is more common than UIP; across autoimmune diseases, NSIP, UIP, organizing pneumonia, and bronchiolitis are the usual lung patterns. COMLEX: Systemic sclerosis can also involve the pulmonary vasculature and cause group 1 pulmonary hypertension, so a scleroderma patient with dyspnea needs evaluation for both.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension, where systemic sclerosis is the leading autoimmune cause in WHO group 1","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"NSIP shows temporal homogeneit","D":"Desquamative interstitial pneumonia fills the airspaces of s"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the key distinguishing characteristics and pathologic findings","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man develops dyspnea following viral pneumonia. Lung biopsy reveals polypoid plugs of organizing connective tissue (Masson bodies) filling alveolar ducts and alveoli without architectural distortion. What key feature distinguishes cryptogenic organizing pneumonia from organizing diffuse alveolar damage?","choices":{"A":"COP: same-age plugs, intact lung; DAD: prior hyaline membranes","B":"COP destroys lung architecture; organizing DAD leaves it intact","C":"COP is irreversible; organizing DAD resolves without any treatment","D":"COP affects distal airways; organizing DAD affects proximal bronchi","E":"COP shows eosinophilic infiltration; organizing DAD shows neutrophils"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In cryptogenic organizing pneumonia the polypoid plugs of loose connective tissue (Masson bodies) are all the same age and the underlying lung architecture is normal, with no interstitial fibrosis or honeycombing (Robbins Ch 15). Organizing diffuse alveolar damage is the organizing phase of an acute lung injury whose exudative stage lined the alveoli with hyaline membranes. COP preserves rather than destroys the architecture, it usually responds well to steroids rather than being irreversible, it involves the distal airspaces, and it is not defined by eosinophils.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Organizing pneumonia's plugs are all of one age over preserved architecture; diffuse alveolar damage passes through an exudative stage with hyaline membranes before its organizing stage, in which type II pneumocytes proliferate and granulation tissue forms in alveolar walls and spaces. COMLEX: Some patients with an organizing-pneumonia pattern present with a picture resembling acute ARDS, so the biopsy pattern, not the clinical picture alone, separates the two.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — the hyaline membranes of the acute exudative stage of ARDS","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"COP preserves rather than dest","E":"COP preserves rather than dest"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze clinical scenarios involving restrictive lung disease and apply knowledge of pathology to predict patient presentation and potential outcomes","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with longstanding sarcoidosis develops progressive dyspnea despite corticosteroid therapy. Imaging shows upper lobe predominant fibrosis with traction bronchiectasis and honeycombing. Pulmonary function tests reveal severe restriction with reduced DLCO. What complication has this patient developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute exacerbation with superimposed infection","B":"Transformation to lymphoma","C":"Cor pulmonale from pulmonary hypertension","D":"Stage 4 sarcoidosis with end-stage fibrosis","E":"Hypersensitivity pneumonitis from medication"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has progressed to Stage 4 sarcoidosis with end-stage pulmonary fibrosis. Stage 4 shows pulmonary fibrosis with architectural distortion, honeycombing, and typically upper lobe predominance (unlike IPF which is lower lobe predominant). A 2021 review gives Scadding's chest-radiograph staging, in which \"stage IV consists of upper-lobe predominant fibrotic changes with volume loss,\" and notes that radiographic resolution can occur with any stage except stage IV (Bernardinello et al., Diagnostics 2021). It is a minority outcome with a poor prognosis: Robbins notes that 65–70% of patients recover with little or no residual disease, while most of the 10–15% who die succumb to progressive pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale. These patients may develop complications including pulmonary hypertension, cor pulmonale, and respiratory failure, and may require lung transplantation. The imaging shows chronic upper-lobe fibrosis and honeycombing that has built up over time, which an acute infection or a drug pneumonitis would not produce, and lymphoma is not among the sarcoidosis outcomes Robbins lists.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' outcome figures for sarcoidosis: 65–70% recover with minimal or no residual disease, 20% keep some permanent loss of lung function or vision, and of the remaining 10–15% most die of progressive pulmonary fibrosis and cor pulmonale. COMLEX: FA lists glucocorticoids for symptomatic sarcoidosis, but established honeycomb fibrosis does not reverse, and cor pulmonale is the cardiopulmonary endpoint to watch for.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — pulmonary hypertension from interstitial lung disease (WHO group 3), the route to cor pulmonale","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"The imaging shows chronic uppe"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze clinical scenarios involving restrictive lung disease and apply knowledge of pathology to predict patient presentation and potential outcomes","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old bird breeder with chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis undergoes lung biopsy showing bridging fibrosis, fibroblastic foci, and honeycomb changes indistinguishable from UIP. Despite complete avoidance of antigen exposure for 6 months, his dyspnea continues to worsen. What explains the progression despite antigen avoidance?","choices":{"A":"Ongoing occult antigen exposure from contaminated home environment","B":"Chronic phase has progressed to irreversible UIP-like fibrosis","C":"Development of superimposed bacterial pneumonia","D":"Malignant transformation to pulmonary lymphoma","E":"Acute exacerbation triggered by viral infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis can progress to a UIP-like pattern with irreversible fibrosis that continues despite antigen avoidance. In this chronic fibrotic phase, the original diagnostic features (granulomas, bronchiolocentric inflammation) may be absent, making it difficult to distinguish from IPF. This represents \"burnt-out\" HP where fibroproliferative mechanisms become self-sustaining and antigen-independent, resulting in progressive respiratory decline despite removing the inciting agent. Lymphoma is not among the outcomes Robbins lists for hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and a viral acute exacerbation would cause a sudden worsening, not steady progression with UIP-like scarring.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Some chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis progresses to a UIP-type fibrosis that lacks the characteristic HP features, and Robbins lists chronic HP with connective tissue disease and asbestosis as UIP look-alikes that must be separated from IPF. COMLEX: Robbins warns that pet birds and moldy basements are easily missed unless asked about specifically — early antigen removal is what prevents progression to fibrosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 4 — asbestosis, another exposure-driven fibrosis whose pattern resembles UIP","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Lymphoma is not among the outc"}},{"id":"sdl25_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze clinical scenarios involving restrictive lung disease and apply knowledge of pathology to predict patient presentation and potential outcomes","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with IPF and baseline FVC of 65% predicted develops acute worsening of dyspnea over 1 week without evidence of infection, pulmonary embolism, or heart failure. High-resolution CT shows new bilateral ground-glass opacities superimposed on reticular changes. What is the most likely diagnosis and expected outcome?","choices":{"A":"Superimposed bacterial pneumonia requiring antibiotics","B":"Pulmonary embolism requiring anticoagulation","C":"Acute exacerbation of IPF with high mortality risk","D":"Heart failure exacerbation requiring diuresis","E":"Drug-induced pneumonitis requiring medication cessation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute exacerbation of IPF is characterized by acute worsening of dyspnea, new bilateral ground-glass opacities on imaging, and absence of alternative causes (infection, aspiration, PE, heart failure). Pathologically, acute/organizing diffuse alveolar damage is superimposed on chronic UIP changes. Treatment is supportive with high-dose corticosteroids, though efficacy is limited. Pulmonary embolism was excluded in the stem, and no new drug exposure is described to suggest a drug pneumonitis; Robbins notes that IPF can have acute exacerbations with diffuse alveolar damage on top of UIP.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In an acute exacerbation of IPF, diffuse alveolar damage is superimposed on the chronic UIP changes, which matches new bilateral ground-glass opacities and a rapid downhill course. COMLEX: Lung transplantation is the only definitive therapy for IPF; Robbins notes a tyrosine kinase inhibitor and a TGF-β antagonist only slow progression, modestly.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — ARDS pathogenesis, the diffuse alveolar damage that is superimposed in an acute exacerbation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Pulmonary embolism was exclude","E":"Pulmonary embolism was exclude"}}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q01_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old coal miner with 30 years of underground exposure develops progressive dyspnea. High-resolution CT shows multiple intensely blackened scars >1 cm in the upper lobes with central necrosis. Microscopic examination reveals dense collagen and pigment. What pathologic entity has developed?","choices":{"A":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis with coal macules","B":"Silicosis with eggshell calcification of hilar nodes","C":"Asbestosis with pleural plaques","D":"Complicated CWP (progressive massive fibrosis)","E":"Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid nodules"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Progressive massive fibrosis (complicated CWP) is characterized by intensely blackened scars ≥1 cm in greatest diameter, typically in the upper lobes. Microscopically, the lesions consist of dense collagen and pigment, with central necrosis due to local ischemia. This develops on a background of simple CWP and requires many years to develop. It leads to significant pulmonary dysfunction, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale. Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes marks silicosis, pleural plaques mark asbestos exposure, and Caplan syndrome needs rheumatoid arthritis together with the pneumoconiosis, none of which is described here. Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis has 1 to 2 mm coal macules and small nodules, whereas blackened scars of 1 cm or more with necrotic centers define progressive massive fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complicated CWP (progressive massive fibrosis) develops on a background of simple CWP over many years: multiple intensely blackened scars of 1 cm or more (up to 10 cm) made of dense collagen and pigment, often necrotic centrally from local ischemia. COMLEX: It occurs in fewer than 10% of coal workers, brings pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale, and can keep worsening even after dust exposure stops.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — obstructive lung disease, since coal workers also develop emphysema and chronic bronchitis independent of smoking","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis has 1 to 2 mm coal macul"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q02_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old sandblaster presents with progressive dyspnea after 15 years of occupational exposure. Lung biopsy reveals nodules with whorled collagen fibers centrally and dust-laden macrophages peripherally. Polarized microscopy shows weakly birefringent particles. What pathogenic mechanism primarily drives fibrosis in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Coating of particles with iron-containing proteinaceous material","B":"Adsorption of tobacco carcinogens onto mineral fibers","C":"Inflammasome activation by crystals, releasing IL-1 and IL-18","D":"Free radical generation by iron molecules within mineral fibers","E":"Type III hypersensitivity reaction to inhaled organic antigens"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has silicosis from sandblasting exposure. Phagocytosis of inhaled crystalline silica by macrophages activates the inflammasome and stimulates the release of IL-1 and IL-18, leading to recruitment of inflammatory cells and activation of fibroblasts with collagen deposition. The whorled collagen pattern with peripheral dust-laden macrophages is characteristic of silicotic nodules. Crystalline forms (quartz, cristobalite, tridymite) are most fibrogenic. An iron-containing protein coat is what forms asbestos bodies, and adsorbed tobacco carcinogens and iron-catalyzed free radicals are Robbins' explanations for the cancer risk of asbestos fibers, not for silica-driven fibrosis. Reactions to inhaled organic antigens cause hypersensitivity pneumonitis, whereas silica is a mineral dust whose crystals activate the macrophage inflammasome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Macrophages that phagocytose crystalline silica (quartz most often) activate the inflammasome and release IL-1 and IL-18, which recruit inflammatory cells and activate interstitial fibroblasts to lay down collagen; crystalline forms are far more fibrogenic than amorphous silica. COMLEX: Silicosis is the most prevalent chronic occupational disease worldwide, so ask about sandblasting, stone cutting, foundry work, and concrete demolition.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 7 — how pulmonary fibrosis reduces the efficiency of gas diffusion","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"An iron-containing protein coa","E":"Reactions to inhaled organic antigens cause hypersensitivity"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q03_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old former shipyard worker develops progressive dyspnea. Lung biopsy shows diffuse interstitial fibrosis beginning in the lower lobes and subpleural regions, with honeycombing and golden-brown fusiform rods with translucent centers. What is the pathognomonic finding that distinguishes this entity from other causes of diffuse interstitial fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Asbestos bodies (iron-coated asbestos fibers)","B":"Eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes","C":"Coal macules adjacent to respiratory bronchioles","D":"Noncaseating granulomas with Schaumann bodies","E":"Intraalveolar Masson bodies of organizing tissue"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Asbestosis is distinguished from other causes of diffuse interstitial fibrosis by the presence of asbestos bodies—golden-brown, fusiform or beaded rods with translucent centers consisting of asbestos fibers coated with iron-containing proteinaceous material. Asbestosis begins in lower lobes and subpleurally (unlike CWP and silicosis which affect upper lobes), progressing to honeycombing. The fibrosis pattern resembles UIP with fibroblastic foci. Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes belongs to silicosis, and coal macules beside respiratory bronchioles belong to coal workers' pneumoconiosis. Noncaseating granulomas with Schaumann bodies point to sarcoidosis, and even these are not pathognomonic, since they occur in other granulomatous diseases. Masson bodies are airspace plugs of organizing pneumonia over normal lung architecture, with no interstitial fibrosis or honeycombing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asbestosis is diffuse interstitial fibrosis distinguished from other causes only by asbestos bodies — golden-brown, fusiform or beaded rods with translucent centers, asbestos fibers coated with iron-containing protein, the iron probably from macrophage ferritin; other particles coated this way are called ferruginous bodies. COMLEX: Symptoms rarely appear before 10 years and are most common 20–30 years after first exposure, so take an occupational history reaching back decades.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — IPF and its UIP pattern, the diffuse fibrosis that asbestosis resembles","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Noncaseating granulomas with Schaumann bodies point to sarco","E":"Masson bodies are airspace plugs of organizing pneumonia ove"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q04_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old stone cutter presents with chronic cough and weight loss. Chest imaging reveals upper lobe nodular opacities, and hilar lymph nodes show thin sheets of peripheral calcification surrounding non-calcified centers. Sputum is positive for acid-fast bacilli. What radiographic finding is characteristic of this occupational pneumoconiosis?","choices":{"A":"Pleural plaques on diaphragmatic surfaces","B":"Progressive massive fibrosis with blackened scars","C":"Bilateral ground-glass opacities in lower lobes","D":"Honeycombing with lower lobe predominance","E":"Eggshell calcification of hilar lymph nodes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has silicosis with superimposed tuberculosis. Eggshell calcification (calcium surrounding a zone lacking calcification) in hilar lymph nodes is characteristic of silicosis. Silicosis significantly increases susceptibility to tuberculosis due to impaired macrophage function. The upper lobe predominance and nodular pattern are typical of silicosis, unlike asbestosis which affects lower lobes. Pleural plaques mark asbestos exposure, and intensely blackened scars are the coal-pigmented lesions of complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Silicosis can lay thin sheets of calcium in hilar nodes that appear radiographically as eggshell calcification (calcium surrounding an uncalcified zone), with nodules concentrated in the upper lung zones. COMLEX: Crystalline silica inhibits macrophage killing of phagocytosed mycobacteria, so silicosis raises tuberculosis risk, and it also doubles lung-cancer risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 3 — the host risk factors that predispose to active TB","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q05_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old insulation worker presents with progressive dyspnea. CT shows bilateral reticular opacities with lower lobe predominance and well-circumscribed calcified plaques on the anterior parietal pleura and diaphragmatic domes. What feature best distinguishes this pneumoconiosis from coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":{"A":"Upper lobe predominance vs lower lobe involvement","B":"Progressive massive fibrosis vs pleural plaques","C":"Caplan syndrome risk vs no rheumatoid association","D":"Lower lobe/subpleural vs upper lobe predominance","E":"Increased tuberculosis risk vs no TB predisposition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Asbestosis characteristically begins in the lower lobes and subpleurally, while coal workers' pneumoconiosis predominantly affects the upper lobes and upper zones of lower lobes. Pleural plaques (well-circumscribed dense collagen, often calcified) are the most common manifestation of asbestos exposure, developing on anterior and posterolateral parietal pleura and diaphragmatic domes. Asbestosis shows honeycombing in advanced cases, while CWP develops progressive massive fibrosis. Caplan syndrome does not separate them either: Robbins (Table 15.6) and First Aid list it for coal dust, silica and asbestos alike. Tuberculosis risk does not separate the two this way: the added TB susceptibility belongs to silicosis, and Robbins finds no convincing evidence that coal workers' pneumoconiosis raises it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asbestos 'is from the roof but affects the base' (FA): asbestosis begins in the lower lobes and subpleurally, whereas coal workers' pneumoconiosis and silicosis favor the upper lobes; pleural plaques of dense, often calcified collagen are the commonest manifestation of asbestos exposure. COMLEX: Plaques mark exposure rather than disease severity — their size and number do not correlate with exposure level or time since exposure, and they are usually asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 1 — pleural effusions, including the recurrent effusions that asbestos exposure causes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q06_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old coal miner presents with dyspnea. Imaging shows tiny discrete nodules in upper lobes. Biopsy reveals 1-2 mm coal macules consisting of carbon-laden macrophages with delicate collagen networks, located adjacent to respiratory bronchioles with associated centrilobular emphysema. Which statement best characterizes this stage of disease?","choices":{"A":"This represents simple CWP with minimal pulmonary dysfunction","B":"This indicates progressive massive fibrosis requiring lung transplantation","C":"This shows acute silicosis with alveolar proteinosis pattern","D":"This demonstrates asbestosis with fibroblastic foci","E":"This suggests Caplan syndrome with rheumatoid nodules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis is characterized by coal macules (1-2 mm) and coal nodules located adjacent to respiratory bronchioles in the upper lobes. Coal macules contain carbon-laden macrophages; nodules also have delicate collagen networks. Dilation of adjacent alveoli may cause centrilobular emphysema. Simple CWP causes little pulmonary dysfunction and is usually benign. Progressive massive fibrosis (complicated CWP) requires lesions ≥1 cm. Asbestosis is a lower-lobe interstitial fibrosis with asbestos bodies, not upper-lobe coal macules. Acute silicosis follows heavy silica exposure over months to a few years and fills alveoli with lipoproteinaceous material, not 1 to 2 mm carbon-laden macules in a coal miner. Caplan syndrome is pneumoconiosis with intrapulmonary nodules in a patient with rheumatoid arthritis, which is not described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Simple CWP consists of coal macules (1–2 mm, carbon-laden macrophages) and slightly larger nodules with a delicate collagen network, placed next to respiratory bronchioles and heaviest in the upper lobes and upper zones of the lower lobes, sometimes with centrilobular emphysema; it causes little or no pulmonary dysfunction. COMLEX: Anthracosis — the same carbon pigment without disease — is common in urban dwellers and smokers, so pigment alone is not pneumoconiosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — centrilobular (centriacinar) emphysema, the lesion that can form around coal macules","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Acute silicosis follows heavy silica exposure over months to","D":"Asbestosis is a lower-lobe int","E":"Caplan syndrome is pneumoconiosis with intrapulmonary nodule"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q07_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old male smoker presents with dyspnea and pneumothorax. CT shows characteristic cystic and nodular abnormalities with upper lobe predominance. Lung biopsy reveals focal collections of cells with grooved, coffee bean-shaped nuclei accompanied by eosinophils, with irregular cystic spaces from airway destruction. Molecular testing shows BRAF V600E mutation. What does this mutation indicate about the nature of this disease?","choices":{"A":"It represents a reactive inflammatory process responsive to smoking cessation","B":"It indicates impaired GM-CSF signaling requiring replacement therapy","C":"It marks the proliferation as a neoplasm rather than reactive process","D":"It suggests autoimmune pathogenesis with anti-GM-CSF antibodies","E":"It indicates hereditary surfactant dysfunction disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis shows focal collections of Langerhans cells (grooved, coffee bean-shaped nuclei) with eosinophils. While >90% of patients are young adult smokers and some improve with smoking cessation (suggesting reactive process), the presence of activating BRAF mutations marks these proliferations as neoplasms. This is a feature shared with Langerhans cell histiocytosis in other tissues. Despite the neoplastic nature, prognosis is usually excellent. Impaired GM-CSF signaling causes pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, and hereditary surfactant dysfunction comes from genes such as ABCA3; neither involves BRAF in Langerhans cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis forms focal collections of Langerhans cells, often with eosinophils, that scar into irregular cysts; activating BRAF mutations, shared with Langerhans cell histiocytosis in other tissues, mark these proliferations as neoplasms. COMLEX: More than 90% of patients are relatively young adult smokers or ex-smokers with cystic and nodular CT changes; the prognosis is usually excellent, though some patients progress to need lung transplantation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 9 — lymphangioleiomyomatosis (LAM), the other cystic lung disease among the miscellaneous lung tumors","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"While >90% of patients are you","D":"Impaired GM-CSF signaling caus"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q08_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with progressive dyspnea and cough productive of gelatinous material. High-resolution CT shows bilateral patchy asymmetric opacifications. Lung biopsy reveals intraalveolar pink homogeneous PAS-positive precipitates containing cholesterol clefts and surfactant proteins, with normal alveolar walls. Serum testing reveals anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies. What is the underlying pathophysiologic defect?","choices":{"A":"Hereditary mutations in ABCA3 lipid transporter genes","B":"Secondary impairment from hematologic malignancy","C":"Congenital GM-CSF or GM-CSF receptor mutations","D":"Autoantibody-mediated neutralization of GM-CSF function","E":"BRAF-mutated Langerhans cell proliferation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient has autoimmune (formerly \"acquired\") pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, which represents 90% of all PAP cases. It is caused by autoantibodies that bind and neutralize GM-CSF function. Loss of GM-CSF signaling blocks terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages, impairing their ability to catabolize surfactant. This leads to intraalveolar accumulation of surfactant. PAP shows PAS-positive material with cholesterol clefts, distinguishing it from other causes of consolidation. ABCA3 mutations cause surfactant dysfunction disorders, mutations in GM-CSF or its receptor cause the very rare hereditary PAP of neonates, and BRAF-mutated Langerhans cells define pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis; the anti-GM-CSF antibodies here point to autoimmune PAP. Secondary PAP is uncommon and accompanies hematopoietic disorders and malignancies, whereas anti–GM-CSF autoantibodies define autoimmune PAP, about 90% of cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Autoimmune PAP (90% of PAP, mainly adults, no familial tendency) is caused by autoantibodies that neutralize GM-CSF; the alveoli fill with pink, homogeneous, PAS-positive precipitate containing cholesterol clefts and surfactant proteins while the alveolar walls stay normal. COMLEX: Adults cough up gelatinous chunks and get secondary infections; whole-lung lavage is the standard of care, and GM-CSF therapy helps more than half of autoimmune cases.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant's composition and function, the material that accumulates when clearance fails","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Secondary PAP is uncommon and accompanies hematopoietic diso","C":"ABCA3 mutations cause surfacta"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q09_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman with pulmonary alveolar proteinosis undergoes whole-lung lavage with clinical improvement. What distinguishes the ultrastructural findings in autoimmune PAP from surfactant dysfunction disorders on electron microscopy?","choices":{"A":"Absence of cholesterol clefts in alveolar spaces","B":"Normal surfactant lamellae in type II pneumocytes","C":"Presence of lamellar bodies with electron-dense cores","D":"Accumulation of Langerhans cells with Birbeck granules","E":"Presence of asbestos bodies with iron coating"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In autoimmune PAP, ultrastructurally the surfactant lamellae in type II pneumocytes are NORMAL, as the defect is in macrophage clearance of surfactant (due to impaired GM-CSF signaling), not in surfactant production. In contrast, surfactant dysfunction disorders (e.g., ABCA3 mutations) show abnormal type II pneumocytes with small surfactant lamellae with electron-dense cores. Both show intraalveolar PAS-positive material, but the pneumocyte ultrastructure differs. Cholesterol clefts are present in the alveolar precipitate of autoimmune PAP, Birbeck granules mark Langerhans cells, and iron-coated asbestos bodies mark asbestosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ultrastructure separates the two: in PAP the surfactant lamellae inside type II pneumocytes are normal because the defect lies in clearance, whereas surfactant dysfunction disorders show abnormal lamellar bodies. COMLEX: Whole-lung lavage benefits PAP regardless of the underlying defect, consistent with surfactant that has accumulated rather than surfactant that is abnormal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type II pneumocytes, whose lamellar bodies store and secrete surfactant","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q10_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old demolition worker with 20 years of exposure to concrete dust presents with dyspnea. Chest CT reveals upper lobe nodular opacities. What particle size range is most dangerous for development of pneumoconiosis because these particles can reach terminal airways and deposit in their linings?","choices":{"A":"1 to 5 micrometers in diameter","B":"10 to 20 micrometers in diameter","C":"Less than 0.5 micrometers in diameter","D":"Greater than 25 micrometers in diameter","E":"5 to 10 micrometers in diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Particles from 1 to 5 µm in diameter are most dangerous because they can reach terminal small airways and air sacs and deposit in their linings. Larger particles are filtered in upper airways, while smaller particles may be exhaled. This size range allows deep penetration into the lung parenchyma. The particle size, along with dust concentration, duration of exposure, and clearance effectiveness, determines dust retention and disease development.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Particles 1–5 µm across are the most dangerous because they reach the terminal small airways and air sacs and deposit in their linings; dust retention also depends on dust concentration, exposure duration, and how well clearance works. COMLEX: Anything that impairs mucociliary clearance — notably cigarette smoking — increases dust accumulation, and smoking worsens the injury from all inhaled mineral dusts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — the bronchioles, where conducting airways end and small inhaled particles deposit","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Larger particles are filtered ","C":"Larger particles are filtered ","D":"Larger particles are filtered ","E":"Larger particles are filtered "}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q11_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 56-year-old former asbestos insulation installer develops lung adenocarcinoma. He has a 40 pack-year smoking history. What magnitude of increased lung cancer risk would be expected from the combination of asbestos exposure and smoking compared to asbestos exposure alone?","choices":{"A":"Two-fold increase with combined exposure","B":"Same risk as asbestos alone","C":"Ten-fold increase with combined exposure","D":"55-fold increase vs 5-fold with asbestos alone","E":"No synergistic effect between asbestos and smoking"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Asbestos and tobacco smoking show remarkable synergy in lung carcinoma development. Studies show asbestos exposure alone increases lung cancer risk 5-fold, while the combination of asbestos and smoking increases risk 55-fold. This synergy occurs because tobacco carcinogens are adsorbed onto asbestos fibers, and smoking impairs mucociliary clearance of fibers. Asbestos acts as both tumor initiator and promoter through free radical generation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Asbestos and smoking are synergistic: one study of asbestos workers found a 5-fold rise in lung carcinoma with asbestos alone and a 55-fold rise with both, because tobacco carcinogens adsorb onto the fibers and smoking slows fiber clearance; asbestos acts as both tumor initiator and promoter. COMLEX: FA notes bronchogenic carcinoma is more common than mesothelioma after asbestos exposure, and that smoking is not a risk factor for mesothelioma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 2 — tobacco, industrial hazards, and air pollution as lung cancer risk factors","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q12_b1","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiology, pathogenesis, morphology, and clinical features of the major pneumoconioses","batch":1,"stem":"A 48-year-old jeweler using chalk molds develops acute onset dyspnea over 6 months with heavy silica exposure. Lung biopsy shows abundant lipoproteinaceous material filling alveoli. What pathologic entity has developed from this intense short-term silica exposure?","choices":{"A":"Chronic silicosis with whorled collagen nodules","B":"Acute silicosis with alveolar proteinosis-like pattern","C":"Progressive massive fibrosis with necrotic centers","D":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis with macules","E":"Asbestosis with honeycombing"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute silicosis results from heavy silica exposure over months to a few years (unlike chronic silicosis requiring decades). It is characterized by accumulation of abundant lipoproteinaceous material within alveoli, morphologically identical to pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. This differs from chronic silicosis, which presents as slowly progressing nodular fibrosing pneumoconiosis with whorled collagen nodules. Acute silicosis has poor prognosis. Asbestosis is a slowly developing lower-lobe fibrosis from asbestos, not the result of months of heavy silica exposure. Progressive massive fibrosis is blackened scars of 1 cm or more made of dense collagen and pigment, not lipoproteinaceous material filling the alveoli. Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis comes from coal dust and shows coal macules, not alveoli filled with lipoproteinaceous material after silica exposure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heavy silica exposure over months to a few years can cause acute silicosis — alveoli filled with abundant lipoproteinaceous material, morphologically identical to alveolar proteinosis — instead of the nodular fibrosis that takes decades. COMLEX: Silicosis onset may be insidious (10–30 years, most common), accelerated (within 10 years), or rapid (weeks to months after intense exposure to fine silica dust), so exposure intensity shapes the presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, where Robbins lists acute silicosis among the secondary causes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Progressive massive fibrosis is blackened scars of 1 cm or m","D":"Simple coal workers' pneumoconiosis comes from coal dust and"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q13_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist examines lung tissue from three different occupational disease cases. Case A shows serpentine chrysotile fibers; Case B shows straight amphibole fibers; Case C shows coal dust. Which case is most likely to develop malignant mesothelioma?","choices":{"A":"Case A (chrysotile) due to greater industrial use","B":"Case B (amphibole) due to deeper lung penetration","C":"Case C (coal) due to contaminating silica content","D":"All three have equal mesothelioma risk","E":"None develop mesothelioma; only lung carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Amphibole asbestos fibers (straight, stiff) are more pathogenic than serpentine chrysotile, particularly for mesothelioma induction. Amphiboles align with airstreams and penetrate deeper into lungs, reaching the interstitium and mesothelial cells of the pleura. They are also less soluble than chrysotiles. Chrysotiles (flexible, curled) impact in upper airways and are removed by mucociliary elevator. Coal dust does not cause mesothelioma. Asbestos exposure causes both lung carcinoma and pleural and peritoneal mesotheliomas, with amphiboles the most potent for mesothelioma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serpentine chrysotile (90% of industrial asbestos) is curled and flexible, impacts in the upper airways, is cleared by the mucociliary elevator, and leaches out because it is more soluble; straight, stiff amphiboles align with the airstream, reach the distal lung near the pleura, and are more pathogenic, especially for mesothelioma. COMLEX: Family members of asbestos workers have higher rates of lung carcinoma and mesothelioma, so household exposure belongs in the history.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 42 → Obj 3 — malignant mesothelioma of the pleura","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Chrysotiles (flexible, curled)","C":"Coal dust does not cause mesot","E":"Asbestos exposure causes both lung carcinoma and pleural and"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q14_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 54-year-old welder presents for occupational health screening. Chest X-ray shows increased radiodensity. Lung biopsy shows iron oxide particles. What is this condition called?","choices":{"A":"Anthracosis from urban air pollution","B":"Berylliosis with granuloma formation","C":"Baritosis from barium sulfate exposure","D":"Stannosis from tin oxide exposure","E":"Siderosis from iron oxide welding fumes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins Table 15.6 (course Pulm SDL 25–27 PDF p49) lists iron oxide as the agent of siderosis, with welding as the exposure, so iron oxide particles in a welder point to siderosis. The same table lists barium sulfate as the cause of baritosis and tin oxide as the cause of stannosis, both from mining, which does not match iron oxide from welding fumes. Anthracosis is carbon pigment from coal dust, urban air, or smoking, not iron oxide, and beryllium causes acute berylliosis or beryllium granulomatosis rather than a harmless density.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Match the dust to the job — Robbins' mineral-dust table pairs iron oxide with siderosis (welding), barium sulfate with baritosis (mining), and tin oxide with stannosis (mining); Robbins describes only coal, silica, and asbestos in detail as the dusts that cause pulmonary fibrosis. COMLEX: Smoking worsens the effects of all inhaled mineral dusts, so a welder who smokes still needs cessation counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — alveolar macrophages, the first-line defense that engulfs inhaled particles","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The same table lists barium su"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q15_b1","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate among the major pneumoconioses based on patient history, radiographic findings, and gross/microscopic morphology","batch":1,"stem":"A 65-year-old former miner dies from respiratory failure. Autopsy reveals lungs with upper lobe predominance of scarring. Three pneumoconioses are being considered based on occupational history. Which microscopic feature would definitively distinguish silicosis from the others?","choices":{"A":"Ferruginous bodies with iron-protein coating on the fibers","B":"Coal macules with carbon-laden macrophages","C":"Central necrosis within massively fibrotic lesions","D":"Whorled collagen nodules, weakly birefringent particles","E":"Honeycombing with lower lobe predominance"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Silicotic nodules show characteristic whorled collagen fibers with peripheral dust-laden macrophages. Polarized microscopy reveals weakly birefringent silicate particles, which is diagnostic. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis shows coal macules with carbon pigment (non-birefringent), while asbestosis shows asbestos bodies (ferruginous bodies) with lower lobe predominance. Central necrosis can occur in silicosis (from TB or ischemia) and in progressive massive fibrosis from coal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The silicotic nodule — a central area of whorled collagen with a peripheral zone of dust-laden macrophages — shows weakly birefringent silicate particles under polarized light; nodules may soften and cavitate from superimposed TB or ischemia. COMLEX: FA's rule — silica, coal, and beryllium come from the base but affect the roof — puts all three in the upper lobes, so distribution alone cannot separate them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 49 → Obj 4 — reactivation TB, whose cavitation can complicate silicotic nodules","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Coal workers' pneumoconiosis s"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q16_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 32-year-old male smoker with pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis achieves complete remission after smoking cessation alone. What does this clinical response suggest about the pathogenesis in this particular case?","choices":{"A":"Presence of BRAF mutation indicating neoplastic process","B":"Reactive inflammation, not neoplastic proliferation","C":"Autoimmune pathogenesis requiring immunosuppression","D":"Defective GM-CSF signaling requiring replacement therapy","E":"Hereditary genetic mutation in surfactant proteins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"While some cases of pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis have BRAF mutations (marking them as neoplasms), >90% of patients are young adult smokers, and about half improve after smoking cessation alone. This suggests that in many cases, the lesions represent a reactive inflammatory process rather than true neoplasia. The disease shows a spectrum from reactive to neoplastic, with smoking cessation responsive cases likely representing the reactive end. Autoimmune mechanisms and defective GM-CSF signaling belong to pulmonary alveolar proteinosis, not to Langerhans cell histiocytosis. Hereditary surfactant-protein mutations cause surfactant dysfunction disorders, whereas pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis is smoking-related and improves in about half of smokers who quit.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Among smokers with pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis, about half improve after stopping smoking, which Robbins reads as evidence that some lesions are a reactive inflammatory process, while BRAF-mutated cases are neoplasms. COMLEX: More than 90% of patients are young adult smokers or ex-smokers, so cessation is the first step; the disease usually has an excellent prognosis but progresses in some patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — DIP and the other smoking-related interstitial diseases that also improve after cessation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Hereditary surfactant-protein mutations cause surfactant dys"}},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q17_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-month-old infant presents with rapidly progressive respiratory failure. Genetic testing reveals autosomal recessive mutations in ABCA3. Electron microscopy of lung tissue would show which characteristic ultrastructural finding distinguishing this from autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis?","choices":{"A":"Small lamellae with dense cores in type II cells","B":"Normal surfactant lamellae in type II pneumocytes","C":"Intraalveolar PAS-positive proteinaceous material","D":"Langerhans cells with Birbeck granules (tennis rackets)","E":"Cholesterol clefts within alveolar spaces"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surfactant dysfunction disorders (like ABCA3 mutations) show ABNORMAL type II pneumocytes with small surfactant lamellae containing electron-dense cores on electron microscopy. This contrasts with autoimmune PAP, where type II pneumocytes have NORMAL surfactant lamellae—the defect is in macrophage clearance, not production. ABCA3 encodes a transmembrane lipid transporter regulating surfactant homeostasis. ABCA3 mutations typically present in early infancy with rapidly progressive respiratory failure. Intraalveolar PAS-positive material and cholesterol clefts are features of alveolar proteinosis in general, so they do not separate ABCA3 disease from the autoimmune form, and Birbeck granules mark Langerhans cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ABCA3, a transmembrane lipid transporter that regulates surfactant homeostasis, is the gene most often mutated in surfactant dysfunction disorders; the autosomal recessive disease usually brings rapidly progressive respiratory failure in the first months of life, and small lamellar bodies with electron-dense cores are diagnostic. COMLEX: Surfactant protein B deficiency (also recessive) affects full-term newborns and is fatal by 3–6 months without lung transplantation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — the key events and timing of lung maturation, including surfactant production","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_ex1_q18_b1","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphology of Langerhans cell histiocytosis and pulmonary alveolar proteinosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old man with autoimmune pulmonary alveolar proteinosis undergoes GM-CSF replacement therapy with partial response. What is the primary mechanism by which GM-CSF deficiency leads to surfactant accumulation?","choices":{"A":"Impaired surfactant production by immature type II pneumocytes","B":"Increased surfactant secretion from lamellar bodies","C":"Macrophages fail to mature and cannot catabolize surfactant","D":"Direct cytotoxicity to type II pneumocytes","E":"Activation of Langerhans cell proliferation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In autoimmune PAP, anti-GM-CSF autoantibodies neutralize GM-CSF function. Loss of GM-CSF signaling blocks the terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages, impairing their ability to catabolize (break down) surfactant. This leads to accumulation of surfactant in alveolar spaces. The defect is in clearance, not production—type II pneumocytes produce normal surfactant. GM-CSF knockout mice develop PAP and are \"cured\" by GM-CSF treatment (Robbins Ch 15), the rationale for GM-CSF therapy that restores macrophage function. Langerhans cell proliferation is the lesion of pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis and has nothing to do with GM-CSF loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loss of GM-CSF signaling blocks the terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages, so they cannot catabolize surfactant; GM-CSF knockout mice develop PAP and are cured by GM-CSF treatment. COMLEX: Secondary PAP — with hematopoietic disorders, malignancies, or immunodeficiency — is thought to impair the same GM-CSF–dependent macrophage maturation, so therapy aimed at the underlying disorder may help.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — alveolar macrophages in pulmonary host defense, whose failure explains PAP's secondary infections","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The defect is in clearance, no"}}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with a history of myocardial infarction presents with orthopnea and bilateral lower lobe crackles. Chest X-ray shows bilateral basal opacities. Lung biopsy reveals engorged alveolar capillaries with intraalveolar transudate appearing as finely granular pale pink material. What is the primary underlying mechanism of this pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased alveolar capillary permeability from endothelial injury","B":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia","C":"Increased hydrostatic pressure from left-sided heart failure","D":"Lymphatic obstruction from tumor compression","E":"Direct alveolar epithelial injury from inhaled toxins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient has hemodynamic (cardiogenic) pulmonary edema caused by increased hydrostatic pressure from left-sided congestive heart failure following MI. Edema accumulates initially in basal regions of lower lobes where hydrostatic pressure is greatest (dependent edema). Histologically, alveolar capillaries are engorged with intraalveolar transudate (protein-poor fluid) appearing as finely granular pale pink material. This contrasts with noncardiogenic edema from alveolar injury, which produces protein-rich inflammatory exudate. Lymphatic obstruction is a rare cause of edema, and nothing here suggests a tumor; the post-infarction heart failure and the transudate point to raised hydrostatic pressure. Hypoalbuminemia lowers oncotic pressure, which is not this patient's problem; his engorged capillaries and transudate point to raised hydrostatic pressure from left heart failure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemodynamic pulmonary edema from raised hydrostatic (pulmonary venous) pressure, most commonly left-sided heart failure, accumulates first in the basal regions of the lower lobes, where hydrostatic pressure is greatest (dependent edema). COMLEX: Volume overload and pulmonary vein obstruction raise hydrostatic pressure the same way, and Robbins stresses that therapy and outcome depend on the underlying cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 10 — the sequence of changes as left atrial pressure rises toward pulmonary edema","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"This contrasts with noncardiog","B":"Hypoalbuminemia lowers oncotic pressure, which is not this p","E":"This contrasts with noncardiog"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with nephrotic syndrome and serum albumin of 2.0 g/dL develops progressive dyspnea. Imaging shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. What mechanism accounts for pulmonary edema formation in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Increased pulmonary venous pressure from mitral stenosis","B":"Neutrophil-mediated endothelial injury and increased permeability","C":"Direct pneumocyte injury from inhaled irritants","D":"Lymphatic obstruction reducing fluid clearance","E":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient has hemodynamic pulmonary edema from decreased oncotic pressure due to severe hypoalbuminemia (nephrotic syndrome). With low plasma oncotic pressure, the balance of Starling forces favors fluid movement from capillaries into interstitium and alveoli. Other causes of decreased oncotic pressure include liver disease and protein-losing enteropathies. This is less common than increased hydrostatic pressure but still produces cardiogenic-type edema with transudate rather than inflammatory exudate. Neutrophil-mediated endothelial injury and direct pneumocyte injury from irritants belong to edema from alveolar wall injury, which produces an inflammatory exudate rather than the transudate of hypoalbuminemia. Raised pulmonary venous (hydrostatic) pressure, as in mitral stenosis, is a separate hemodynamic cause; nephrotic syndrome causes edema by lowering oncotic pressure through hypoalbuminemia. Lymphatic obstruction is a rare, separate cause of edema, whereas nephrotic syndrome falls under decreased oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Decreased plasma oncotic pressure — hypoalbuminemia from nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, or protein-losing enteropathy — is a less common hemodynamic cause of pulmonary edema than raised hydrostatic pressure (Robbins Table 15.1). COMLEX: Whatever the mechanism, pulmonary congestion and edema produce heavy, wet lungs, and treatment targets the underlying cause — here, the protein loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 13 — the Starling forces of pulmonary interstitial fluid that low oncotic pressure tips toward edema","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Raised pulmonary venous (hydrostatic) pressure, as in mitral","D":"Lymphatic obstruction is a rare, separate cause of edema, wh"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is hospitalized with severe pancreatitis and develops acute respiratory failure. Chest imaging shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. Arterial blood gas reveals severe hypoxemia despite supplemental oxygen. What category of pulmonary edema has this patient developed?","choices":{"A":"Hemodynamic edema from left ventricular failure","B":"Noncardiogenic edema from alveolar septal injury","C":"Hemodynamic edema from decreased oncotic pressure","D":"Lymphatic obstruction from mediastinal fibrosis","E":"Hemodynamic edema from pulmonary vein obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has developed noncardiogenic pulmonary edema from alveolar septal injury in the setting of severe pancreatitis, which triggers systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS). Circulating inflammatory mediators cause primary injury to vascular endothelium or alveolar epithelial cells, producing inflammatory exudate that leaks into interstitium and alveoli. This is a key feature of acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). The bilateral infiltrates and severe hypoxemia refractory to oxygen support the diagnosis of ARDS. Lymphatic obstruction is a rare hemodynamic cause and nothing here involves the mediastinum, whereas pancreatitis is on Robbins' list of indirect injuries that cause alveolar damage. Left ventricular failure causes hemodynamic edema from raised hydrostatic pressure, whereas pancreatitis is an indirect, systemic inflammatory injury to the alveolar septa. Decreased oncotic pressure is a hemodynamic cause tied to hypoalbuminemia, whereas pancreatitis injures the alveolar septa through systemic inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Noncardiogenic edema follows injury to the alveolar septa — primary endothelial injury, or epithelial damage with secondary microvascular injury — producing an inflammatory exudate; systemic inflammatory response syndrome from sepsis, burns, pancreatitis, or extensive trauma injures the lung indirectly. COMLEX: Abrupt hypoxemia with bilateral pulmonary edema and no cardiac failure defines acute lung injury, and ARDS is its severe clinical form.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 13 — physiologic shunt, the perfused but unventilated alveoli behind refractory hypoxemia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Left ventricular failure causes hemodynamic edema from raise","C":"Decreased oncotic pressure is a hemodynamic cause tied to hy"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with gram-negative sepsis develops ARDS. What is the earliest event in the pathogenesis that sets in motion the vicious cycle of increasing inflammation and pulmonary damage?","choices":{"A":"Endothelial activation by circulating inflammatory mediators or pneumocyte injury","B":"Neutrophil degranulation releasing proteases and reactive oxygen species","C":"Accumulation of protein-rich edema fluid in alveolar spaces","D":"Formation of hyaline membranes from inspissated protein and cellular debris","E":"Type II pneumocyte necrosis with surfactant abnormalities"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Endothelial activation is an important early event initiating ARDS pathogenesis. In sepsis, circulating inflammatory mediators (e.g., TNF, cytokines) activate pulmonary endothelium directly. Alternatively, pneumocyte injury is sensed by resident alveolar macrophages that secrete TNF and other mediators acting on neighboring endothelium. Activated endothelial cells express increased adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines, setting in motion the inflammatory cascade. Neutrophil recruitment, edema accumulation, and hyaline membrane formation are subsequent events. Type II pneumocyte necrosis with surfactant abnormalities comes later, as edema fluid and hyaline membranes form, after the early endothelial activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ALI/ARDS starts with injury to pneumocytes and pulmonary endothelium; endothelial activation — set off directly by circulating mediators in sepsis or severe tissue injury, or indirectly by signals from resident alveolar macrophages after pneumocyte injury — is the key early event in the vicious cycle. COMLEX: Sepsis, diffuse pulmonary infection, gastric aspiration, and trauma head Robbins' list of ARDS triggers, and several are often present at once.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 9 — viral bronchiolitis and laryngotracheobronchitis, infections that can progress to ARDS","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Neutrophil recruitment, edema ","E":"Type II pneumocyte necrosis with surfactant abnormalities co"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"During ARDS pathogenesis, activated neutrophils migrate into alveoli and release multiple injurious mediators. What mechanism do neutrophils use that directly contributes to lung damage beyond traditional degranulation?","choices":{"A":"Production of surfactant to stabilize alveoli","B":"Release of neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs)","C":"Phagocytosis of bacterial pathogens and debris","D":"Secretion of growth factors promoting fibroblast proliferation","E":"Expression of adhesion molecules for endothelial binding"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neutrophils adhering to activated endothelium migrate into interstitium and alveoli, where they degranulate releasing proteases, reactive oxygen species (ROS), and cytokines. Experimental evidence shows neutrophils also release neutrophil extracellular traps (NETs) that contribute directly to lung damage. NETs consist of chromatin decorated with antimicrobial proteins that can trap and kill pathogens but also injure host tissue. These neutrophil-mediated injuries create a feedback loop of inflammation and endothelial damage central to ARDS. Growth factors that drive fibroblast growth, such as TGF-β and PDGF, come mainly from macrophages in the later fibroproliferative phase, not from neutrophils injuring the lung. Surfactant comes from type II pneumocytes, whose necrosis in ARDS causes surfactant abnormalities; neutrophils instead release proteases, reactive oxygen species, and NETs. Adhesion molecules are expressed by activated endothelium so neutrophils can adhere; they are not a neutrophil product that damages the lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neutrophils that adhere to activated endothelium migrate into the interstitium and alveoli and release proteases, reactive oxygen species, and cytokines; experimental evidence adds neutrophil extracellular traps as a direct cause of lung damage, closing the feedback loop of inflammation and endothelial injury. COMLEX: Robbins notes ARDS is more common and carries a worse prognosis in people with alcohol use disorder and in smokers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 5 — the neutrophil-rich exudate of the hepatization stages of lobar pneumonia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Surfactant comes from type II pneumocytes, whose necrosis in","E":"Adhesion molecules are expressed by activated endothelium so"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with ARDS develops characteristic hyaline membranes lining the alveoli. What is the primary composition and pathophysiologic mechanism leading to hyaline membrane formation?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil extracellular traps with chromatin and antimicrobial proteins","B":"Mucin secreted by hyperplastic goblet cells","C":"Fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with debris of necrotic alveolar epithelial cells","D":"Proliferating type II pneumocytes attempting epithelial regeneration","E":"Collagen deposition from fibroblast activation and TGF-β signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyaline membranes are the characteristic histologic feature of the acute (exudative) phase of diffuse alveolar damage in ARDS. Robbins describes them as fibrin-rich edema fluid mixed with the remnants of necrotic epithelial cells, lining the alveolar ducts (Ch 15): endothelial injury makes capillaries leaky so protein-rich exudate floods the alveoli, and necrosis of alveolar epithelium contributes the cellular debris. Similar membranes are seen in neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. They are not mucin from goblet cells, neutrophil extracellular traps, proliferating type II cells (a later reparative change) or collagen from fibroblasts (the organizing phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyaline membranes are fibrin-rich, protein-rich edema fluid inspissated with debris from necrotic alveolar epithelial cells; they are the hallmark of diffuse alveolar damage and look like the membranes of neonatal hyaline membrane disease. COMLEX: Early loss of functional surfactant stiffens the lungs, which is why ARDS patients need intubation and high ventilatory pressures.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — surfactant deficiency of the newborn, the other setting where hyaline membranes form","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with chronic mitral stenosis undergoes lung biopsy showing abundant macrophages containing golden-brown pigment within alveoli, along with thickened alveolar walls and interstitial fibrosis. The lungs appear firm and brown grossly. What are the pigment-laden cells and gross appearance called?","choices":{"A":"Ferruginous bodies and asbestosis","B":"Heart failure cells and brown induration","C":"Anthracotic pigment and coal workers' pneumoconiosis","D":"Cholesterol clefts and organizing pneumonia","E":"Carbon-laden macrophages and smoker's lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In long-standing pulmonary congestion (e.g., mitral stenosis), alveolar microhemorrhages lead to red blood cell extravasation. Alveolar macrophages phagocytose the red cells and accumulate hemosiderin (iron-containing breakdown product of hemoglobin), appearing golden-brown on histology. These are called \"heart failure cells.\" Hemosiderin-laden macrophages become abundant, and fibrosis and thickening of alveolar walls cause the soggy lungs to become firm and brown grossly—termed \"brown induration.\" This impairs respiratory function and predisposes to infection. Ferruginous (asbestos) bodies are iron-coated asbestos fibers of asbestosis, anthracotic pigment is black carbon, and organizing pneumonia fills airspaces with connective tissue plugs; none matches hemosiderin-laden macrophages from chronic congestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Long-standing congestion, classically mitral stenosis, causes alveolar microhemorrhages; macrophages that ingest the red cells fill with hemosiderin ('heart failure' cells), and fibrosis with thickened alveolar walls turns the soggy lungs firm and brown (brown induration). COMLEX: These changes impair respiratory function and predispose to infection, so chronic congestion is not a benign finding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — diffuse alveolar hemorrhage syndromes, where hemosiderin-laden macrophages also mark old bleeding","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old trauma patient develops ARDS. Lung biopsy in the acute phase reveals diffuse alveolar damage with characteristic eosinophilic hyaline membranes lining alveolar ducts. What additional morphologic features would be expected in the acute exudative stage?","choices":{"A":"Hyaline membranes, edema, capillary congestion, and type II cell necrosis","B":"Proliferating fibroblasts within alveolar walls and organizing collagen deposition","C":"Type II pneumocyte hyperplasia reconstituting alveolar lining epithelium","D":"Honeycombing with cystic fibrotic airspaces and architectural distortion","E":"Noncaseating granulomas with multinucleated giant cells and asteroid bodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The acute exudative stage of diffuse alveolar damage (DAD/ARDS) shows: (1) intraalveolar hyaline membranes (inspissated protein-rich edema and cellular debris) lining alveolar ducts, (2) interstitial and intraalveolar edema, (3) capillary congestion with microthrombi, and (4) type II pneumocyte necrosis. These lesions are not evenly distributed. Fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition occur in the later organizing/fibroproliferative phase. Type II pneumocyte hyperplasia occurs during resolution. Honeycombing represents end-stage fibrosis. Noncaseating granulomas with asteroid bodies belong to sarcoidosis, not to diffuse alveolar damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins' key concept for diffuse alveolar damage: hyaline membranes lining alveolar walls with edema, scattered neutrophils and macrophages, and epithelial cell necrosis, plus congestion and fibrin deposition in the acute exudative stage. COMLEX: Profound dyspnea and tachypnea herald ARDS, followed by diffuse bilateral infiltrates and hypoxemia that may be refractory to oxygen therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 6 — the layers of the respiratory membrane that edema and hyaline membranes thicken","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Honeycombing represents end-st"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A patient who survives the acute phase of ARDS enters the organizing/fibroproliferative phase. What cellular events and mediators drive this phase of injury?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophil degranulation with protease and reactive oxygen species release","B":"Type II pneumocyte necrosis with surfactant abnormalities","C":"Endothelial activation with increased adhesion molecule expression","D":"Macrophage removal of debris with TGF-β and PDGF release stimulating fibrosis","E":"Alveolar epithelial fluid clearance restoring normal gas exchange"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In the organizing/fibroproliferative phase, if inflammatory triggers abate, macrophages remove intraalveolar debris and release fibrogenic cytokines including TGF-β (transforming growth factor-β) and PDGF (platelet-derived growth factor). These factors stimulate fibroblast growth and collagen deposition, leading to fibrosis of alveolar walls. Some patients enter extended resolution (months) with type II pneumocyte proliferation replacing type I cells and endothelial restoration. Others develop impaired cardiopulmonary function from persistent fibrosis. Neutrophil degranulation with proteases and reactive oxygen species belongs to the early injury cycle, before macrophages clear debris and release fibrogenic cytokines. Endothelial activation is the important early event that starts ARDS, not the organizing phase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: If the inflammatory triggers abate, macrophages clear the intraalveolar debris and release fibrogenic TGF-β and platelet-derived growth factor, which stimulate fibroblast growth and collagen deposition in the alveolar walls. COMLEX: ARDS remains lethal despite supportive care — a 2016 study of ICUs in 50 countries found mortality of 35% for mild, 40% for moderate, and 46% for severe ARDS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 1 — IPF, where profibrotic factors such as TGF-β likewise drive interstitial fibrosis","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutrophil degranulation with proteases and reactive oxygen ","C":"Endothelial activation is the important early event that sta"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 28-year-old mountaineer develops dyspnea, cough, and hypoxemia at 4,500 meters altitude without evidence of heart failure or infection. Chest imaging shows bilateral pulmonary infiltrates. What category of pulmonary edema is this, and what is its underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Hemodynamic edema from increased hydrostatic pressure","B":"Edema of undetermined origin (high-altitude edema)","C":"Noncardiogenic edema from bacterial pneumonia","D":"Hemodynamic edema from decreased oncotic pressure","E":"Edema from direct alveolar injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-altitude pulmonary edema (HAPE) is classified as \"edema of undetermined origin\" as the exact mechanism remains incompletely understood. It occurs at high altitude in otherwise healthy individuals. Proposed mechanisms include hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction causing uneven perfusion and stress failure of capillaries, increased sympathetic activity, and altered fluid balance. HAPE is not purely hemodynamic or injury-related but represents a unique category. Treatment includes descent and oxygen administration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins files high-altitude and neurogenic (CNS trauma) pulmonary edema under 'edema of undetermined origin'; Guyton's proposed mechanism is uneven hypoxic arteriolar constriction that forces flow through fewer open vessels, raising capillary pressure there until local edema forms. COMLEX: Guyton notes that breathing oxygen usually reverses the process within hours.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 7 — HAPE pathophysiology and clinical findings","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"HAPE is not purely hemodynamic","C":"HAPE is not purely hemodynamic","D":"HAPE is not purely hemodynamic"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"Robbins subdivides pulmonary edema due to alveolar wall injury into direct and indirect injury. Five ICU patients develop noncardiogenic pulmonary edema. In which patient does the edema arise from indirect rather than direct alveolar injury?","choices":{"A":"A woman who aspirated gastric contents","B":"A boy rescued after near-drowning","C":"A firefighter after heavy smoke inhalation","D":"A man after multiple blood transfusions","E":"A woman after thoracic radiation therapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In Robbins Table 15.1, edema due to alveolar wall (microvascular or epithelial) injury is divided into direct injury, from bacterial pneumonia, inhaled gases such as high concentrations of oxygen or smoke, liquid aspiration of gastric contents or near-drowning, radiation, and lung trauma, and indirect injury, from systemic inflammatory response syndrome (sepsis, burns, pancreatitis, extensive trauma), blood transfusion, and drugs and chemicals such as bleomycin, methadone, amphotericin B, heroin, cocaine, kerosene, and paraquat. Transfusion-related edema is therefore the indirect injury. Gastric aspiration, near-drowning, smoke inhalation, and radiation all injure the alveolar wall directly from the airspace or the lung itself.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins divides injury edema into direct injury (bacterial pneumonia, inhaled high-concentration oxygen or smoke, aspiration of gastric contents or near-drowning, radiation, lung trauma) and indirect injury (SIRS from sepsis, burns, pancreatitis, or extensive trauma; transfusion; drugs such as bleomycin, heroin, cocaine, and paraquat). COMLEX: Transfusion-associated lung injury is on Robbins' list of ARDS triggers, so new hypoxemia during or after transfusion should raise it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 3 — oxygen toxicity, one of the direct inhaled-gas injuries in the same table","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric aspiration, near-drown","E":"Gastric aspiration, near-drown"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the major categories of pulmonary edema and list the underlying clinical etiologies associated with each","batch":1,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with breast cancer develops progressive dyspnea. CT shows diffuse interstitial thickening and pleural effusions. Biopsy reveals tumor infiltration of pulmonary lymphatics. What mechanism explains the pulmonary edema?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic obstruction preventing normal fluid clearance","B":"Increased hydrostatic pressure from tumor compression of pulmonary veins","C":"Direct tumor invasion causing alveolar epithelial injury","D":"Decreased oncotic pressure from cancer cachexia","E":"Tumor-secreted cytokines causing endothelial activation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lymphatic obstruction is a rare cause of pulmonary edema. Lymphatics normally clear excess interstitial fluid; when obstructed by tumor (lymphangitic carcinomatosis), sarcoidosis, or post-radiation fibrosis, fluid accumulates in the interstitium. Breast, gastric, and lung cancers commonly cause lymphangitic spread. Imaging shows interstitial thickening and Kerley B lines. Unlike hemodynamic edema, lymphatic obstruction produces chronic progressive symptoms. The edema is typically interstitial rather than alveolar. Increased hydrostatic pressure comes from left heart failure, volume overload, or pulmonary vein obstruction, whereas this biopsy shows tumor within the lymphatics. Alveolar epithelial injury causes noncardiogenic edema with an inflammatory exudate, a different category from lymphatic obstruction. Decreased oncotic pressure comes from hypoalbuminemia (nephrotic syndrome, liver disease, protein-losing enteropathy), a different category from the lymphatic obstruction shown. Cytokine-driven endothelial activation starts the alveolar wall injury of ARDS, not the edema of tumor-filled lymphatics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lymphatic obstruction is the rare third hemodynamic cause of pulmonary edema in Robbins Table 15.1; normally the pulmonary lymphatics pump continually filtered fluid back to the circulation and keep interstitial pressure slightly negative so the alveoli stay dry (Guyton). COMLEX: When tumor blocks those lymphatics, the fluid that capillaries filter all the time has no exit, so edema can form without any rise in capillary pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 41 → Obj 10 — metastatic disease of the lung, here spreading through the lymphatics","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Increased hydrostatic pressure comes from left heart failure","C":"Alveolar epithelial injury causes noncardiogenic edema with ","D":"Decreased oncotic pressure comes from hypoalbuminemia (nephr","E":"Cytokine-driven endothelial activation starts the alveolar w"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man develops ARDS following aspiration of gastric contents. Which cytokine released by resident alveolar macrophages sensing pneumocyte injury acts on neighboring endothelium to initiate endothelial activation?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin-10 (anti-inflammatory cytokine)","B":"TGF-β (transforming growth factor-beta)","C":"TNF (tumor necrosis factor)","D":"GM-CSF (granulocyte-macrophage colony-stimulating factor)","E":"Surfactant protein-D"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When pneumocyte injury occurs (e.g., from aspiration), resident alveolar macrophages act as immune sentinels sensing the damage. They secrete tumor necrosis factor (TNF) and other mediators that act on neighboring pulmonary endothelium, initiating endothelial activation. TNF is a key early proinflammatory cytokine in ARDS pathogenesis. Activated endothelial cells then express adhesion molecules (E-selectin, ICAM-1, VCAM-1), procoagulant proteins, and chemokines that recruit neutrophils and amplify inflammation. TGF-β is a fibrogenic cytokine released later during the organizing phase. IL-10 attenuates the immune response and inhibits activated macrophages, so it does not activate endothelium. GM-CSF signaling drives the terminal differentiation of alveolar macrophages so they can clear surfactant; it is not the mediator that activates endothelium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Resident alveolar macrophages act as sentinels that sense pneumocyte injury and secrete tumor necrosis factor onto the neighboring endothelium; Robbins' figure of the early injured alveolus also shows IL-1 and reactive oxygen species. COMLEX: Gastric aspiration is a direct lung injury in Robbins' classification and one of the conditions most often associated with ARDS.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 2 — aspiration pneumonia as a clinical pneumonia syndrome","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"IL-10 attenuates the immune response and inhibits activated ","B":"TGF-β is a fibrogenic cytokine","D":"GM-CSF signaling drives the terminal differentiation of alve"}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"During ARDS pathogenesis, activated endothelium expresses increased levels of specific molecules that facilitate neutrophil recruitment. What three major categories of molecules are upregulated on activated endothelium?","choices":{"A":"Surfactant proteins, phospholipids, and collectins","B":"Matrix metalloproteinases, collagenases, and elastases","C":"Immunoglobulins, complement components, and acute phase reactants","D":"Growth factors, angiogenic factors, and anti-apoptotic proteins","E":"Adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Endothelial activation in ARDS leads to expression of: (1) adhesion molecules (E-selectin, P-selectin, ICAM-1, VCAM-1) that mediate neutrophil rolling and firm adhesion; (2) procoagulant proteins (tissue factor) that promote microthrombi formation; and (3) chemokines (IL-8, MCP-1) that attract and activate neutrophils. Together, these changes facilitate neutrophil extravasation into alveoli and amplify the inflammatory cascade. Some mediators also directly injure endothelial cells, worsening capillary leak and edema formation. Proteases and elastases are released by the neutrophils after they arrive, not expressed by the endothelium, and immunoglobulins, complement, and acute phase reactants are not among the endothelial products Robbins lists. Surfactant comes from type II pneumocytes, not from activated endothelium, which expresses adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines. Growth factors such as TGF-β and PDGF come from macrophages in the later fibroproliferative phase, not from activated endothelium recruiting neutrophils.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cytokines induce activated endothelial cells to express more adhesion molecules, procoagulant proteins, and chemokines; neutrophils then adhere, migrate into the interstitium and alveoli, and degranulate. COMLEX: Disseminated intravascular coagulation appears in Robbins' list of conditions associated with ARDS, reflecting the procoagulant side of the injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 7 — the neutrophilic intra-alveolar inflammation of bacterial pneumonia","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Surfactant comes from type II pneumocytes, not from activate","B":"Proteases and elastases are re","D":"Growth factors such as TGF-β and PDGF come from macrophages "}},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the detailed pathogenesis of ARDS, focusing on the cascade of inflammation, endothelial activation, neutrophil involvement, and formation of intraalveolar exudate","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with ARDS develops worsening ventilation-perfusion mismatch contributing to severe hypoxemia. What anatomic distribution pattern of diffuse alveolar damage explains this physiologic derangement?","choices":{"A":"Uniform involvement of all lung regions with equal compliance","B":"Exclusively subpleural distribution with central sparing","C":"Exclusive involvement of upper lobes with lower lobe sparing","D":"Central airway involvement with peripheral airspace sparing","E":"Uneven: stiff, poorly aerated regions beside near-normal ones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The lesions in ARDS/DAD are NOT evenly distributed throughout the lungs. There are typically areas that are stiff and poorly aerated (consolidated regions with edema, hyaline membranes, and atelectasis) alongside regions with nearly normal compliance and ventilation. Poorly aerated regions continue to be perfused (blood flow continues), creating ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) mismatch where perfusion exceeds ventilation. This mismatch exacerbates hypoxemia and cyanosis, explaining why supplemental oxygen alone often fails to correct hypoxemia in ARDS. Diffuse alveolar damage involves the alveoli in an uneven patchwork, not an upper-lobe-only pattern or the central airways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ARDS lesions are not evenly distributed — stiff, poorly aerated regions sit beside regions of nearly normal compliance and ventilation — and because the poorly aerated areas stay perfused, ventilation–perfusion mismatch worsens hypoxemia and cyanosis. COMLEX: That mismatch is why ARDS hypoxemia may resist oxygen therapy, and respiratory acidosis can develop.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 12 — the V/Q ratio and its effect on alveolar PO2 and PCO2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 74-year-old man dies of acute decompensated left-sided heart failure. Sections from the lower lobes show pulmonary edema. Which histologic description best fits the alveolar findings of this hemodynamic edema, as distinct from the injury-related edema of ARDS?","choices":{"A":"Waxy hyaline membranes lining distended alveolar ducts","B":"Neutrophil-rich exudate with fibrin filling alveoli","C":"Type II cell hyperplasia with granulation tissue","D":"Engorged capillaries with a fine, pale pink transudate","E":"Polypoid plugs of loose fibroblastic tissue in airspaces"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes hemodynamic pulmonary edema, most commonly from left-sided heart failure, as accumulating first in the basal regions of the lower lobes, where hydrostatic pressure is greatest (dependent edema); histologically the alveolar capillaries are engorged and the alveoli contain an intraalveolar transudate that appears as finely granular pale pink material, sometimes with microhemorrhages and hemosiderin-laden macrophages. Waxy hyaline membranes made of fibrin-rich edema fluid and necrotic epithelial remnants mark the acute exudative stage of ARDS (diffuse alveolar damage). A neutrophil-rich fibrinous exudate filling alveoli describes bacterial pneumonia. Type II pneumocyte proliferation with granulation tissue is the organizing stage of diffuse alveolar damage. Polypoid plugs of loose fibroblastic tissue in airspaces describe organizing pneumonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hemodynamic edema shows engorged alveolar capillaries and a finely granular, pale pink intraalveolar transudate, sometimes with microhemorrhages; injury-related edema instead leaks a protein-rich exudate that organizes into hyaline membranes. COMLEX: The basal, dependent distribution of hemodynamic edema follows hydrostatic pressure, which is greatest in the lower lobes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 8 — the zone differences in pressure and blood flow down the upright lung","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A 47-year-old man dies on day 3 of ARDS after severe pancreatitis. At autopsy, before any sections are cut, which gross appearance of the lungs would the pathologist expect in this acute exudative stage?","choices":{"A":"Light, pale, voluminous lungs","B":"Firm, brown, indurated lungs","C":"Heavy, firm, red, boggy lungs","D":"Small lungs with honeycomb cysts","E":"Lungs with apical bullae and blebs"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Robbins states that in the acute exudative stage of ARDS the lungs are heavy, firm, red, and boggy, reflecting congestion, interstitial and intraalveolar edema, inflammation, fibrin deposition, and diffuse alveolar damage with hyaline membranes; radiologically this appears as patchy ground-glass opacities with or without consolidation. Light, voluminous lungs that overlap the heart describe advanced emphysema. Firm, brown lungs with thickened alveolar walls are the brown induration of long-standing congestion, as in mitral stenosis. Small lungs with honeycomb cysts describe end-stage fibrosis such as UIP, which may follow ARDS in a minority of survivors but not on day 3. Apical bullae and subpleural blebs are features of emphysema.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the acute exudative stage of ARDS the lungs are heavy, firm, red, and boggy, reflecting congestion, interstitial and intraalveolar edema, inflammation, fibrin deposition, and diffuse alveolar damage; imaging shows patchy ground-glass opacities with or without consolidation. COMLEX: Robbins attributes most ARDS deaths to sepsis, multiorgan failure, or severe lung injury; survivors usually recover pulmonary function.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 6 — alveolar changes in pneumonia versus emphysema, other gross contrasts in lung weight and aeration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl27_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the key morphological features of both chronic pulmonary congestion and the acute exudative stage of ARDS","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with ARDS who survives the acute phase develops progressive pulmonary fibrosis. Histology shows fibroblast proliferation within alveolar walls, collagen deposition, and type II pneumocyte hyperplasia. What phase of diffuse alveolar damage does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Organizing/fibroproliferative phase with attempted repair","B":"Acute exudative phase with hyaline membrane formation","C":"Resolution phase with complete restoration of normal architecture","D":"Chronic congestion phase with hemosiderin-laden macrophages","E":"Progressive massive fibrosis phase similar to complicated pneumoconiosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The organizing/fibroproliferative phase follows the acute phase if inflammatory triggers abate. Macrophages remove intraalveolar debris and release TGF-β and PDGF, stimulating fibroblast proliferation and collagen deposition within alveolar walls. Type II pneumocytes proliferate attempting to reconstitute the alveolar lining. Some patients enter extended resolution with eventual restoration; others develop persistent fibrosis with impaired function. This contrasts with the acute exudative phase (hyaline membranes, edema, necrosis) that precedes it. Hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) mark long-standing pulmonary congestion, as in mitral stenosis, not the fibroproliferative phase of ARDS. Progressive massive fibrosis is the blackened scarring of complicated coal workers' pneumoconiosis, not a phase of diffuse alveolar damage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In the proliferative (organizing) stage of diffuse alveolar damage, type II pneumocytes proliferate and granulation tissue forms in alveolar walls and spaces; later, residual type II cells replace type I cells and uninjured capillary endothelium restores the vessels. COMLEX: Some survivors pass through a resolution phase lasting months, while a minority are left with interstitial fibrosis and chronic lung disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — type II pneumocytes, the progenitors that re-line injured alveoli","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Hemosiderin-laden macrophages (heart failure cells) mark lon","E":"Progressive massive fibrosis is the blackened scarring of co"}}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q01_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis presents with large, foul-smelling stools, abdominal distention, and failure to thrive. Laboratory studies reveal deficiencies of vitamins A, D, E, and K. What is the underlying pathophysiologic mechanism responsible for these clinical manifestations?","choices":{"A":"Chronic pulmonary infections with Pseudomonas aeruginosa","B":"Biliary cirrhosis with hepatic synthetic dysfunction","C":"Intestinal obstruction from meconium ileus","D":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with fat malabsorption","E":"Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency with diabetes mellitus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs in 85-90% of CF patients with \"severe\" CFTR mutations on both alleles (e.g., ΔF508/ΔF508). Thick viscous secretions obstruct pancreatic ducts, leading to protein and fat malabsorption. This causes steatorrhea (large foul-smelling stools), abdominal distention, and poor weight gain. Fat malabsorption leads to deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), which can cause avitaminosis manifestations. Hypoproteinemia may be severe enough to cause generalized edema. Pseudomonas infection drives the lung disease, not steatorrhea, and the vitamin loss here comes from pancreatic fat malabsorption rather than from biliary cirrhosis, which affects only a minority of patients. Meconium ileus is small-bowel obstruction by viscid mucus plugs at or soon after birth, not the cause of fat malabsorption in a 6-year-old. Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency means diabetes mellitus, seen in up to half of adults with CF, not fat malabsorption with steatorrhea.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thick secretions plug the pancreatic ducts, causing exocrine atrophy and fibrosis; the resulting fat and protein malabsorption brings large foul-smelling stools, abdominal distention, poor weight gain, and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K, often within the first year of life. COMLEX: FA lists pancreatic enzyme replacement (pancrelipase) for the pancreatic insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 9 — clinically differentiating obstructive lung diseases, where extrapulmonary clues point to CF","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Pseudomonas infection drives t","B":"Pseudomonas infection drives t","C":"Meconium ileus is small-bowel obstruction by viscid mucus pl","E":"Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency means diabetes mellitus, "}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q02_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with cystic fibrosis and his wife present for infertility evaluation. Physical examination and semen analysis reveal obstructive azoospermia. What anatomic abnormality most commonly accounts for infertility in men with CF?","choices":{"A":"Congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens","B":"Testicular atrophy from chronic systemic illness","C":"Obstruction of seminal vesicles by viscous secretions","D":"Hypothalamic-pituitary dysfunction from malnutrition","E":"Epididymal fibrosis from recurrent infections"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Approximately 95% of males with CF are infertile due to obstructive azoospermia, most commonly from congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens (CBAVD). Robbins (Ch 10) calls it a frequent finding in these patients and says it is caused in 80% of cases by biallelic CFTR mutations. CBAVD can also occur as an isolated finding in males with CFTR mutations who have nonclassic/atypical CF without other manifestations. Robbins attributes male CF infertility to obstructive azoospermia from congenital absence of the vas deferens; testicular atrophy and hypothalamic-pituitary failure would cause nonobstructive azoospermia, and epididymal infection is not the described mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95% of men with CF who reach adulthood are infertile from obstructive azoospermia, most often congenital bilateral absence of the vas deferens, which is caused by biallelic CFTR mutations in 80% of cases. COMLEX: FA notes spermatogenesis may be unaffected in CF, while women are subfertile from amenorrhea and abnormally thick cervical mucus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, including Kartagener syndrome, whose affected males are also infertile","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins attributes male CF inf","D":"Robbins attributes male CF inf","E":"Robbins attributes male CF inf"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q03_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis presents with acute abdominal pain and jaundice. Ultrasound reveals common bile duct obstruction. Aside from acute biliary obstruction, what chronic hepatic complication develops in less than 10% of CF patients?","choices":{"A":"Primary sclerosing cholangitis with bile duct strictures","B":"Hepatocellular carcinoma from chronic inflammation","C":"Diffuse biliary cirrhosis from chronic obstruction","D":"Steatohepatitis from fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","E":"Portal vein thrombosis from hypercoagulability"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Significant liver disease occurs late in CF natural history and is the third most common cause of death after cardiopulmonary and transplantation-related complications. Diffuse biliary cirrhosis develops in <10% of CF patients from chronic bile duct obstruction by viscous secretions. Symptomatic or biochemical liver disease typically begins at puberty with prevalence ~15%. Asymptomatic hepatomegaly may be present in up to one-third of individuals. Acute obstruction of common bile duct can occur from stones or sludge. Hepatic steatosis is actually common in CF and is not a rare complication or one caused by vitamin deficiency, and portal vein thrombosis is not among the hepatic manifestations Robbins lists. Robbins describes CF liver disease as mucus-plugged bile canaliculi, steatosis, and focal or diffuse biliary cirrhosis; sclerosing cholangitis is not among its CF complications.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mucus plugs the bile canaliculi; focal biliary cirrhosis develops in about one-third of CF patients, but diffuse biliary cirrhosis with nodularity of the whole liver in fewer than 10%. COMLEX: After cardiopulmonary and transplantation-related complications, liver disease is the commonest cause of death in CF; it usually appears around puberty (prevalence about 15%), and stones or sludge in the common bile duct cause acute jaundice.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — lung transplantation, whose complications rank ahead of liver disease as a cause of CF deaths","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins describes CF liver disease as mucus-plugged bile can"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q04_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn with cystic fibrosis develops intestinal obstruction within the first 24 hours of life from thick, tenacious meconium. What is this clinical presentation called?","choices":{"A":"Distal intestinal obstruction syndrome of older patients","B":"Midgut volvulus from intestinal malrotation","C":"Rectal prolapse from chronic straining","D":"Meconium ileus from inspissated meconium","E":"Necrotizing enterocolitis of prematurity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Meconium ileus is intestinal obstruction in neonates caused by abnormally thick, viscous meconium that obstructs the small intestine (typically terminal ileum). It may be the presenting sign of CF (Robbins Ch 10). The thick meconium results from abnormal CFTR function in intestinal epithelium, causing decreased chloride and water secretion into the lumen. This produces dehydrated, inspissated intestinal contents. Meconium ileus is virtually pathognomonic for CF when it occurs. Rectal prolapse is a later complication of persistent diarrhea in children, and necrotizing enterocolitis is a hazard of prematurity, not an obstruction by thick meconium. Distal intestinal obstruction occurs in older individuals as recurrent right lower quadrant pain, not as obstruction within the first day of life. Midgut volvulus follows malrotation, with fibrous Ladd bands and abnormal bowel position, not obstruction by thick, tenacious meconium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meconium ileus — small-bowel obstruction by thick, viscid mucus plugs — brings 5–10% of CF cases to attention at birth or soon after; older patients can develop distal intestinal obstruction with recurrent right lower quadrant pain and a palpable mass. COMLEX: FA's newborn screen for CF measures immunoreactive trypsinogen, which rises when pancreatic ducts clog.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, the lung consequence of the same viscid secretions","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Distal intestinal obstruction occurs in older individuals as","B":"Midgut volvulus follows malrotation, with fibrous Ladd bands"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q05_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy with CF undergoes sweat chloride testing. The test reveals markedly elevated sweat chloride concentration. What is the pathophysiologic mechanism underlying hypertonic sweat production in cystic fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Increased chloride secretion into sweat duct lumen","B":"Increased sodium reabsorption via hyperactive ENaC","C":"Decreased luminal bicarbonate secretion","D":"Impaired mucus secretion in sweat glands","E":"Decreased chloride reabsorption from sweat duct lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In sweat gland ducts, the major function of CFTR is to reabsorb luminal chloride ions and augment sodium reabsorption via ENaC. Loss of CFTR function leads to DECREASED reabsorption of sodium chloride from the sweat duct lumen, resulting in production of hypertonic sweat with high NaCl content. This is the sine qua non of classic CF and forms the basis for the sweat chloride test (First Aid 2025: increased Cl⁻ concentration in pilocarpine-induced sweat). Parents often detect \"salty\" sweat in affected infants. In the sweat duct CFTR reabsorbs luminal chloride, so its loss decreases NaCl reabsorption rather than increasing chloride secretion into the lumen. Defective bicarbonate transport acidifies pancreatic duct secretions; hypertonic sweat comes from failed chloride reabsorption in the sweat duct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In sweat ducts CFTR reabsorbs luminal chloride and augments sodium reabsorption through ENaC, so loss of CFTR leaves NaCl in the duct and produces hypertonic, salty sweat — the sine qua non of classic CF and the basis of the sweat chloride test. COMLEX: FA notes salt and water lost in sweat can cause contraction alkalosis and hypokalemia, an effect like a loop diuretic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — the tissue-specific functions of CFTR and its mutation classes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"In the sweat duct CFTR reabsorbs luminal chloride, so its lo","C":"Defective bicarbonate transport acidifies pancreatic duct se"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q06_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"An 18-year-old with CF has chronic pulmonary infections with Pseudomonas aeruginosa. What is the primary pathophysiologic mechanism in the airways that predisposes to these recurrent infections?","choices":{"A":"Isotonic, low-volume surface fluid; poor ciliary clearance","B":"Hypertonic surface fluid causing osmotic damage to epithelium","C":"Decreased chloride concentration in airway secretions","D":"Hypoalbuminemia causing decreased oncotic pressure","E":"Primary immunodeficiency with neutrophil dysfunction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In respiratory epithelium, CFTR is crucial for luminal chloride secretion. CFTR mutations cause: (1) loss/reduction of chloride secretion into lumen, and (2) increased luminal sodium absorption (loss of ENaC inhibition). These ion changes increase passive water reabsorption from lumen, lowering the water content of the surface fluid layer. Unlike sweat, there's NO difference in salt concentration—instead, CF airways have an isotonic but LOW-VOLUME surface fluid layer. This dehydration leads to defective mucociliary action and accumulation of viscid secretions that obstruct passages and predispose to infections. Plasma oncotic pressure governs edema, not mucus clearance, and CF is not a primary immunodeficiency: infections persist because mucociliary clearance fails, and the immune reactions that do occur cannot clear biofilm-protected Pseudomonas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In airway epithelium CFTR loss cuts chloride secretion and frees ENaC from inhibition, so sodium and water are reabsorbed; the surface fluid stays isotonic but shrinks in volume, crippling mucociliary clearance. COMLEX: Static, hypoxic mucus favors alginate-producing mucoid P. aeruginosa that forms protective biofilms; FA lists chest physiotherapy, dornase alfa, and hypertonic saline to clear the mucus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 3 — P. aeruginosa among the key bacterial pneumonia organisms","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Unlike sweat, there's NO diffe","C":"Unlike sweat, there's NO diffe"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q07_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"The CFTR protein consists of multiple functional domains. What are the two cytoplasmic domains that bind and hydrolyze ATP, which is essential for opening and closing the chloride channel pore?","choices":{"A":"Transmembrane domains containing six α-helices each","B":"Regulatory domain containing protein kinase A phosphorylation sites","C":"Nucleotide-binding domains (NBDs)","D":"Anion selectivity filter regulating chloride conductance","E":"PDZ-binding motif for cytoskeletal anchoring"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CFTR structure includes: (1) two transmembrane domains (each with six α-helices) forming the chloride channel, (2) two cytoplasmic nucleotide-binding domains (NBDs) that bind and hydrolyze ATP—essential for opening/closing the channel pore, and (3) a regulatory domain (R domain) with protein kinase A phosphorylation sites. CFTR activation requires cAMP-induced protein kinase A phosphorylation of the R domain. ATP binding/hydrolysis at NBDs is enhanced by R domain phosphorylation and controls channel gating. The two transmembrane domains, each of six α-helices, form the chloride channel; ATP is bound and hydrolyzed at the cytoplasmic nucleotide-binding domains. The ion-conducting pore is formed by the transmembrane domains, whereas ATP binding and hydrolysis occur at the two cytoplasmic nucleotide-binding domains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR has two transmembrane domains of six α-helices that form the chloride pore, two cytoplasmic nucleotide-binding domains where ATP binding and hydrolysis open and close the channel, and an R domain that cAMP-activated protein kinase A phosphorylates. COMLEX: Gating (class III) mutations abolish this ATP-dependent activation, which is why potentiators that hold the gate open (ivacaftor in FA) help them.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 13 — β2-adrenergic bronchodilation, which works through the same cAMP second messenger","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The two transmembrane domains, each of six α-helices, form t","D":"The ion-conducting pore is formed by the transmembrane domai"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q08_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with CF has the ΔF508 mutation, the most common CFTR mutation found in ~70% of Northern European patients. What class of CFTR mutation is ΔF508, and what is its effect on the CFTR protein?","choices":{"A":"Class I null mutation causing premature termination","B":"Class III gating mutation preventing ATP binding","C":"Class IV conduction mutation reducing chloride flow","D":"Class II processing mutation causing ER retention","E":"Class V production mutation reducing mRNA synthesis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ΔF508 (deletion of phenylalanine at position 508) is a Class II processing mutation. These mutations cause defective protein folding and impaired trafficking from endoplasmic reticulum to Golgi apparatus. The misfolded protein is recognized by ER quality control and degraded by proteasomes before reaching the cell surface. Class II mutations result in near-complete lack of CFTR protein at the apical epithelial surface. This is a \"severe\" mutation (<10% residual CFTR activity) associated with classic CF phenotype. Class I null mutations cause premature termination of translation and no CFTR protein, whereas ΔF508 is a class II folding and processing defect. Class III gating mutations abrogate ATP binding and hydrolysis in a normal amount of surface CFTR, whereas ΔF508 is misfolded and degraded before reaching the surface. Class IV conduction mutations leave a normal amount of CFTR at the membrane with reduced chloride flow, whereas ΔF508 protein rarely reaches the surface. Class V production mutations affect splice sites or the promoter and reduce the amount of normal protein, whereas ΔF508 makes a misfolded protein retained in the ER.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ΔF508 deletes three nucleotides coding for phenylalanine at position 508 — a class II processing mutation in which the misfolded protein is retained in the ER and degraded before reaching the apical membrane; it is found in about 70% of Northern European CF patients. COMLEX: Correctors (lumacaftor, tezacaftor in FA) help the protein fold and traffic to the surface, and corrector–potentiator combinations are approved for patients with two copies of ΔF508.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 2 — panacinar emphysema from α1-antitrypsin deficiency, another lung disease of a misfolded protein","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Class I null mutations cause premature termination of transl","B":"Class III gating mutations abrogate ATP binding and hydrolys","C":"Class IV conduction mutations leave a normal amount of CFTR ","E":"Class V production mutations affect splice sites or the prom"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q09_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A CF patient has one \"severe\" CFTR mutation (ΔF508) and one \"mild\" mutation (R117H). Which organ system manifestation shows the most consistent genotype-phenotype correlation based on mutation severity?","choices":{"A":"Pulmonary disease severity and age of onset","B":"Pancreatic insufficiency and exocrine function","C":"Male fertility and vas deferens development","D":"Hepatic cirrhosis progression","E":"Sinonasal polyp formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Genotype-phenotype correlation is MOST CONSISTENT for pancreatic disease. Two \"severe\" mutations (e.g., ΔF508/ΔF508) cause virtual absence of CFTR function and are associated with pancreatic insufficiency (85-90% of patients). However, presence of one \"mild\" mutation (e.g., ΔF508/R117H) can prevent pancreatic insufficiency, resulting in pancreas-sufficient phenotype (10-15%). In contrast, genotype-phenotype correlations are far LESS consistent for pulmonary disease due to secondary modifiers (environmental factors, modifier genes, infections). Pancreas-sufficient patients retain enough function to not require enzyme supplementation. Absent vas deferens occurs even in mild, nonclassic CF with no other features, so it does not track mutation severity, and Robbins names the pancreas, not the liver or sinuses, as the organ whose involvement follows the genotype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Genotype predicts pancreatic status best: one mild allele with some residual CFTR activity can prevent the pancreatic insufficiency seen with two severe alleles, whereas lung disease correlates poorly because modifier genes (MBL2, TGFB1, IFRD1) and infections intervene. COMLEX: Patients who share a CFTR genotype can follow very different pulmonary courses, so lung prognosis cannot be read from the genotype alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 43 → Obj 1 — pulmonary host defenses, the arena in which CF's modifier genes act","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Absent vas deferens occurs eve","E":"Absent vas deferens occurs eve"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q10_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 3-year-old child with cystic fibrosis presents with chronic cough and sputum culture positive for Staphylococcus aureus. By what age do 80% of patients with classic CF harbor Pseudomonas aeruginosa?","choices":{"A":"6 years of age","B":"10 years of age","C":"18 years of age","D":"25 years of age","E":"35 years of age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By 18 years of age, 80% of patients with classic cystic fibrosis harbor Pseudomonas aeruginosa. Cardiorespiratory complications, particularly persistent lung infections and obstructive pulmonary disease, are the most common cause of death in CF patients in the United States. P. aeruginosa colonization is associated with more severe pulmonary disease and faster decline in lung function. Additionally, 3.5% of patients harbor Burkholderia cepacia by age 18, which portends even worse prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Robbins gives two figures — 80% of classic CF patients harbor P. aeruginosa by age 18 (clinical section), and approximately 80% are colonized by age 20 (pathogenesis section); S. aureus, H. influenzae, and P. aeruginosa are the commonest lung pathogens, and FA places S. aureus in infancy and early childhood and P. aeruginosa in adulthood. COMLEX: Burkholderia cepacia (3.5% by age 18) can cause fulminant 'cepacia syndrome' with higher mortality.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 6 — complications of bacterial pneumonia predicted by the causative organism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q11_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with CF develops glucose intolerance and diabetes mellitus requiring insulin therapy. What is the underlying mechanism for endocrine pancreatic dysfunction in adult CF patients?","choices":{"A":"Autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells","B":"Insulin resistance from chronic inflammation and malnutrition","C":"Pancreatic beta cell mutations in CFTR gene","D":"Ectopic ACTH production from pulmonary tumors","E":"Destruction of pancreatic parenchyma including the islets"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Endocrine pancreatic insufficiency (i.e., diabetes mellitus) occurs in up to 50% of adults with CF and is thought to be caused by destruction of pancreatic parenchyma including the islets. Chronic obstruction and inflammation lead to progressive fibrosis and atrophy of both exocrine and endocrine components. This is CF-related diabetes (CFRD), which differs from type 1 diabetes (not autoimmune) and type 2 diabetes (not primarily insulin resistance). CFRD is the most common comorbidity in adults with CF. Ectopic ACTH is a paraneoplastic product of lung cancer that causes Cushing syndrome, unrelated to CF diabetes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diabetes develops in up to 50% of adults with CF and is attributed to destruction of pancreatic parenchyma including the islets; in severe pancreatic disease the ducts plug completely, the exocrine glands atrophy, and fibrosis leaves islets within a fibrofatty stroma. COMLEX: FA describes CF pancreatic disease progressing from exocrine insufficiency to endocrine dysfunction (CF-related diabetes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — lung transplantation, a late therapy for end-stage CF lung disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q12_b1","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — What are the clinical features of cystic fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy with CF develops recurrent nasal polyps requiring surgical removal. What percentage of CF patients develop recurrent sinonasal polyps?","choices":{"A":"5% of individuals with CF","B":"50% to 60% of individuals with CF","C":"10% to 25% of individuals with CF","D":"75% of individuals with CF","E":"Less than 1% of individuals with CF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Recurrent sinonasal polyps occur in 10% to 25% of individuals with CF. Children who present with nasal polyps should be tested for CF, as this may be the presenting sign. The polyps result from chronic sinusitis and inflammation secondary to thick, viscid secretions obstructing the paranasal sinuses. Other upper respiratory manifestations include chronic rhinosinusitis and pansinusitis, which are nearly universal in CF patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Recurrent sinonasal polyps occur in 10–25% of people with CF, so a child who presents with nasal polyps should be tested for CF. COMLEX: In an adult, nasal polyps with asthma and NSAID sensitivity point instead to aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease (FA).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease, the asthma phenotype that is the other polyp-associated airway disease","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q13_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A 5-year-old with CF develops rectal prolapse. What is the underlying mechanism that predisposes CF patients to this gastrointestinal complication?","choices":{"A":"Chronic constipation from intestinal dysmotility","B":"Increased intraabdominal pressure from hepatosplenomegaly","C":"Weakness of pelvic floor muscles from malnutrition","D":"Persistent diarrhea from malabsorption and increased stool bulk","E":"Colonic polyps causing mechanical obstruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistent diarrhea from fat and protein malabsorption may result in rectal prolapse in up to 10% of children with CF. Pancreatic insufficiency causes protein and fat malabsorption with increased fecal loss, seen as large, foul-smelling stools, abdominal distention and poor weight gain (Robbins). Pancreas-sufficient patients usually escape these gastrointestinal complications. Robbins ties rectal prolapse in CF to persistent diarrhea from malabsorption; hepatosplenomegaly, colonic polyps, constipation and pelvic floor weakness from malnutrition are not part of that mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Persistent diarrhea from fat and protein malabsorption causes rectal prolapse in up to 10% of children with CF, and hypoproteinemia can be severe enough to cause generalized edema. COMLEX: Pancreas-sufficient patients usually escape these GI complications and grow normally, so prolapse in a CF child signals pancreatic insufficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of CF, including pancreatic insufficiency and steatorrhea","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Robbins ties rectal prolapse i"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q14_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A CF patient undergoes measurement of nasal transepithelial potential difference as part of diagnostic workup. What characteristic finding distinguishes CF from normal individuals?","choices":{"A":"Less negative baseline nasal potential difference","B":"More negative baseline nasal potential difference","C":"No difference in baseline nasal potential difference","D":"Positive baseline nasal potential difference","E":"Fluctuating nasal potential difference with respiration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Individuals with CF demonstrate a significantly MORE NEGATIVE baseline nasal potential difference than controls. This is due to increased sodium absorption (hyperactive ENaC without CFTR inhibition) and decreased chloride secretion through the nasal epithelium. Nasal potential difference measurement is a useful diagnostic adjunct, especially in patients with borderline or normal sweat chloride tests (those with at least one \"mild\" CFTR mutation). This test measures the voltage across the nasal epithelium in vivo.","boardPrep":"Step 1: With CFTR lost, ENaC absorbs more sodium and chloride secretion falls, so people with CF have a significantly more negative baseline nasal transepithelial potential difference than controls (Robbins, FA). COMLEX: Nasal potential difference is the adjunct when sweat chloride is normal or intermediate, as with at least one mild allele; CFTR sequencing is the diagnostic gold standard.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the ciliated respiratory epithelium in which this ion transport is measured","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q15_b1","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the knowledge of pathology, pathogenesis, and morphology to Cystic Fibrosis","batch":1,"stem":"A newborn with CF is being evaluated for nutritional complications. The mother asks why her infant is at risk for bleeding disorders. What vitamin deficiency secondary to pancreatic insufficiency increases bleeding risk?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin A deficiency causing impaired clotting factor synthesis","B":"Vitamin D deficiency causing impaired calcium-dependent coagulation","C":"Vitamin K deficiency impairing synthesis of factors II, VII, IX, X","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causing megaloblastic changes","E":"Vitamin C deficiency causing capillary fragility"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fat malabsorption from exocrine pancreatic insufficiency leads to deficiency of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K). Vitamin K is essential for hepatic synthesis of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (the vitamin K-dependent factors), as well as protein C and protein S. Vitamin K deficiency manifests as prolonged PT/INR and increased bleeding risk. Other avitaminoses include: vitamin A (night blindness, xerophthalmia), vitamin D (rickets, osteomalacia), and vitamin E (hemolytic anemia, neuromuscular abnormalities). Vitamin B12 deficiency causes macrocytic, megaloblastic anemia and neurologic signs, not a bleeding tendency; CF fat malabsorption depletes the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K. Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes easy bruising from defective collagen, but CF pancreatic insufficiency depletes the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic insufficiency causes fat malabsorption and deficiency of vitamins A, D, E, and K; vitamin K is needed for maturation of clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X and proteins C and S, so deficiency raises PT and aPTT and causes bleeding (FA). COMLEX: Robbins adds that vitamin A deficiency may contribute to squamous metaplasia of the pancreatic duct lining in CF.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — the clinical features of CF, including fat-soluble vitamin deficiency","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Other avitaminoses include: vi","B":"Other avitaminoses include: vi","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes macrocytic, megaloblastic anem","E":"Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) causes easy bruising from defe"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q16_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"CFTR regulates transport of which ion in addition to chloride that is particularly important in pancreatic duct function?","choices":{"A":"Potassium ions via inwardly rectifying channels","B":"Calcium ions via voltage-gated channels","C":"Magnesium ions via ATP-dependent transporters","D":"Bicarbonate ions via direct and SLC26 transport","E":"Phosphate ions via sodium-phosphate cotransporters"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CFTR regulates transport of bicarbonate ions both directly (the CFTR anion channel transports bicarbonate in addition to chloride) and indirectly through reciprocal interactions with SLC26 family anion exchangers co-expressed on the apical surface. In some CFTR mutants, chloride transport is preserved but bicarbonate transport is markedly abnormal. Acidic secretions (due to absent bicarbonate) increase mucin precipitation, duct plugging, and bacterial binding. Pancreatic insufficiency is virtually ALWAYS present when CFTR mutations have abnormal bicarbonate conductance. CFTR also regulates other channels, including inwardly rectifying potassium channels (Kir6.1), but Robbins ties duct plugging in the pancreas to the loss of bicarbonate secretion, not potassium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR's anion channel conducts bicarbonate as well as chloride and also regulates the apical SLC26 anion exchangers; some mutants keep chloride transport but lose bicarbonate transport, secreting acidic fluid that precipitates mucin and plugs ducts. COMLEX: Pancreatic insufficiency is virtually always present when CFTR mutations impair bicarbonate conductance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — pancreatic insufficiency among the clinical features of CF","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q17_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studies a CFTR mutation affecting intronic splice sites, resulting in reduced amounts of otherwise normal CFTR protein at the cell surface. What class of mutation is this?","choices":{"A":"Class V reduced protein abundance","B":"Class II abnormal protein folding","C":"Class III defective regulation","D":"Class IV decreased conductance","E":"Class I defective protein synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Class V mutations are production mutations that affect intronic splice sites or the CFTR promoter, resulting in reduced amounts of normal CFTR protein. The protein that is produced is functional, but there's simply less of it. Robbins groups classes IV to VI as \"mild\" mutations with less than 20% residual CFTR activity (classes I to III, the \"severe\" ones, have less than 10%; course Pulm SDL 28 PDF p42). Class V mutations can benefit from \"potentiator\" therapy (which improves function of the reduced CFTR present) and \"amplifier\" therapy (which increases the amount of CFTR protein the cell makes). Class II mutations misfold the protein, which is degraded before it reaches the cell surface, rather than leaving reduced amounts of normal protein there. Class IV conduction mutations leave a normal amount of CFTR at the membrane with reduced chloride flow, not a reduced amount of normal protein. Class I mutations cause premature termination and no CFTR protein at all, not reduced amounts of normal protein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Class V (production) mutations hit intronic splice sites or the CFTR promoter, so less normal protein is made; Robbins groups classes I–III as severe (under 10% residual activity) and classes IV–VI as mild. COMLEX: Potentiators improve the function of the reduced CFTR present in class V, and amplifiers raise the amount of protein made.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, where adult-onset idiopathic cases have been tied to CFTR mutations","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Class II mutations misfold the protein, which is degraded be","D":"Class IV conduction mutations leave a normal amount of CFTR ","E":"Class I mutations cause premature termination and no CFTR pr"}},{"id":"sdl28_ex1_q18_b1","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Detail the structure and function of the cystic fibrosis gene including normal structure and function and the mutational spectrum of the disease","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old man presents with recurrent acute pancreatitis but no other features of CF. Genetic testing reveals biallelic CFTR mutations (one \"mild,\" one \"severe\"). What category of CF does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Classic CF with typical multi-system involvement","B":"Nonclassic CF with single-organ involvement","C":"CF carrier state with no clinical manifestations","D":"Acquired CFTR dysfunction from environmental factors","E":"Modifier gene effects masking typical CF phenotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Nonclassic or atypical CF describes patients with biallelic CFTR mutations who present with isolated organ manifestations without other features of classic CF. These include: idiopathic chronic pancreatitis, late-onset chronic pulmonary disease, idiopathic bronchiectasis, and obstructive azoospermia from congenital bilateral absence of vas deferens (CBAVD). Most have one \"mild\" and one \"severe\" mutation. Identifying these patients is important for management and genetic counseling. They typically have normal or borderline sweat chloride tests. Classic CF comes from two severe mutations and shows pancreatic insufficiency, sinopulmonary infections, and gastrointestinal symptoms, not isolated recurrent pancreatitis. A carrier is heterozygous, whereas this man has biallelic CFTR mutations with clinical disease. Modifier genes such as MBL2 and TGFB1 mainly alter the severity of lung disease and meconium ileus; isolated pancreatitis with one mild and one severe allele defines nonclassic CF.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nonclassic (atypical) CF means biallelic CFTR mutations — usually one mild and one severe — presenting as single-organ disease without other CF features: idiopathic chronic pancreatitis, late-onset chronic lung disease, idiopathic bronchiectasis, or obstructive azoospermia. COMLEX: Identifying these patients matters for management and for genetic counseling of the family.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 8 — bronchiectasis, where adult-onset idiopathic cases have been linked to CFTR mutations","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Classic CF comes from two severe mutations and shows pancrea","C":"A carrier is heterozygous, whereas this man has biallelic CF","E":"Modifier genes such as MBL2 and TGFB1 mainly alter the sever"}}]};
